        {"name":"ROM_END_BYTES","value":12283}
      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Power-on and reset: interrupts off, A = 0, then BOOT.\nDoes not return.","comment":"A = 0 for BOOT's OUT and its copy count"},
        "5": {"comment":"Not a restart: a JP to RST $08's RAM vector, three bytes on"},
        "8": {"synopsis":"RST $08: through the RAM vector at $4000 to EXPECT_CHAR, which checks that (HL) holds the\nbyte after the RST.","comment":"Via $4000: JP EXPECT_CHAR"},
        "11": {"comment":"POP HL / JP (HL): a CALL here returns with HL = its return address"},
        "13": {"comment":"Go on at SECTOR_BOOT"},
        "16": {"synopsis":"RST $10: through the RAM vector at $4003 to NEXT_CHAR, the next character of the text.","comment":"Via $4003: JP NEXT_CHAR"},
        "19": {"synopsis":"Calls the driver of the device block DE points at, asking for input (B = 1).\nEntry: DE = a device block.\nExit: as the driver leaves it; BC is restored.","comment":"B = 1, the input bit of a block's type"},
        "22": {"comment":"Go on at DEV_CALL"},
        "24": {"synopsis":"RST $18: through the RAM vector at $4006 to CP_HL_DE.","comment":"Via $4006: JP CP_HL_DE"},
        "27": {"synopsis":"Calls the driver of the device block DE points at, for output (B = 2).\nEntry: DE = a device block, A = the character.\nExit: BC is restored.","comment":"B = 2, the output bit of a block's type"},
        "32": {"synopsis":"RST $20: through the RAM vector at $4009 to TYPE_FLAGS.","comment":"Via $4009: JP TYPE_FLAGS"},
        "35": {"synopsis":"Calls the driver of the device block DE points at, with B = 4.\nEntry: DE = a device block.\nExit: BC is restored.","comment":"B = 4"},
        "40": {"synopsis":"RST $28: through the RAM vector at $400C, a RET until something writes a jump there.\nKEY_SCAN runs it when a key reads as $01 (BREAK).","comment":"Via $400C: RET at power-on"},
        "43": {"synopsis":"Reads the keyboard through its device block at $4015 (driver KEY_SCAN).\nExit: A = the key's code, 0 when no key is newly pressed.\nChanges: AF, DE (the block's address).","comment":"DE = the keyboard's block"},
        "48": {"synopsis":"RST $30: through the RAM vector at $400F, a RET at power-on.","comment":"Via $400F: RET at power-on"},
        "51": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in A to the display through its device block at $401D (driver\nDISPLAY_DRIVER).\nEntry: A = the character.\nChanges: DE.","comment":"DE = the display's block"},
        "56": {"synopsis":"RST $38, the maskable interrupt (IM 1): through the RAM vector at $4012, EI / RET at\npower-on.","comment":"Via $4012: EI / RET at power-on"},
        "59": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in A to the printer through its device block at $4025 (driver\nPRINTER_DRIVER).\nEntry: A = the character.\nChanges: DE.","comment":"DE = the printer's block"},
        "64": {"comment":"Go on at KEY_LINE_EDIT, which reads a line from the keyboard"},
        "70": {"synopsis":"The shared tail of DEV_INPUT, DEV_OUTPUT and DEV_CONTROL.","comment":"Go on at DEV_DISPATCH"},
        "73": {"synopsis":"Waits for a key: KEY_READ until it gives a code that is not zero.\nExit: A = the key's code.\nChanges: AF, DE.","comment":"A key at last?"},
        "80": {"synopsis":"Key codes for keyboard row 6 ($3840), two per key: the code without SHIFT, then with it.\nKEY_SCAN picks the pair by the key's bit in the row and the byte by SHIFT (bit 0 of $3880).\nIn Klive's key matrix the row is ENTER, CLEAR, BREAK, up, down, left, right and SPACE: ENTER\n$0D, CLEAR $1F, BREAK $01, up $5B/$1B, down $0A/$1A, left $08/$18, right $09/$19, SPACE $20.\nObserved: BREAK reads as $01, which runs RST $28 at $0456."},
        "96": {"synopsis":"A delay: counts BC down to zero (BC = 0 counts 65536 times).\nEntry: BC = the count.\nExit: BC = 0, A = 0, Z set.\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "102": {"synopsis":"The NMI (the RESET key): SP to $0600, then NMI_RESTART, or POWER_ON when $37EC reads other\nthan $FF or $00. On the HT-1080Z $37EC is extension ROM and reads $FF.\nObserved: the RESET key at the prompt runs NMI, NMI_RESTART, $1A18 and BASIC_READY, and\nBASIC prints READY again.\nDoes not return.","comment":"A temporary stack, in ROM: writes are lost"},
        "105": {"comment":"Is $37EC $FF or $00 (A + 1 below 2)?"},
        "111": {"comment":"No: start again from power-on"},
        "117": {"synopsis":"Sets up BASIC's RAM after BOOT:\n1. Copies 39 bytes from RAM_IMAGE_4080 to $4080.\n2. Writes \":\", 0 and \",\" at $41E5-$41E7 and stores $41E8 at $40A7 (the line buffer\n   PROMPT_LINE fills).\n3. Fills $4152-$41A5 with 28 jumps to $012D, which stops with error code $2C, and the 21\n   3-byte slots from $41A6 on with a RET each.\n4. Zeroes $42E8, sets SP to $41F8 and calls RESET_STACK, then CLS_CMD (display codes $1C and\n   $1F).\n5. Asks the power-on question (MEM_QUESTION) and sets the top of memory from the answer.\n6. Calls NEW_PROGRAM, prints SCROLL_TEXT and goes on at BASIC_READY, BASIC's prompt.\nDoes not return.","comment":"DE = the RAM the 39 bytes go to"},
        "128": {"comment":"HL = $41E5: three bytes before the line buffer"},
        "139": {"comment":"$40A7 = $41E8, the line buffer"},
        "142": {"comment":"DE = $012D, the hooks' target: error $2C"},
        "145": {"comment":"B = 28 jumps"},
        "150": {"comment":"A JP $012D","synopsis":"BASIC_INIT's loop writing B jumps (JP DE) from HL on."},
        "159": {"comment":"B = 21 RETs, one per 3-byte slot"},
        "161": {"comment":"RET, then skip the slot's other two bytes","synopsis":"BASIC_INIT's loop writing B RETs, one at the start of each 3-byte slot from HL on."},
        "168": {"comment":"$42E8 = 0 (B is 0 after the DJNZ)"},
        "172": {"comment":"A stack below the line buffer, for the question"},
        "178": {"comment":"Send $1C and $1F to the display"},
        "181": {"synopsis":"The power-on question: prints READY_TEXT and reads the answer (PROMPT_LINE); a BREAK (carry)\nasks again. An empty answer tests RAM upwards from $434D (MEM_TEST_LOOP); a number sets the\ntop itself (MEM_ANSWER). Both go on at MEM_TOP_SET.\nObserved: NEW LINE alone on a 16K Series I made the top $7FFF; the answer 20000 made it\n$4E1E."},
        "190": {"comment":"Carry (BREAK)? Ask again"},
        "192": {"comment":"The answer's first character"},
        "194": {"comment":"Any answer? Take its number"},
        "196": {"comment":"No answer: test RAM from $434D up"},
        "199": {"synopsis":"Tests each byte from HL + 1 up, until address $0000: writes its complement and compares,\nthen puts the byte back. Stops at the first byte that does not keep what was written.\nObserved: on a 16K machine it stopped at $8000.","comment":"The next byte; has HL wrapped to $0000?"},
        "204": {"comment":"Write the complement..."},
        "208": {"comment":"... does it read back? Restore the byte either way"},
        "214": {"synopsis":"The answer is a number: READ_LINE_NUM reads it into DE; anything after it is a syntax error.\nThen the byte below that address is tested once (written with $8F), and a byte that does not\nkeep it asks the question again.","comment":"DE = the answer's number"},
        "218": {"comment":"Text after the number? Stop with error code 2 (report SN)"},
        "229": {"comment":"No RAM there: ask again"},
        "231": {"synopsis":"HL = the first address that failed the test, or the tested byte. Takes the byte below it as\nthe top of memory: stops with error code 12 (OM) when that is below $4414, else stores it at\n$40B1 and the top less 50 at $40A0.","comment":"The last address that held its byte"},
        "235": {"comment":"Is the top below $4414?"},
        "236": {"comment":"Yes: stop with error code 12 (report OM)"},
        "239": {"comment":"DE = -50"},
        "242": {"comment":"$40B1 = the top of memory"},
        "246": {"comment":"$40A0 = the top less 50"},
        "255": {"comment":"Print SCROLL_TEXT: 27 new lines"},
        "258": {"comment":"Go on at BASIC's prompt"},
        "261": {"synopsis":"The power-on question's text, \"READY \" and a zero that ends it; MEM_QUESTION prints it and\nPROMPT_LINE adds \"? \".\nObserved: the screen shows READY ? at power-on."},
        "268": {"synopsis":"Five zero bytes after the question's text; nothing reads them."},
        "273": {"synopsis":"27 carriage returns ($0D) and a zero that ends them: printed once memory is set up, so the\nscreen scrolls before BASIC's first prompt."},
        "301": {"synopsis":"Where the 28 RAM hooks at $4152-$41A5 jump at power-on (BASIC_INIT): stops with error code\n$2C (report SN, the last entry of the table at ERROR_CODES), so a statement or function whose\nhook nothing has filled is refused.","comment":"E = $2C: report SN"},
        "303": {"comment":"Stop with the error"},
        "306": {"synopsis":"The POINT function, and with SET_CMD and RESET_CMD the SET and RESET statements: one routine\nfor the three, told apart by A (0 test, $80 set, $01 reset; SET's and RESET's LD A,n entries\nare hidden in the LD BC,nn at $0134 and $0137). Reads (X, Y): X below 128, Y below 48, else\nstops with error code 8 (report FC). The point is block (Y mod 3) * 2 + (X mod 2) of the\ncharacter cell at $3C00 + (Y / 3) * 64 + X / 2; a cell that is not a graphics character (bit\n7 clear) counts as $80, all blocks off.\n1. SET ORs the block's bit into the cell, RESET clears it; both store the cell, now a\n   graphics character.\n2. POINT gives -1 when the block is on, 0 when it is off, through SIGN_TO_INT (the integer\n   result).\nThen the closing \")\" must follow.\nEntry: HL = the text at the keyword (POINT) or after it (SET, RESET).\nExit: HL past the \")\".","comment":"Past POINT"},
        "307": {"comment":"A = 0: test the point"},
        "308": {"comment":"Skipped; LD A,$80 here is SET_CMD"},
        "309": {"synopsis":"The SET statement: A = $80 (LD A,$80, hidden in the LD BC,nn at $0134), then POINT_FN's\ncommon code."},
        "311": {"comment":"Skipped; LD A,$01 here is RESET_CMD"},
        "312": {"synopsis":"The RESET statement: A = $01 (LD A,$01, hidden in the LD BC,nn at $0137), then POINT_FN's\ncommon code."},
        "314": {"comment":"Keep the action: 0 test, $80 set, $01 reset"},
        "317": {"comment":"A = X, a byte expression"},
        "320": {"comment":"X 128 or more? Stop with error code 8 (report FC)"},
        "325": {"comment":"Keep X"},
        "328": {"comment":"A = Y"},
        "331": {"comment":"Y 48 or more? Error code 8 (FC)"},
        "336": {"comment":"D counts the threes in Y from -1: the text row"},
        "338": {"synopsis":"POINT_FN's loop: D = Y / 3, the cell's row.","comment":"One row more per 3 taken off"},
        "343": {"comment":"C = Y mod 3, the block's row in the cell"},
        "346": {"comment":"A = X"},
        "347": {"comment":"E = X * 2; D:E is shifted right twice"},
        "351": {"synopsis":"POINT_FN's loop shifting D:E right by one, D's low bit into E: twice, so DE = row * 64 + X /\n2 and carry = X's low bit.","comment":"D's low bit into E's top"},
        "359": {"comment":"B = (Y mod 3) * 2 + X's low bit + 1"},
        "363": {"comment":"A = 0, carry set: the first pass makes it 1"},
        "365": {"synopsis":"POINT_FN's loop making A = 1 shifted left B - 1 times, the block's bit.","comment":"Doubled, plus carry"},
        "368": {"comment":"C = the block's bit in the cell"},
        "369": {"comment":"DE = the cell in video RAM ($3C00 on)"},
        "373": {"comment":"Is the cell a graphics character (bit 7)?"},
        "378": {"comment":"No: take it as $80, every block off"},
        "380": {"synopsis":"POINT_FN with B = the cell as a graphics character: POINT goes on at PIXEL_TEST, SET at\nPIXEL_SET, RESET below.","comment":"B = the cell"},
        "381": {"comment":"The action: 0 test, $80 set, $01 reset"},
        "384": {"comment":"Test (POINT)? Go to PIXEL_TEST"},
        "386": {"comment":"The cell back, as a graphics character"},
        "387": {"comment":"SET? Add the bit at PIXEL_SET"},
        "390": {"comment":"RESET: every bit but the block's"},
        "395": {"synopsis":"SET's and RESET's end: stores the cell, then PIXEL_CLOSE.","comment":"The cell with the block changed"},
        "396": {"synopsis":"The end of POINT_FN, SET and RESET: the \")\" must follow; returns past it.","comment":"The closing ) must follow"},
        "399": {"synopsis":"SET: ORs the block's bit into the cell and stores it at PIXEL_STORE.","comment":"Add the block"},
        "402": {"synopsis":"POINT: the result is -1 if the block's bit is on in the cell, else 0, as an integer\n(SIGN_TO_INT); then PIXEL_CLOSE.","comment":"Is the block on?"},
        "403": {"comment":"Carry if on, so A = $FF (-1) or 0"},
        "406": {"comment":"Keep the text pointer"},
        "407": {"comment":"The integer result: -1 or 0"},
        "413": {"synopsis":"The INKEY$ function: a 1-character string of the key held over at $4099, or else of a key\nnewly pressed (KEY_GET); the empty string when there is neither. $4099 is cleared once used.\nEntry: HL = the text at INKEY$.\nExit: HL past it; the result as a string ($40AF = 3).","comment":"Past INKEY$"},
        "414": {"comment":"Keep the text pointer"},
        "415": {"comment":"A key held over at $4099?"},
        "419": {"comment":"Yes: take it"},
        "421": {"comment":"No: a newly pressed key?"},
        "425": {"comment":"None: the empty string"},
        "427": {"synopsis":"INKEY_FN with a key in A: clears $4099 and makes the 1-character string of it (room through\nSTR_ALLOC_DESC, the text at $40D4, finished at STR_PUSH_TEMP).","comment":"Keep the key"},
        "428": {"comment":"$4099 = 0: the held key is used"},
        "432": {"comment":"A = 1, the string's length"},
        "433": {"comment":"Room for one character (STR_ALLOC_DESC; its text at $40D4)"},
        "437": {"comment":"HL = the new string's text"},
        "440": {"comment":"The key is its character"},
        "441": {"comment":"Finish the string result at STR_PUSH_TEMP"},
        "444": {"synopsis":"INKEY_FN without a key: the result is the empty string, whose descriptor is the zero byte at\n$1928 (a length of 0), with type 3.","comment":"$4121 = $1928, a zero byte"},
        "450": {"comment":"Type 3: a string"},
        "455": {"comment":"The text pointer back"},
        "457": {"synopsis":"The CLS statement (and the screen clear BASIC_INIT and KEY_LINE_EDIT use): sends $1C (home,\nnormal width) and $1F (clear to the screen's end) to the display through DISPLAY_CHAR.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"$1C: home, normal width"},
        "462": {"comment":"$1F: clear to the screen's end"},
        "467": {"synopsis":"The RANDOM statement: stores the refresh register R at $40AB, the middle byte of the 24-bit\nseed RND_NEXT steps ($40AA-$40AC).\nChanges: A.","comment":"A = R, which changes every instruction"},
        "469": {"comment":"$40AB = it"},
        "473": {"synopsis":"Writes one pulse to the cassette: port $FF bits 0-1 go 01, then 10, then 00, with short\nwaits between, and a longer wait after (the DJNZ loops). The other bits of port $FF are kept\nfrom its copy at $403D.\nChanges: AF, B, HL.","comment":"Bits 0-1 = 01"},
        "479": {"comment":"A short wait"},
        "481": {"synopsis":"TAPE_PULSE's first wait."},
        "483": {"comment":"Bits 0-1 = 10"},
        "491": {"synopsis":"TAPE_PULSE's second wait."},
        "493": {"comment":"Bits 0-1 = 00"},
        "499": {"comment":"A longer wait after the pulse"},
        "501": {"synopsis":"TAPE_PULSE's wait after the pulse."},
        "504": {"synopsis":"Stops the cassette motor: clears bit 2 of port $FF (the relay) through PORT_FF_UPDATE.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Keep HL"},
        "505": {"comment":"H = $FB clears bit 2"},
        "510": {"synopsis":"Selects the recorder and starts its motor. When the text holds \"#\", a number and a comma\nmust follow: -1 picks recorder 1, -2 recorder 2, anything else stops with error code 8\n(report FC); without \"#\" it is recorder 1. Port $FE gets 0 or $FF (bit 4 selects recorder\n2), then bit 2 of port $FF is set (the relay).\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL past \"#n,\" when there was one.\nChanges: AF, DE.","comment":"Does the text hold #?"},
        "513": {"comment":"A = 0: recorder 1, when no # is given"},
        "515": {"comment":"No #: recorder 1"},
        "517": {"comment":"DE = the number after #"},
        "520": {"comment":"A comma must follow"},
        "522": {"comment":"A = E AND D: $FF for -1, $FE for -2"},
        "524": {"comment":"Neither? Stop with error code 8 (report FC)"},
        "529": {"comment":"A = 0 for -1, $FF for -2"},
        "530": {"synopsis":"TAPE_START with A = the value for port $FE: writes it, then sets the motor bit.","comment":"Bit 4: recorder 2"},
        "533": {"comment":"Keep HL"},
        "534": {"comment":"L = 4 sets bit 2, the motor relay"},
        "537": {"synopsis":"TAPE_STOP's and TAPE_START's end: PORT_FF_UPDATE with the HL each set, then HL back.","comment":"Write port $FF"},
        "542": {"synopsis":"Writes port $FF its own last value again, which clears the cassette-in latch (bit 7 of a\nread of port $FF).\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"Keep every bit, add none"},
        "545": {"synopsis":"Writes port $FF from its copy at $403D: keeps the bits set in H, sets the bits set in L, and\nkeeps the result as the new copy.\nEntry: H = the bits to keep, L = the bits to set.\nExit: A = the value written.","comment":"The last value written to port $FF"},
        "548": {"comment":"Keep H's bits, set L's"},
        "550": {"comment":"Out; it also clears the cassette-in latch"},
        "552": {"comment":"The new copy"},
        "556": {"synopsis":"Toggles the top right corner of the screen ($3C3F) between \"*\" and a space ($2A XOR $0A =\n$20): shown once per block read.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"The corner's character"},
        "559": {"comment":"* and space swap"},
        "565": {"synopsis":"Reads a byte from the cassette, highest bit first, with 8 TAPE_READ_BITs.\nExit: A = the byte.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Keep BC and HL"},
        "567": {"comment":"8 bits"},
        "569": {"synopsis":"TAPE_READ_BYTE's loop: one more bit into A."},
        "577": {"synopsis":"Reads one bit from the cassette into A: waits for a pulse (bit 7 of port $FF, the\ncassette-in latch), waits past it, clears the latch, waits through the time a data pulse\nwould take and reads the latch again: set means a 1.\nEntry: A = the bits so far.\nExit: A shifted left, the new bit in bit 0.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Keep BC and A"},
        "579": {"synopsis":"TAPE_READ_BIT's wait for the clock pulse.","comment":"Has the latch caught a pulse?"},
        "584": {"comment":"Wait past the clock pulse"},
        "586": {"synopsis":"TAPE_READ_BIT's wait past the clock pulse."},
        "588": {"comment":"Clear the latch for the data pulse"},
        "591": {"comment":"Wait out the data pulse's time"},
        "593": {"synopsis":"TAPE_READ_BIT's wait for the data pulse."},
        "595": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: a data pulse, a 1"},
        "598": {"comment":"A, the bits so far"},
        "599": {"comment":"The latch into carry, then into A"},
        "602": {"comment":"Keep the result"},
        "603": {"comment":"Clear the latch again"},
        "609": {"synopsis":"Writes the byte in A to the cassette twice: once by calling TAPE_WRITE_BYTE, once by falling\ninto it.","comment":"Once here, once by falling in"},
        "612": {"synopsis":"Writes the byte in A to the cassette, highest bit first: each bit is a clock pulse\n(TAPE_PULSE), then a second pulse for a 1 or an equal wait for a 0.\nEntry: A = the byte.\nExit: every register kept.","comment":"Keep HL, BC, DE and AF"},
        "616": {"comment":"C = 8 bits"},
        "618": {"comment":"D = the byte"},
        "619": {"synopsis":"TAPE_WRITE_BYTE's loop, one bit a pass.","comment":"The clock pulse"},
        "622": {"comment":"The next bit into carry"},
        "625": {"comment":"A 0? A wait instead of the data pulse"},
        "627": {"comment":"A 1: the data pulse"},
        "630": {"synopsis":"TAPE_WRITE_BYTE: one bit done; the next, or the end.","comment":"8 bits done?"},
        "633": {"comment":"Every register back"},
        "638": {"synopsis":"TAPE_WRITE_BYTE's 0 bit: a wait as long as the data pulse.","comment":"About a pulse's time"},
        "640": {"synopsis":"TAPE_WBIT_ZERO's wait."},
        "644": {"synopsis":"Starts writing to the cassette: TAPE_START (with its optional \"#n,\"), then 255 zero bytes\nand the sync byte $A5.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nChanges: AF, B, DE.","comment":"Select the recorder, motor on"},
        "647": {"comment":"255 zeros, the leader"},
        "650": {"synopsis":"TAPE_WRITE_LEAD's loop writing the zeros."},
        "655": {"comment":"Then $A5, the sync byte"},
        "659": {"synopsis":"Starts reading the cassette: TAPE_START (with its optional \"#n,\"), then TAPE_SYNC_SEARCH.\nEntry: HL = the text.","comment":"Select the recorder, motor on"},
        "662": {"synopsis":"Reads bits from the cassette until the last eight read are $A5, the sync byte (so at any bit\noffset), then puts \"**\" at the top right corner of the screen ($3C3E-$3C3F).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Keep HL"},
        "663": {"comment":"No bits yet"},
        "664": {"synopsis":"TAPE_SYNC_SEARCH's loop, a bit a pass.","comment":"One more bit: are the last 8 $A5?"},
        "671": {"comment":"** at the corner: in sync"},
        "681": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM's end block ($78): reads the program's entry address (TAPE_READ_WORD), keeps it at\n$40DF, stops the motor and falls into SYSTEM_CMD to ask again.","comment":"HL = the entry address"},
        "684": {"comment":"$40DF = it, for /"},
        "687": {"comment":"Motor off"},
        "690": {"synopsis":"The SYSTEM statement: loads machine code from the cassette.\n1. Prints \"*\" (PROMPT_LINE adds \"? \") and reads a reply; BREAK goes on at NMI_RESTART, an\n   empty reply stops with error code 2 (report SN).\n2. \"/\" goes on at SYS_RUN. Anything else is a name: finds the sync (TAPE_FIND_SYNC), then a\n   header ($55) whose name matches the reply's first 6 characters.\n3. Reads blocks: $3C is a data block (length, a 0 meaning 256; load address; the bytes; a\n   checksum of the address's two bytes and the data), a bad checksum puts \"C\" at the\n   screen's top right; $78 is the end block (SYS_END_BLOCK). TAPE_BLINK marks each block.\n   Runs on its own stack ($4288) and does not return.","comment":"RAM hook at $41E2, a RET at power-on"},
        "693": {"comment":"A stack of its own"},
        "696": {"comment":"A new line"},
        "699": {"comment":"The prompt: *"},
        "704": {"comment":"Read the reply"},
        "707": {"comment":"BREAK? Back to BASIC at NMI_RESTART"},
        "710": {"comment":"The reply's first character"},
        "711": {"comment":"Empty? Stop with error code 2 (report SN)"},
        "714": {"comment":"/: run what was loaded"},
        "718": {"comment":"Motor on, in sync; HL = the name typed"},
        "721": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM_CMD's search for a header: bytes until $55, then the name.","comment":"Wait for $55, a header"},
        "728": {"comment":"Up to 6 characters of the name"},
        "730": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM_CMD's loop comparing the tape's name with the reply's.","comment":"End of the typed name?"},
        "734": {"comment":"Does the tape's character match?"},
        "738": {"comment":"No: look for the next header"},
        "743": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM_CMD's block loop: blinks the corner and reads the next block.","comment":"Blink the corner"},
        "746": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM_CMD: reads bytes until a block's type, $3C (data) or $78 (end).","comment":"The block's type byte"},
        "749": {"comment":"$78: the end block"},
        "753": {"comment":"$3C: a data block? Else read on"},
        "757": {"comment":"B = the length (0 for 256)"},
        "761": {"comment":"HL = where it loads"},
        "764": {"comment":"C = the checksum: H + L to start"},
        "766": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM_CMD's loop loading a data block's bytes and adding them to C.","comment":"A data byte"},
        "769": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "771": {"comment":"Into the checksum"},
        "775": {"comment":"The block's checksum: does it match?"},
        "779": {"comment":"Yes: the next block"},
        "781": {"comment":"No: C at the screen's top right"},
        "788": {"synopsis":"Reads a word from the cassette, low byte first.\nExit: HL = the word, A = its high byte.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"L first"},
        "792": {"comment":"Then H"},
        "797": {"synopsis":"SYSTEM's \"/\": jumps to the address after it, or to the entry address kept at $40DF when none\nis given. Text after the address asks again (SYSTEM_CMD's prompt).","comment":"DE = the entry address from the tape"},
        "802": {"comment":"Anything after the /?"},
        "803": {"comment":"Yes: DE = that address"},
        "806": {"comment":"Text after it? Ask again"},
        "808": {"comment":"Into the program"},
        "810": {"synopsis":"Sends a character to where BASIC's output goes, by $409C: 0 the display (DISPLAY_CHAR),\npositive the printer (PRINTER_CHAR), negative the cassette (TAPE_WRITE_BYTE). Calls the RAM\nhook at $41C1 first (a RET at power-on).\nEntry: A = the character.\nExit: A kept.","comment":"Keep BC"},
        "811": {"comment":"C = the character"},
        "812": {"comment":"RAM hook at $41C1, a RET at power-on"},
        "815": {"comment":"$409C: where output goes"},
        "819": {"comment":"The character back"},
        "821": {"comment":"Negative: to the cassette"},
        "824": {"comment":"Not zero: to the printer"},
        "826": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in A to the display (DISPLAY_OUT) and keeps the cursor's column at\n$40A6 (DISPLAY_COLUMN). Falls in from OUT_CHAR.\nEntry: A = the character.\nExit: A kept; DE kept.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "827": {"comment":"To the display"},
        "831": {"comment":"A = the cursor's column"},
        "834": {"comment":"$40A6 = it"},
        "840": {"synopsis":"Gives the display cursor's column from the low byte of its address ($4020, in the display's\nblock): 0-63, or 0-31 when bit 3 of the port $FF copy ($403D) says wide characters.\nExit: A = the column.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Bit 3 of the port $FF copy: wide?"},
        "845": {"comment":"The cursor's low byte, from its block"},
        "850": {"comment":"Wide: every other address, so halve it"},
        "853": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_COLUMN's end: the column within the row.","comment":"Column 0-63"},
        "856": {"synopsis":"Reads the keyboard once (KEY_READ), after the RAM hook at $41C4 (a RET at power-on).\nExit: A = a newly pressed key's code, or 0.\nChanges: AF; DE kept.","comment":"RAM hook at $41C4, a RET at power-on"},
        "859": {"comment":"Keep DE"},
        "860": {"comment":"A = a new key or 0"},
        "865": {"synopsis":"Reads a line from the keyboard into the line buffer (its address at $40A7), at most 240\ncharacters, through KEY_LINE_EDIT, and ends it with a zero. Clears the held key at $4099 and\nthe column at $40A6 first, and calls the RAM hook at $41AF (a RET at power-on).\nExit: HL = the byte before the buffer; carry set if the line was ended with BREAK, else A =\n0.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL; BC kept.","comment":"No key held over"},
        "869": {"comment":"Column 0"},
        "872": {"comment":"RAM hook at $41AF, a RET at power-on"},
        "875": {"comment":"Keep BC"},
        "876": {"comment":"HL = the line buffer"},
        "879": {"comment":"At most 240 characters"},
        "881": {"comment":"Read the line; B = the count typed"},
        "884": {"comment":"Keep the flags: carry for BREAK"},
        "885": {"comment":"BC = the count"},
        "889": {"comment":"A zero after the last character"},
        "891": {"comment":"HL = the buffer"},
        "896": {"comment":"The byte before it"},
        "897": {"comment":"BREAK? Return with carry"},
        "898": {"comment":"A = 0, carry clear"},
        "900": {"synopsis":"Waits for a key through KEY_GET (so through its RAM hook).\nExit: A = the key's code.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"A key at last?"},
        "907": {"synopsis":"Sends output back to the display ($409C = 0) and, when the printer's line holds characters\n(column $409B not 0), ends it with $0D (PRINTER_CHAR).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"$409C = 0: output to the display"},
        "911": {"comment":"Does the printer's line hold anything?"},
        "916": {"comment":"Yes: end it"},
        "924": {"synopsis":"Sends a character to the printer (PRINTER_OUT) and keeps the printer's column at $409B: $0C\nand $0D make it 0, $0A is sent as $0D (so also 0), anything else adds 1.\nEntry: A = the character.\nExit: every register kept.","comment":"Keep AF, DE and BC"},
        "927": {"comment":"C = the character"},
        "928": {"comment":"E = 0: the column after $0C or $0D"},
        "930": {"comment":"$0C?"},
        "934": {"comment":"$0A?"},
        "938": {"comment":"Sent as $0D"},
        "941": {"synopsis":"PRINTER_CHAR: a $0D gives column 0, anything else the column + 1.","comment":"$0D: column 0"},
        "945": {"comment":"Else the column + 1"},
        "950": {"synopsis":"PRINTER_CHAR: stores the new column and sends the character.","comment":"$409B = the printer's column"},
        "954": {"comment":"Out to the printer"},
        "962": {"synopsis":"Calls a device's driver. The device block's type byte must have every bit of B set; if it\ndoes not, $4033 is called instead (LD A,0 / RET at power-on).\nThe driver is called with IX = the block, C = A, the flags of CP 2 on the matching bits (Z\nfor an output call) and its address from bytes 1-2 of the block; it returns through $03DD,\nwhich restores DE, IX and HL and pops the BC that DEV_INPUT, DEV_OUTPUT or DEV_CONTROL\npushed.\nEntry: DE = the device block, A = the character, B = the bits asked for, the caller's BC on\nthe stack.\nExit: A as the driver leaves it.","comment":"IX = the device block"},
        "969": {"comment":"The driver returns to $03DD"},
        "973": {"comment":"C = the character"},
        "976": {"comment":"Has the type every bit asked for?"},
        "977": {"comment":"No: the hook at $4033"},
        "980": {"comment":"Z for an output call"},
        "988": {"comment":"Into the driver"},
        "995": {"synopsis":"The keyboard's driver: scans rows 0-6 ($3801, $3802, ... $3840) against the copy of each row\nkept from $4036, and turns the first key that was not down at the last scan into a code, n\nbeing its number (row * 8 + bit):\n- Rows 0-3: $40 + n; with SHIFT ($3880 bit 0) $60 + n, or n itself when $3840 bit 4 (the\n  down key) is down too.\n- Rows 4-5: $10 + n ($30-$3F), with bit 4 flipped for the last four keys; SHIFT flips bit 4.\n- Row 6: ROW6_KEY_CODES.\nThen waits $0DAC counts (DELAY_BC), and runs RST $28 when the code is $01 (BREAK).\nExit: A = the code, or 0 if no key went down.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE.","comment":"HL = the rows' copies"},
        "998": {"comment":"BC = row 0's address"},
        "1001": {"comment":"D = the row's number"},
        "1003": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN's loop over rows 0-6, comparing each with its copy.","comment":"Read the row"},
        "1005": {"comment":"Which keys changed?"},
        "1006": {"comment":"The row's copy for next time"},
        "1007": {"comment":"Any newly down?"},
        "1010": {"comment":"The next row"},
        "1012": {"comment":"Its address bit: $3801, $3802 ... $3840"},
        "1014": {"comment":"Up to $3840; at $3880 no key: A = 0"},
        "1018": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN with a newly pressed key in row D: makes n = row * 8 + the lowest new bit's number.","comment":"E = the new keys"},
        "1019": {"comment":"D = row * 8"},
        "1024": {"comment":"C = bit 0's mask"},
        "1026": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN's loop finding the lowest new key in the row.","comment":"This key?"},
        "1030": {"comment":"No: the next key number"},
        "1035": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN with n in D: turns it into a code by its row and SHIFT.","comment":"B = the SHIFT row"},
        "1039": {"comment":"A = $40 + n"},
        "1042": {"comment":"Rows 4-6?"},
        "1046": {"comment":"SHIFT down?"},
        "1048": {"comment":"No: $40 + n"},
        "1050": {"comment":"$60 + n"},
        "1053": {"comment":"And the down key ($3840 bit 4)?"},
        "1058": {"comment":"No: $60 + n"},
        "1060": {"comment":"Yes: n itself"},
        "1065": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN for rows 4-6: row 6 goes to KEY_ROW6; rows 4-5 give $10 + n.","comment":"Row 6?"},
        "1069": {"comment":"$10 + n: $30-$3F"},
        "1071": {"comment":"The last four keys ($3C-$3F)?"},
        "1075": {"comment":"Flip bit 4"},
        "1077": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN for rows 4-5: SHIFT flips bit 4 of the code.","comment":"SHIFT down?"},
        "1081": {"comment":"SHIFT flips bit 4"},
        "1085": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN for row 6: the code is from ROW6_KEY_CODES, two bytes a key, the second with SHIFT.","comment":"Two bytes per key"},
        "1086": {"comment":"SHIFT down?"},
        "1090": {"comment":"Yes: the second byte"},
        "1091": {"synopsis":"KEY_ROW6: reads the code from ROW6_KEY_CODES.","comment":"The code from ROW6_KEY_CODES"},
        "1099": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN: D = the key's code, then KEY_DEBOUNCE.","comment":"D = the code"},
        "1100": {"synopsis":"KEY_SCAN's end: waits $0DAC counts (DELAY_BC), returns the code in A and runs RST $28 for\nBREAK ($01).","comment":"A debounce wait"},
        "1106": {"comment":"A = the code"},
        "1107": {"comment":"BREAK ($01)?"},
        "1110": {"comment":"Yes: the RST $28 hook"},
        "1112": {"synopsis":"The display's driver (the block at $401D: bytes 3-4 the cursor's address in $3C00-$3FFF,\nbyte 5 the character under the cursor, 0 when the cursor is not shown).\n1. Input (carry from DEV_DISPATCH): DISP_READ gives the character at the cursor.\n2. Output: puts back the character under the cursor, then by the code in C: $00-$1F control\n   codes (DISP_CONTROL); $20-$3F stored as they are; $40-$7F stored as $00-$1F (lower case\n   as upper case); $80-$BF graphics, stored as they are; $C0-$FF n - $C0 spaces. Stepping\n   past $3FFF scrolls the screen up a row (DISP_STORE). Then DISP_CURSOR shows the cursor\n   again ($5F) and stores its address.\nEntry: IX = the block, C = the character, carry set for input.\nExit: A = the character sent (output).\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1118": {"comment":"Carry: an input call"},
        "1120": {"comment":"Is the cursor shown (byte 5 not 0)?"},
        "1126": {"comment":"Put back the character under it"},
        "1127": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_DRIVER: sorts the character in C by its range.","comment":"The character"},
        "1128": {"comment":"Below $20: a control code"},
        "1133": {"comment":"$80 or more: graphics or spaces"},
        "1137": {"comment":"$20-$3F: stored as they are"},
        "1141": {"comment":"$40-$5F become $00-$1F"},
        "1143": {"comment":"$60-$7F: $20 less again, to $00-$1F"},
        "1149": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_DRIVER: stores the character and steps the cursor (DISP_STORE).","comment":"Store it, step on"},
        "1152": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_DRIVER's end, where every control code returns too: keeps the cursor in $3C00-$3FFF,\nshows it ($5F, keeping the character under it in byte 5) if it is shown, and stores its\naddress in bytes 3-4.\nExit: A = C.","comment":"Keep the cursor in $3C00-$3FFF"},
        "1158": {"comment":"D = the character at the cursor"},
        "1159": {"comment":"Cursor shown?"},
        "1165": {"comment":"Keep the character under it"},
        "1168": {"comment":"Show the cursor, $5F"},
        "1170": {"synopsis":"DISP_CURSOR: stores the cursor's address in the block.","comment":"The cursor's address back to its block"},
        "1176": {"comment":"A = the character sent"},
        "1178": {"synopsis":"The display's input: A = the character at the cursor (the one kept under it when the cursor\nis shown).\nExit: A = the character.","comment":"Cursor shown? Its kept character"},
        "1183": {"comment":"Else the character at the cursor"},
        "1185": {"synopsis":"Display code $1D: the cursor to the start of its row.","comment":"The row's start"},
        "1190": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_DRIVER for $80-$FF: $80-$BF are graphics, stored as they are; $C0 is nothing;\n$C1-$FF write $01-$3F spaces.","comment":"$80-$BF: graphics, stored as they are"},
        "1194": {"comment":"$C0: nothing"},
        "1198": {"comment":"B = the number of spaces"},
        "1199": {"synopsis":"DISP_CODE_80's loop writing B spaces.","comment":"A space"},
        "1208": {"synopsis":"Display code $0E: shows the cursor, by keeping the character at it in byte 5 (a character\nstored as $00 leaves it hidden).","comment":"The character at the cursor"},
        "1209": {"synopsis":"Stores A in byte 5: the character under the cursor, 0 for no cursor.","comment":"Byte 5: 0 is no cursor"},
        "1213": {"synopsis":"Display code $0F: hides the cursor (byte 5 = 0).","comment":"0: no cursor"},
        "1216": {"synopsis":"Display code $1C: the cursor to the top left corner, $3C00, and normal width (bit 3 of port\n$FF cleared, in its copy at $403D too).","comment":"Top left"},
        "1219": {"comment":"Clear bit 3: normal width"},
        "1227": {"comment":"To port $FF"},
        "1230": {"synopsis":"Display code $08: the cursor back one character (two addresses when wide) and blanks it.","comment":"Back one"},
        "1231": {"comment":"Wide?"},
        "1238": {"comment":"Yes: back one more"},
        "1239": {"synopsis":"DISP_BACKSPACE: blanks the character.","comment":"A space"},
        "1242": {"synopsis":"Display code $18: the cursor left one character (twice DISP_LEFT_ONE when wide).","comment":"Wide?"},
        "1247": {"comment":"Yes: one step here, one by falling in"},
        "1250": {"synopsis":"Moves the cursor left one address; from column 0 it goes to the end of the same row.","comment":"At column 0?"},
        "1253": {"comment":"Back one"},
        "1254": {"comment":"Not at column 0: done"},
        "1255": {"synopsis":"Display code $1A: the cursor a row down (64 on); DISP_LEFT_ONE also comes here to reach its\nrow's end.","comment":"64 on"},
        "1260": {"synopsis":"Display code $19: the cursor right one address; from the row's end it goes to the start of\nthe same row.","comment":"On one"},
        "1261": {"comment":"Into the next row?"},
        "1265": {"synopsis":"Display code $1B: the cursor a row up (64 back); DISP_RIGHT also comes here to reach its\nrow's start.","comment":"64 back"},
        "1270": {"synopsis":"Display code $17: wide characters (bit 3 of port $FF set, in its copy at $403D too), and the\ncursor to the next even address.","comment":"Set bit 3: wide characters"},
        "1278": {"comment":"To port $FF"},
        "1280": {"comment":"The cursor to an even address"},
        "1286": {"synopsis":"DISPLAY_DRIVER for codes $00-$1F; each returns to DISP_CURSOR. $08 back and blank, $0A-$0D a\nnew line, $0E cursor on, $0F cursor off, $17 wide, $18 left, $19 right, $1A down, $1B up,\n$1C home, $1D the row's start, $1E clear to the row's end, $1F clear to the screen's end;\nthe others do nothing.","comment":"Each code returns to DISP_CURSOR"},
        "1290": {"comment":"$08: back and blank"},
        "1294": {"comment":"Others below $0A: nothing"},
        "1297": {"comment":"$0A-$0D: a new line"},
        "1301": {"comment":"$0E: cursor on"},
        "1303": {"comment":"$0F: cursor off"},
        "1307": {"comment":"$17: wide"},
        "1311": {"comment":"$18: left"},
        "1315": {"comment":"$19: right"},
        "1319": {"comment":"$1A: down"},
        "1323": {"comment":"$1B: up"},
        "1327": {"comment":"$1C: home"},
        "1331": {"comment":"$1D: the row's start"},
        "1336": {"comment":"$1E: clear to the row's end"},
        "1340": {"comment":"$1F: clear to the screen's end"},
        "1344": {"comment":"Others: nothing"},
        "1345": {"synopsis":"Stores A at the cursor and steps it on (two addresses when wide); past $3FFF it scrolls:\nrows 1-15 move up one (DISP_SCROLL), the last row is cleared and the cursor goes back a row.\nEntry: HL = the cursor, A = the character.\nExit: HL = the new cursor.\nChanges: AF, DE.","comment":"Store it"},
        "1347": {"comment":"Wide?"},
        "1354": {"comment":"Yes: one more"},
        "1355": {"synopsis":"DISP_STORE: past the screen's end?","comment":"Past $3FFF?"},
        "1359": {"comment":"Back a row, to the last"},
        "1363": {"comment":"Keep the cursor"},
        "1364": {"synopsis":"Moves rows 1-15 up one ($3C40-$3FFF to $3C00) and clears the last row; the cursor kept on\nthe stack is popped at the end.","comment":"Rows 1-15 up one"},
        "1370": {"comment":"Keep BC"},
        "1377": {"comment":"HL = $3FC0, the last row"},
        "1380": {"synopsis":"Display codes $0A-$0D: the cursor to the next row's start and that row cleared; from the\nlast row the screen scrolls.","comment":"The row's start"},
        "1384": {"comment":"Keep it, for a scroll"},
        "1385": {"comment":"The next row"},
        "1389": {"comment":"Past the screen? Scroll"},
        "1394": {"comment":"Drop the old row's start"},
        "1395": {"synopsis":"Display code $1E: clears from the cursor to the end of its row; DISP_NEW_LINE uses it on the\nnew row.","comment":"Keep the cursor"},
        "1396": {"comment":"DE = the next row's start"},
        "1404": {"synopsis":"Display code $1F: clears from the cursor to the end of the screen.","comment":"Keep the cursor"},
        "1405": {"synopsis":"Clears from the cursor to $3FFF, then HL from the stack.","comment":"DE = $4000, the screen's end"},
        "1408": {"synopsis":"Writes spaces from HL up to DE, then the cursor from the stack.","comment":"A space"},
        "1411": {"comment":"Up to DE?"},
        "1419": {"comment":"The cursor back"},
        "1421": {"synopsis":"The printer's driver (the block at $4025: byte 3 the lines per page, 0 for no paging, byte 4\nthe lines printed on this page). By the character in C:\n- 0: the status (PRN_STATUS), Z when ready.\n- $0B, and $0C when paging: line feeds ($0A) to the page's end, then the line count is 0.\n- Anything else: waits until ready and writes it to port $FD; a $0D counts a line, and a\n  full page sets the count to 0.\nEntry: IX = the block, C = the character.\nExit: A = the character.\nChanges: AF, B.","comment":"The character"},
        "1423": {"comment":"0: the status"},
        "1425": {"comment":"$0B: to the page's end"},
        "1429": {"comment":"$0C?"},
        "1433": {"comment":"Lines per page 0? Print $0C as it is"},
        "1439": {"synopsis":"PRINTER_DRIVER: line feeds to the end of the page, then the line count is 0.","comment":"B = the lines left on the page"},
        "1446": {"synopsis":"PRN_FORM_FEED's loop, one line feed a pass.","comment":"Wait until ready"},
        "1451": {"comment":"A line feed"},
        "1454": {"comment":"To the printer"},
        "1456": {"comment":"Every line left"},
        "1460": {"synopsis":"PRINTER_DRIVER: writes the character when the printer is ready and counts a line on $0D.","comment":"Keep the character"},
        "1461": {"synopsis":"PRN_CHAR's wait until the printer is ready.","comment":"Ready?"},
        "1468": {"comment":"To the printer"},
        "1470": {"comment":"$0D?"},
        "1473": {"comment":"Yes: one more line on the page"},
        "1476": {"comment":"Page full?"},
        "1482": {"comment":"A = the character"},
        "1484": {"synopsis":"PRINTER_DRIVER: the line count back to 0 (a new page).","comment":"Line count 0"},
        "1489": {"synopsis":"Reads the printer's status from port $FD.\nExit: A = bits 4-7 of it; Z set when they read %0011, which the driver takes as ready.","comment":"Bits 4-7 of the status"},
        "1494": {"comment":"%0011: ready?"},
        "1497": {"synopsis":"Reads a line from the keyboard into a buffer, showing it on the display with the cursor on.\nCharacters $20 and up and $0A are stored and shown while there is room (B), else ignored.\nNEW LINE ($0D) ends the line; BREAK ($01) ends it with carry; CLEAR ($1F) clears the screen\nand starts the line again. $08 takes back a character, $18 the whole line (both stop at a\nstored $0A), $09 pads with spaces to the next multiple of 8 columns, $19 sends $17 (wide\ncharacters); other codes are ignored.\nAt the end it stores $0D after the text and shows it, hides the cursor ($0F) and gives the\ncount.\nEntry: HL = the buffer, B = the most characters.\nExit: HL = the buffer, B = the characters typed, carry set for BREAK.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE.","comment":"Keep the buffer's start"},
        "1498": {"comment":"$0E: show the cursor"},
        "1503": {"comment":"C = the room at the start"},
        "1504": {"synopsis":"KEY_LINE_EDIT's loop, one key a pass; editing keys return here.","comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "1507": {"comment":"A character to store?"},
        "1511": {"comment":"NEW LINE: end"},
        "1516": {"comment":"CLEAR: start again"},
        "1520": {"comment":"BREAK: end with carry"},
        "1524": {"comment":"Editing keys return to KEY_LINE_LOOP"},
        "1528": {"comment":"$08: back one"},
        "1532": {"comment":"$18: back to the start"},
        "1536": {"comment":"Right arrow: to the next tab stop"},
        "1540": {"comment":"Shift + right arrow: wide characters"},
        "1544": {"comment":"Down arrow ($0A) is stored like a character; others are ignored"},
        "1546": {"comment":"Any other control key: back to the key loop through KEY_LINE_LOOP"},
        "1547": {"comment":"Drop the pushed key-loop address"},
        "1548": {"synopsis":"Line input: stores the key in the buffer and echoes it, if there is room.\nEntry: A = the key, HL = the next free byte, B = the room left.\nExit: back to the key loop at KEY_LINE_LOOP; with no room left the byte is written but not\nkept","comment":"Into the buffer (overwritten next time if there is no room)"},
        "1549": {"comment":"Room left?"},
        "1551": {"comment":"No: wait for the next key"},
        "1553": {"comment":"Echo it and step past it"},
        "1558": {"comment":"One byte less room"},
        "1561": {"synopsis":"Line input, CLEAR key: clears the screen and starts the line again at the buffer's start.","comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "1564": {"comment":"Room back to the whole buffer"},
        "1565": {"comment":"HL = the buffer's start, kept on the stack"},
        "1567": {"comment":"Back to the key loop"},
        "1570": {"synopsis":"Rubs out one character, then stops at a stored line feed ($0A) or at the line's start.","comment":"Rub out one character"},
        "1573": {"comment":"The character now before the cursor"},
        "1576": {"comment":"A line feed: stop there"},
        "1579": {"synopsis":"Line input, shift + left arrow: rubs out characters back to the start of the line or to the\nlast line feed typed.\nEntry: B = the room left, C = the buffer's size.","comment":"Anything typed?"},
        "1581": {"comment":"Yes: rub out another"},
        "1584": {"synopsis":"Line input, left arrow: rubs out the last character typed, unless the line is empty or the\nlast character is a line feed.\nEntry: HL = the next free byte, B = the room left, C = the buffer's size.\nExit: HL one back and B one more, with the cursor moved back ($08 to the display).","comment":"Nothing typed?"},
        "1587": {"comment":"Look at the last character"},
        "1589": {"comment":"A line feed is not rubbed out"},
        "1593": {"comment":"Step back over it"},
        "1594": {"comment":"$08: the display moves the cursor back and blanks"},
        "1599": {"comment":"One more byte of room"},
        "1601": {"synopsis":"Line input, shift + right arrow: sends $17 to the display, which switches it to wide\n(32-column) characters by setting bit 3 of $403D (DISP_WIDE)."},
        "1606": {"synopsis":"Line input, right arrow: stores and echoes spaces up to the next column that is a multiple\nof 8, as far as the room allows.\nEntry: HL = the next free byte, B = the room left.","comment":"A = the cursor's column"},
        "1609": {"comment":"E = 8 - column MOD 8: spaces to the next tab stop"},
        "1616": {"synopsis":"One space per pass, until the tab stop or the end of the room.","comment":"No room left?"},
        "1619": {"comment":"Store a space"},
        "1623": {"comment":"Echo it"},
        "1628": {"comment":"One byte less room"},
        "1629": {"comment":"Reached the tab stop?"},
        "1633": {"synopsis":"Line input, BREAK key: ends the line like NEW LINE but with carry set.","comment":"Carry flags BREAK to the caller"},
        "1634": {"synopsis":"Line input, NEW LINE (and BREAK): ends the buffer with $0D, echoes it, turns the cursor off\nand returns.\nExit: HL = the buffer's start, B = the number of characters typed (the $0D not counted), A =\nthe key; carry set for BREAK.","comment":"Keep the key and the BREAK flag"},
        "1635": {"comment":"$0D ends the buffer"},
        "1638": {"comment":"Echo the new line"},
        "1641": {"comment":"$0F: cursor off"},
        "1646": {"comment":"B = size - room left: the length typed"},
        "1649": {"comment":"Back the key and the flag"},
        "1650": {"comment":"HL = the buffer's start"},
        "1652": {"synopsis":"The power-on sequence after POWER_ON:\n1. Writes 0 to port $FF.\n2. Copies RAM_VECTOR_IMAGE to $4000-$4035, 128 times over (A goes down by 2 from 0 each\n   pass), which also makes a delay.\n3. Zeroes the next 39 bytes, $4036-$405C.\n4. Goes on at BASIC_INIT, through SECTOR_BOOT's test unless BREAK ($3840 bit 2) is held. The\n   test reads $37EC, which on the HT-1080Z is extension ROM and reads $FF, so it is\n   BASIC_INIT either way.\nObserved: a power-on reached BASIC_INIT and never SECTOR_BOOT.\nDoes not return.","comment":"A = 0 from POWER_ON"},
        "1654": {"synopsis":"BOOT's copy of RAM_VECTOR_IMAGE to $4000, repeated until A is back to 0."},
        "1665": {"comment":"A down by 2: 128 passes to reach 0 again"},
        "1669": {"comment":"Then zero 39 bytes from $4036"},
        "1671": {"synopsis":"BOOT's loop zeroing B bytes from DE on."},
        "1675": {"comment":"BREAK held (row 6 bit 2)? Straight to BASIC"},
        "1683": {"comment":"A stack in RAM for SECTOR_BOOT"},
        "1686": {"comment":"Is $37EC $FF or $00 (A + 1 below 2)?"},
        "1692": {"comment":"Yes (always on the HT-1080Z): BASIC"},
        "1695": {"synopsis":"Reads 256 bytes through the addresses $37E1 and $37EC-$37EF into $4200 and jumps there. On\nthe HT-1080Z those addresses are extension ROM, where writes are lost: BOOT never comes here\n(see BOOT), and nothing else calls it but the JP at $000D.\nDoes not return."},
        "1714": {"comment":"Wait while bit 0 of $37EC is set","synopsis":"SECTOR_BOOT's wait while bit 0 of $37EC is set."},
        "1728": {"comment":"Wait for bit 1 of $37EC, then take a byte from $37EF","synopsis":"SECTOR_BOOT's loop: waits for bit 1 of $37EC, then copies a byte from $37EF to (BC), 256\ntimes."},
        "1737": {"comment":"Run what was read"},
        "1740": {"synopsis":"The NMI's way back to BASIC: RESET_TO_BC resets the stack and continues at $1A18, the POP BC\ninside the LD A,$C1 at $1A17, and on into BASIC's prompt.\nObserved: after the RESET key, $1A18 and BASIC_READY were reached and READY printed.","comment":"BC = where RESET_TO_BC continues"},
        "1746": {"synopsis":"The image BOOT copies to RAM at $4000-$4035 (54 bytes; a run read it back there).\n$4000-$4014, seven 3-byte entries, the targets of the restarts: RST $08 JP EXPECT_CHAR, RST\n$10 JP NEXT_CHAR, RST $18 JP CP_HL_DE, RST $20 JP TYPE_FLAGS, RST $28 RET, RST $30 RET, RST\n$38 EI / RET.\n$4015, $401D and $4025: three 8-byte device blocks, each a type byte, the driver's address\nand two letters, which DEV_DISPATCH reads: $01, KEY_SCAN, \"KI\" (KEY_READ's block); $07,\nDISPLAY_DRIVER, with $3C00 at bytes 3-4, \"DO\" (DISPLAY_OUT's); $06, PRINTER_DRIVER, \"PR\"\n(PRINTER_OUT's). $402D JP $5000, $4030 RST $00, $4033 LD A,0 / RET: DEV_DISPATCH jumps to\n$4033 when a block's type lacks the bits asked for."},
        "1800": {"synopsis":"Adds 0.5 (the constant at SNG_HALF) to the floating-point accumulator.\nChanges: as FP_ADD.","comment":"The constant 0.5"},
        "1803": {"synopsis":"Adds the single-precision number at HL to the accumulator.\nEntry: HL = four bytes, low mantissa byte first, exponent last.","comment":"BCDE = the number at HL"},
        "1808": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the accumulator from the single-precision number at HL; the result is left in the\naccumulator.","comment":"BCDE = the number at HL"},
        "1811": {"synopsis":"Single-precision subtraction: the accumulator becomes BCDE minus the accumulator (the\naccumulator's sign is turned, then FP_ADD). The single-precision table at SINGLE_OP_TABLE\nreaches it as its second entry.","comment":"Turn the accumulator's sign"},
        "1814": {"synopsis":"Single-precision addition: the accumulator ($4121-$4124) becomes BCDE plus the accumulator.\nThe single-precision table at SINGLE_OP_TABLE reaches it as its first entry.\nEntry: BCDE = a number, B its exponent, C its sign and top mantissa bits.\n1. If BCDE is zero nothing changes; if the accumulator is zero BCDE is stored.\n2. The larger exponent goes in the accumulator; BCDE is shifted right by the difference (25\n   bits or more apart, the smaller number is simply lost).\n3. Mantissas are added (same signs) or subtracted, then normalised, rounded and packed.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"BCDE zero?"},
        "1817": {"comment":"The accumulator's exponent"},
        "1820": {"comment":"Accumulator zero: the result is BCDE"},
        "1824": {"comment":"Exponent difference"},
        "1825": {"comment":"The accumulator is the larger: no swap"},
        "1827": {"comment":"Negate the difference"},
        "1829": {"comment":"Push the accumulator, put BCDE in its place"},
        "1837": {"comment":"BCDE = the old accumulator"},
        "1839": {"synopsis":"Aligns BCDE on the accumulator and adds or subtracts the mantissas.\nEntry: A = the exponent difference, the accumulator holding the larger number.","comment":"25 bits or more apart: the accumulator stands"},
        "1842": {"comment":"Keep the difference"},
        "1843": {"comment":"Implicit top bits back; H bit 7 set if the signs agree"},
        "1848": {"comment":"Shift BCDE's mantissa right by the difference"},
        "1851": {"comment":"Signs differ?"},
        "1855": {"comment":"Yes: subtract"},
        "1858": {"comment":"Add the accumulator's mantissa to CDE"},
        "1861": {"comment":"No carry out: round and store"},
        "1864": {"comment":"Carry out: one more in the exponent"},
        "1866": {"comment":"Exponent wrapped: overflow"},
        "1869": {"comment":"Shift the carry back in from the top, one bit"},
        "1876": {"synopsis":"Subtracts BCDE's mantissa from the accumulator's (B is the extension byte), negating the\nresult if it went below zero, then normalises it.","comment":"Extension byte: 0 - B"},
        "1879": {"comment":"Low byte: accumulator - E"},
        "1883": {"comment":"Middle byte"},
        "1887": {"comment":"Top byte"},
        "1890": {"synopsis":"Negates C:D:E:B if carry is set (turning the sign flag at $4125), then normalises.","comment":"Negative: two's complement and turn the sign"},
        "1893": {"synopsis":"Normalises the mantissa C:D:H:L (C on top, L the extension byte) so that bit 7 of C is set,\ntakes the shift off the exponent at $4124, then rounds and packs (FP_ROUND_PACK). A result\nof zero, or an exponent that runs out, gives zero.\nEntry: C, D, E, B the mantissa (moved here into C, D, H, L).","comment":"L = extension, H = low byte"},
        "1895": {"comment":"B counts the shift, in bits"},
        "1896": {"synopsis":"While the top byte is zero, shifts the mantissa left a whole byte."},
        "1897": {"comment":"Top byte zero?"},
        "1899": {"comment":"No: shift bit by bit"},
        "1901": {"comment":"Up one byte"},
        "1905": {"comment":"Count 8 more bits"},
        "1908": {"comment":"All four bytes zero?"},
        "1912": {"synopsis":"Makes the accumulator zero (exponent 0) and returns.","comment":"Exponent 0 means the number is zero"},
        "1913": {"synopsis":"Stores A as the accumulator's exponent; the call at $1405 comes here with A = 0, a zero\nresult."},
        "1917": {"synopsis":"Shifts the mantissa C:D:H:L left one bit and counts it in B.","comment":"One bit more of shift"},
        "1918": {"comment":"Shift low bytes, carry out of H"},
        "1919": {"comment":"Into D"},
        "1922": {"comment":"Into C"},
        "1925": {"synopsis":"Repeats the one-bit shift until bit 7 of C is set, then takes the shift off the exponent.","comment":"Top bit still clear?"},
        "1928": {"comment":"A = the shift (minus)"},
        "1929": {"comment":"Back to C:D:E, B the extension"},
        "1931": {"comment":"No shift: round and store"},
        "1934": {"comment":"Exponent minus the shift"},
        "1939": {"comment":"No carry: it went below zero, result zero"},
        "1941": {"comment":"Exponent exactly 0: zero"},
        "1942": {"synopsis":"Rounds C:D:E by the extension byte B (up when bit 7 is set), puts the sign from $4125 into\nbit 7 of C and stores BCDE in the accumulator.\nEntry: C:D:E normalised, B = the extension byte, $4124 = the exponent. The division enters\nat FP_ROUND_A (inside LD A,B) with the rounding bit in bit 7 of A.","comment":"A = the extension byte"},
        "1943": {"synopsis":"FP_ROUND_PACK's entry with the rounding byte already in A (bit 7 set rounds up), from\nFP_DIV."},
        "1946": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: round up"},
        "1950": {"comment":"B = the exponent"},
        "1951": {"comment":"$4125: bit 7 set for a positive result"},
        "1953": {"comment":"Sign into C's top bit"},
        "1957": {"comment":"Store BCDE in the accumulator"},
        "1960": {"synopsis":"Adds one to the mantissa C:D:E; if it carries out of the top, the mantissa becomes $800000\nand the exponent at (HL) goes up by one.\nEntry: HL = $4124 when the carry can happen.","comment":"Low byte up; no carry: done"},
        "1962": {"comment":"Middle byte"},
        "1964": {"comment":"Top byte"},
        "1966": {"comment":"Carried out: mantissa $80 00 00"},
        "1968": {"comment":"And the exponent one up"},
        "1970": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 10 (report OV): a result too large for the exponent.","comment":"Error code 10: OV"},
        "1975": {"synopsis":"Adds the three mantissa bytes at HL ($4121 on) to E, D, C, with carry between them.\nExit: carry set if the top byte carried out; HL two on; A = C."},
        "1987": {"synopsis":"Negates C:D:E:B as a 32-bit two's complement number (B lowest) and turns the sign flag at\n$4125.","comment":"Turn the sign flag"},
        "1993": {"comment":"A = L = 0"},
        "1995": {"comment":"0 - B"},
        "1997": {"comment":"0 - E - borrow"},
        "2000": {"comment":"0 - D - borrow"},
        "2003": {"comment":"0 - C - borrow"},
        "2007": {"synopsis":"Shifts the mantissa C:D:E right by A bits; the bits shifted out go into B, the extension\nbyte.\nEntry: A = the bit count, 0 to 24.\nExit: A = 0, L = 0.","comment":"Extension byte starts empty"},
        "2009": {"synopsis":"Whole bytes first: shifts down one byte per 8 bits of count.","comment":"Fewer than 8 bits left?"},
        "2013": {"comment":"Down a byte"},
        "2016": {"comment":"Zero in at the top"},
        "2020": {"synopsis":"L = the bits left + 1, for the one-bit loop.","comment":"Bits left, plus one"},
        "2023": {"synopsis":"One bit per pass, a zero in at the top.","comment":"Carry clear: a zero shifts in"},
        "2024": {"comment":"Done?"},
        "2027": {"synopsis":"Shifts C:D:E:B right one bit with carry in at the top; FP_ADD enters here with L = 1 to put\nthe carry out of the mantissa addition back in.","comment":"Carry into C's top bit"},
        "2035": {"comment":"Out of E into the extension byte"},
        "2040": {"synopsis":"The single-precision constant 1.0 (00 00 00 81), read by LOG_FN at $0828 and at $1458."},
        "2044": {"synopsis":"LOG_FN's series for POLY_EVAL at ODD_POLY: a count (3), then three single-precision\ncoefficients, about 0.59898, 0.96147 and 2.88539, each read as four bytes by\nFP_LOAD_MEM/BCDE_FROM_MEM."},
        "2057": {"synopsis":"The LOG function: the natural logarithm of the accumulator. Stops with error code 8 (report\nFC) for zero or a negative number.\n1. Splits off the binary exponent k, leaving m in 0.5 to 1.\n2. t = 1 - sqrt 2 / (m + sqrt 1/2), the series at LOG_SERIES evaluated on t (ODD_POLY).\n3. Adds -0.5 and k, then multiplies by ln 2 (FP_MUL_LN2).","comment":"A = 1 positive, 0 zero, $FF negative"},
        "2060": {"comment":"Zero or negative: error code 8 (FC)"},
        "2064": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "2068": {"comment":"BCDE = 0.70711 (sqrt 1/2)"},
        "2074": {"comment":"A = the exponent less the bias: k"},
        "2076": {"comment":"Accumulator now m, between 0.5 and 1"},
        "2077": {"comment":"Keep sqrt 1/2"},
        "2079": {"comment":"m + sqrt 1/2"},
        "2084": {"comment":"BCDE = 1.41421 (sqrt 2)"},
        "2085": {"comment":"sqrt 2 / (m + sqrt 1/2)"},
        "2088": {"comment":"1 - that"},
        "2094": {"comment":"The series on it"},
        "2100": {"comment":"BCDE = -0.5"},
        "2106": {"comment":"Add it"},
        "2109": {"comment":"k"},
        "2110": {"comment":"Add k as a number"},
        "2113": {"synopsis":"Multiplies the accumulator by ln 2 (0.69315, from BCDE $80 31 72 18); falls into FP_MUL.","comment":"BCDE = 0.69315 (ln 2)"},
        "2119": {"synopsis":"Single-precision multiplication: the accumulator becomes BCDE times the accumulator. The\nsingle-precision table at SINGLE_OP_TABLE reaches it as its third entry.\n1. The exponents are added (FP_EXP_COMBINE), which also settles the result's sign.\n2. BCDE's mantissa is kept at $414F-$4151 and added, shifted, for each set bit of the\n   accumulator's three mantissa bytes, lowest first (MUL_BYTE, run three times by RET).\n3. The product is normalised by FP_NORMALIZE.","comment":"Accumulator zero: result zero"},
        "2123": {"comment":"L = 0: add the exponents"},
        "2128": {"comment":"Top byte of the multiplicand"},
        "2132": {"comment":"Its lower two bytes"},
        "2136": {"comment":"Product starts at zero"},
        "2141": {"comment":"Normalise when the bytes are done"},
        "2145": {"comment":"Two more passes of MUL_BYTE by RET"},
        "2150": {"comment":"First byte of the accumulator's mantissa"},
        "2153": {"synopsis":"Multiplies the product by one byte of the accumulator's mantissa, bit by bit; RET carries on\nwith the next byte (the addresses FP_MUL pushed).\nEntry: HL = the byte.","comment":"The multiplier byte"},
        "2155": {"comment":"Zero: just shift a byte"},
        "2158": {"comment":"Keep the pointer"},
        "2159": {"comment":"Eight bits"},
        "2161": {"synopsis":"One multiplier bit: adds the multiplicand if it is set, then shifts.","comment":"Next bit into carry"},
        "2164": {"comment":"Bit clear: just shift"},
        "2167": {"comment":"Add the multiplicand's low bytes"},
        "2173": {"comment":"And its top byte"},
        "2177": {"synopsis":"Shifts the product C:D:E:B right one bit, the add's carry in on top.","comment":"Carry into the product's top"},
        "2188": {"comment":"One bit fewer"},
        "2189": {"comment":"Rest of the multiplier byte"},
        "2190": {"comment":"More bits?"},
        "2192": {"synopsis":"Drops the top word of the stack and returns: in MUL_BYTE the saved pointer, in\nFP_EXP_COMBINE (and at $2FA6) the caller's return, so the caller's caller gets a zero\nresult."},
        "2194": {"synopsis":"For a zero multiplier byte: shifts the product right a whole byte.","comment":"Down a byte, zero in at the top"},
        "2199": {"synopsis":"Divides the accumulator by 10 (the constant at $0DD8).","comment":"Keep the number"},
        "2202": {"comment":"The accumulator becomes 10.0"},
        "2208": {"synopsis":"Divides the number on the stack (pushed by FP_PUSH) by the accumulator.","comment":"BCDE = the pushed number"},
        "2210": {"synopsis":"Single-precision division: the accumulator becomes BCDE divided by the accumulator. The\nsingle-precision table at SINGLE_OP_TABLE reaches it as its fourth entry. Stops with error\ncode 20 (report /0) for a zero divisor.\n1. The exponents are subtracted (FP_EXP_COMBINE with L = $FF).\n2. The divisor's mantissa is written into the subtract routine in RAM at $4080 (copied from\n   RAM_IMAGE_4080 at power-on): at $4081, $4085 and $4089.\n3. A restoring division takes the dividend B:H:L (with a top byte at $408C) one bit at a\n   time, building the quotient in C:D:E, until bit 7 of C is set.","comment":"Divisor zero?"},
        "2213": {"comment":"Error code 20: /0"},
        "2216": {"comment":"L = $FF: subtract the exponents"},
        "2221": {"comment":"Two up: corrects the quotient's position"},
        "2223": {"comment":"Top byte of the divisor"},
        "2225": {"comment":"Into the RAM routine's third SBC"},
        "2230": {"comment":"Into its second SBC"},
        "2235": {"comment":"Into its first SUB"},
        "2238": {"comment":"Dividend: B:H:L"},
        "2240": {"comment":"Quotient starts at zero"},
        "2244": {"comment":"The dividend's top byte (the RAM routine's LD A,n)"},
        "2247": {"synopsis":"One quotient bit: tries to subtract the divisor; keeps the difference if it did not go below\nzero (carry = the quotient bit).","comment":"Keep the dividend, in case"},
        "2249": {"comment":"B:H:L minus the divisor, A = the top byte"},
        "2253": {"comment":"Borrow from the top byte"},
        "2256": {"comment":"Below zero: back the old dividend ($08D9)"},
        "2258": {"comment":"Keep the new top byte"},
        "2261": {"comment":"Drop the saved dividend"},
        "2263": {"comment":"Quotient bit 1"},
        "2264": {"synopsis":"JP NC never jumps here (carry is set): it skips POP BC / POP HL at $08D9-$08DA, the entry\nFP_DIV_LOOP takes when the subtraction went below zero (it backs the dividend).","comment":"Bytes C1 E1: POP BC, POP HL at $08D9"},
        "2267": {"comment":"Quotient's top byte; carry kept"},
        "2270": {"comment":"Bit 7 of C set: full; next bit as rounding"},
        "2274": {"comment":"Quotient bit in at the bottom"},
        "2284": {"comment":"Dividend left one bit"},
        "2288": {"comment":"With its top byte"},
        "2295": {"comment":"Any quotient bit yet?"},
        "2300": {"comment":"None: one off the exponent"},
        "2306": {"comment":"Exponent left: go on"},
        "2308": {"comment":"Ran out: error code 10 (OV)"},
        "2311": {"synopsis":"Double precision: combines the exponents of the accumulator and the second operand at\n$4127-$412E for a division (B = the operand's exponent turned). The bytes 2E AF at $0909\nhide XOR A at $090A, the entry for a multiplication (B = the exponent itself). Both then\nload C = the operand's sign byte ($412D) and go on into FP_EXP_COMBINE with L = 0.","comment":"$FF: the exponent turned, for a division"},
        "2313": {"comment":"Bytes AF at $090A: XOR A, the multiplication's entry"},
        "2315": {"comment":"The operand's sign and top mantissa byte"},
        "2319": {"comment":"Its exponent, turned or not"},
        "2322": {"comment":"L = 0"},
        "2324": {"synopsis":"Adds (L = 0) or subtracts (L = $FF, by adding the turned exponent) the exponents of BCDE and\nthe accumulator, stores the result at $4124 and the result's sign flag at $4125.\nEntry: B = the operand's exponent, C its sign byte.\nExit: HL = $4124; the implicit top bits set in C and at $4123. Out of range it does not\nreturn: the caller's caller gets zero, or error code 10 (OV).","comment":"Operand zero?"},
        "2328": {"comment":"0 or $FF"},
        "2332": {"comment":"The accumulator's exponent, complemented when A is $FF"},
        "2333": {"comment":"Plus the operand's"},
        "2335": {"comment":"Carry and bit 7 agree: out of range"},
        "2341": {"comment":"Take the bias off again"},
        "2344": {"comment":"Exponent zero: drop the caller, result zero"},
        "2347": {"comment":"Unpack; A bit 7 set if the signs agree"},
        "2350": {"comment":"The result's sign flag"},
        "2351": {"synopsis":"DEC HL and return; also reached from $10B8 and $11EF."},
        "2353": {"synopsis":"For an argument outside the exponent's range (the EXP code at $144A and $1454): drops two\nstacked words, then gives zero for a negative accumulator, otherwise error code 10 (OV).","comment":"Sign of the accumulator"},
        "2356": {"comment":"Turned: plus for a negative number"},
        "2358": {"synopsis":"The exponent was out of range: bit 7 of A says which way."},
        "2359": {"synopsis":"Drops the caller's return; then zero if the flags say plus, else error code 10 (OV).","comment":"Drop the caller"},
        "2360": {"comment":"Too small: zero"},
        "2363": {"comment":"Too large: error code 10 (OV)"},
        "2366": {"synopsis":"Multiplies the accumulator by 10: four times itself (exponent + 2) plus itself, doubled.\nStops with error code 10 (report OV) if the exponent overflows.","comment":"BCDE = the accumulator"},
        "2369": {"comment":"Zero stays zero"},
        "2372": {"comment":"Times four"},
        "2374": {"comment":"Too large"},
        "2378": {"comment":"x4 + x"},
        "2381": {"comment":"Doubled: x10"},
        "2389": {"synopsis":"The sign of the floating-point accumulator (single or double).\nExit: A = 1 and flags P/NZ for a positive number, 0 and Z for zero, $FF and M for negative.","comment":"Exponent zero: the number is zero"},
        "2394": {"comment":"Sign byte"},
        "2397": {"synopsis":"CP $2F changes nothing that is used; its second byte $2F at $095E is CPL, an entry pushed by\nFP_CMP and DBL_CMP_ARG2: the sign of the turned bit 7 of A as 1 or $FF.","comment":"Byte $2F: CPL at $095E"},
        "2399": {"synopsis":"Gives A = $FF (M) if bit 7 of A is set, otherwise 1 (P), both NZ.","comment":"Bit 7 into carry"},
        "2400": {"synopsis":"Gives A = $FF (M) if carry is set, otherwise 1 (P), both NZ.","comment":"$FF or 0"},
        "2401": {"comment":"Minus: done"},
        "2404": {"synopsis":"Converts A, a signed byte, into a single-precision number in the accumulator.","comment":"Exponent for an 8-bit whole number"},
        "2406": {"comment":"Lower mantissa bytes zero"},
        "2409": {"synopsis":"Makes a single-precision number in the accumulator from the signed mantissa A:D:E with\nexponent B (B = $88 for a byte, $90 for 16 bits, $98 for 24).","comment":"Exponent first"},
        "2412": {"comment":"Top mantissa byte"},
        "2417": {"comment":"Sign flag: plus, for now"},
        "2419": {"comment":"Sign bit into carry: negate if set"},
        "2423": {"synopsis":"The ABS function: makes the accumulator positive, whatever its numeric type. A string stops\nwith error code 24 (report TM).","comment":"Plus or zero: unchanged"},
        "2427": {"synopsis":"Negates the accumulator by its type: an integer through INT_NEG_ACC, a string stops with\nerror code 24 (TM), a single or double falls into FP_NEGATE. FIX_FN also ends here.","comment":"Type of the accumulator"},
        "2428": {"comment":"Integer"},
        "2431": {"comment":"String: type mismatch"},
        "2434": {"synopsis":"Turns the sign bit of the floating-point accumulator ($4123, bit 7)."},
        "2442": {"synopsis":"The SGN function: the accumulator becomes the integer 1, 0 or -1 by its sign (any numeric\ntype; a string stops with error code 24, TM)."},
        "2445": {"synopsis":"Stores A, sign-extended, as an integer result (also used at $0197 and $25BF).","comment":"Low byte"},
        "2446": {"comment":"Its sign spread into H"},
        "2449": {"comment":"Integer result"},
        "2452": {"synopsis":"The sign of the accumulator for any numeric type, as FP_SIGN gives it (A = 1, 0 or $FF with\nflags to match). A string stops with error code 24 (report TM).","comment":"Type of the accumulator"},
        "2453": {"comment":"String"},
        "2456": {"comment":"Single or double"},
        "2459": {"comment":"The integer"},
        "2462": {"synopsis":"The sign of the integer in HL as 1, 0 or $FF (also reached from $1CE7).","comment":"Zero?"},
        "2465": {"comment":"Sign from H's top bit"},
        "2468": {"synopsis":"Pushes the accumulator's four bytes under the return address: $4121-$4122 first, so that POP\nBC / POP DE take it back as BCDE. HL and DE are kept.","comment":"Keep HL"},
        "2469": {"comment":"Low word under the return address"},
        "2474": {"comment":"Then the exponent word on top"},
        "2481": {"synopsis":"Copies the single-precision number at HL into the accumulator (through BCDE)."},
        "2484": {"synopsis":"Stores BCDE in the accumulator: E, D at $4121, C, B at $4123. HL is kept."},
        "2485": {"comment":"Low word"},
        "2490": {"comment":"Sign byte and exponent"},
        "2495": {"synopsis":"Loads BCDE from the accumulator."},
        "2498": {"synopsis":"Loads E, D, C, B from four bytes at HL.\nExit: HL past them."},
        "2500": {"synopsis":"Loads D, C, B from three bytes at HL (the call at $28AD); HL past them."},
        "2505": {"synopsis":"INC HL and return (also reached from $1060, $106E and $1A95)."},
        "2507": {"synopsis":"Copies the accumulator's four bytes ($4121-$4124) to HL."},
        "2510": {"comment":"Four bytes"},
        "2514": {"synopsis":"Copies as many bytes as the type byte at $40AF says (2, 3, 4 or 8) from HL to DE."},
        "2515": {"synopsis":"Copies as many bytes as the type byte at $40AF says from DE to HL.","comment":"Length by the accumulator's type"},
        "2519": {"synopsis":"Copies B bytes from DE to HL; DE and HL past them."},
        "2527": {"synopsis":"Unpacks the signs of the accumulator and BCDE for arithmetic: sets the implicit top bit in\n$4123 and in C, and stores the accumulator's sign, turned, in bit 7 of $4125.\nExit: HL = $4125; A bit 7 set (and M) if the two signs agree."},
        "2530": {"comment":"Sign byte into carry"},
        "2532": {"comment":"Top bit set: the implicit 1"},
        "2535": {"comment":"Turned sign into bit 7"},
        "2539": {"comment":"$4125: bit 7 set for plus"},
        "2540": {"comment":"C: the same"},
        "2545": {"comment":"BCDE's sign into bit 7"},
        "2546": {"comment":"Set if the signs agree"},
        "2548": {"synopsis":"Copies the second operand at $4127 into the accumulator, by the accumulator's type."},
        "2551": {"synopsis":"Copies the number at HL into the accumulator, by the accumulator's type (COPY_HL_TO_DE with\nDE = ACC_ADDR's address).","comment":"Where to copy, after ACC_ADDR"},
        "2556": {"synopsis":"Copies the accumulator to the second operand at $4127, by the accumulator's type."},
        "2562": {"synopsis":"Pushes the copy routine in DE as the return address, then finds the accumulator's address."},
        "2563": {"synopsis":"Gives DE = the first byte of the accumulator for its type: $411D for a double, $4121\notherwise.","comment":"Integer, string, single: from $4121"},
        "2566": {"comment":"Not a double?"},
        "2568": {"comment":"Double: four more low bytes"},
        "2572": {"synopsis":"Single-precision comparison of the accumulator with BCDE; the single-precision table at\nSINGLE_OP_TABLE reaches it as its fifth entry.\nExit: A = 1 if the accumulator is greater, 0 if equal, $FF if less; flags to match.","comment":"BCDE zero: the accumulator's sign"},
        "2577": {"comment":"The result goes through $095E"},
        "2581": {"comment":"Accumulator zero: from BCDE's sign"},
        "2586": {"comment":"Signs differ?"},
        "2590": {"comment":"Yes: from BCDE's sign"},
        "2592": {"comment":"Compare the magnitudes"},
        "2595": {"synopsis":"Turns the magnitude comparison into the result: carry (BCDE smaller) with BCDE's sign."},
        "2598": {"synopsis":"Compares B, C, D, E with the accumulator's exponent and mantissa bytes, top first.\nExit: NZ with carry set if BCDE is the smaller; equal, it drops its return and the word\nunder it and returns with A = 0.","comment":"Exponents"},
        "2603": {"comment":"Top mantissa bytes"},
        "2607": {"comment":"Middle"},
        "2611": {"comment":"Low"},
        "2614": {"comment":"Equal: drop $0A20's return and $095E"},
        "2617": {"synopsis":"Integer comparison of HL with DE; the integer table at INT_OP_TABLE reaches it as its fifth\nentry.\nExit: A = 1 if HL is greater, 0 if equal, $FF if less.","comment":"Signs differ?"},
        "2620": {"comment":"Yes: from HL's sign"},
        "2623": {"comment":"High bytes"},
        "2627": {"comment":"Low bytes"},
        "2633": {"synopsis":"Copies the number at DE to the second operand at $4127, then compares (DBL_CMP_ARG2)."},
        "2639": {"synopsis":"Double-precision comparison of the accumulator ($411D-$4124) with the second operand\n($4127-$412E).\nExit: A = 1 if the accumulator is greater, 0 if equal, $FF if less.","comment":"The operand's exponent"},
        "2643": {"comment":"Operand zero: the accumulator's sign"},
        "2647": {"comment":"The result goes through $095E"},
        "2654": {"comment":"The operand's sign byte"},
        "2657": {"comment":"Accumulator zero: from the operand's sign"},
        "2661": {"comment":"Signs differ?"},
        "2664": {"comment":"Back to the exponents"},
        "2666": {"comment":"Eight bytes, top first"},
        "2668": {"synopsis":"Compares one byte pair; the first that differs decides.","comment":"Operand byte - accumulator byte"},
        "2670": {"comment":"Differ: decide"},
        "2678": {"comment":"Equal: drop $095E, A = 0"},
        "2680": {"synopsis":"Double-precision comparison for the double table at DOUBLE_OP_TABLE (fifth entry):\nDBL_CMP_ARG2 with the result turned round by a second pass through $095E.\nExit: A = 1 if the second operand is greater, 0 if equal, $FF if less."},
        "2687": {"synopsis":"The CINT function (also the type-conversion table's entry at $18A5): makes the accumulator\nan integer at $4121. A double goes through single precision first; the whole part is taken\ndownwards (FP_WHOLE_PART). Stops with error code 10 (report OV) outside -32768 to 32767,\nwith error code 24 (TM) for a string.","comment":"Type of the accumulator"},
        "2688": {"comment":"The integer, if it is one"},
        "2691": {"comment":"Already an integer"},
        "2692": {"comment":"String: type mismatch"},
        "2695": {"comment":"Double: to single first"},
        "2698": {"comment":"Out of range ends at the overflow error"},
        "2702": {"synopsis":"Converts the single-precision accumulator to an integer if it is below 2^15 in size, and\nreturns to the caller's caller (dropping the word under the return); 2^15 or more returns\nnormally, except for exactly -32768. INT_FN calls it to get an integer result when one fits.","comment":"The exponent"},
        "2705": {"comment":"2^15 or more?"},
        "2709": {"comment":"C:D:E = the whole part"},
        "2712": {"comment":"HL = its lower two bytes"},
        "2713": {"synopsis":"Drops a word, then stores HL as the integer result.","comment":"Drop the word under the return"},
        "2714": {"synopsis":"Stores HL as an integer result: at $4121, with type 2 at $40AF."},
        "2717": {"comment":"Type 2: integer"},
        "2719": {"synopsis":"Stores A as the accumulator's type at $40AF."},
        "2723": {"synopsis":"Allows exactly -32768 ($8000) among the numbers of 2^15 or more; any other returns NZ.","comment":"BCDE = -32768"},
        "2729": {"comment":"The accumulator equal to it?"},
        "2732": {"comment":"No: return (to the overflow error from CINT_FN)"},
        "2733": {"comment":"HL = $8000"},
        "2737": {"synopsis":"The CSNG function (also the conversion table's entry at $18A9): makes the accumulator single\nprecision. A string stops with error code 24 (report TM)."},
        "2738": {"comment":"Already single"},
        "2739": {"comment":"Integer"},
        "2742": {"comment":"String"},
        "2745": {"synopsis":"Converts the double-precision accumulator to single, rounded by its fifth mantissa byte\n($4120).","comment":"BCDE = the top four bytes"},
        "2748": {"comment":"Type 4"},
        "2751": {"comment":"Zero stays zero"},
        "2754": {"comment":"Implicit top bit for rounding"},
        "2757": {"comment":"Next byte down as the rounding byte"},
        "2764": {"synopsis":"Converts the integer accumulator to single precision."},
        "2767": {"synopsis":"Sets type 4 and converts HL, a signed 16-bit number, into the accumulator as single\nprecision."},
        "2770": {"comment":"Mantissa H:L:0"},
        "2774": {"comment":"Exponent for 16 bits"},
        "2779": {"synopsis":"The CDBL function (also the conversion table's entry at TYPE_CONV_TABLE): makes the\naccumulator double precision, an integer by way of single. A string stops with error code 24\n(TM)."},
        "2780": {"comment":"Already double"},
        "2781": {"comment":"String"},
        "2784": {"comment":"Integer: to single first"},
        "2787": {"synopsis":"Clears the four low bytes of the double accumulator ($411D-$4120) and sets type 8."},
        "2796": {"synopsis":"Sets type 8, double."},
        "2798": {"synopsis":"LD BC,$043E only skips the next two bytes: $0AEF is LD A,4, the entry that sets type 4\n(single), called by DBL_TO_SNG, HL_TO_SNG and others; both then go on at SET_TYPE.","comment":"Bytes 3E 04: LD A,4 at $0AEF"},
        "2804": {"synopsis":"Returns if the accumulator holds a string (the conversion table's entry at $18A7); else\nfalls into TYPE_MISMATCH."},
        "2806": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 24 (report TM).","comment":"Error code 24: TM"},
        "2811": {"synopsis":"Takes the whole part of the single-precision accumulator, rounded downwards (a negative\nnumber's mantissa is lowered by one before the shift and raised after).\nEntry: A = the accumulator's exponent.\nExit: C:D:E = the whole part as a 24-bit two's complement number; BCDE = 0 if A = 0. HL is\nkept.","comment":"Zero exponent: all zero"},
        "2818": {"comment":"BCDE = the accumulator"},
        "2821": {"comment":"Implicit top bit"},
        "2824": {"comment":"H bit 7 = the sign"},
        "2826": {"comment":"Negative: mantissa minus one"},
        "2829": {"comment":"Shift by 24 - the exponent's bits"},
        "2835": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "2837": {"comment":"Then plus one, after the shift"},
        "2842": {"comment":"And two's complement"},
        "2847": {"synopsis":"Lowers D:E by one, and B:C too on a borrow."},
        "2852": {"synopsis":"DEC BC and return (also reached from $2BA2)."},
        "2854": {"synopsis":"The FIX function: the whole part towards zero. A positive number goes to INT_FN; a negative\none is negated, put through INT_FN and negated back (NEGATE_ANY)."},
        "2855": {"comment":"Integer: unchanged"},
        "2856": {"comment":"Plus or zero: as INT"},
        "2862": {"comment":"Make it positive"},
        "2871": {"synopsis":"The INT function: the largest whole number not above the accumulator. The result is an\ninteger when it fits (FP_TO_INT), otherwise single or double. A string stops with error code\n24 (report TM)."},
        "2872": {"comment":"Integer: unchanged"},
        "2873": {"comment":"Double"},
        "2875": {"comment":"String"},
        "2877": {"comment":"Fits an integer: returns from there"},
        "2880": {"synopsis":"Rounds the single-precision accumulator down to a whole number, kept as single.\nExit: A = the whole part's low byte. Also called at $1415, $144D, $14ED and $155B."},
        "2884": {"comment":"2^23 or more: already whole"},
        "2886": {"comment":"Low byte, for the caller"},
        "2891": {"comment":"C:D:E = the whole part"},
        "2894": {"comment":"Exponent for 24 bits"},
        "2896": {"comment":"Keep the low byte"},
        "2899": {"comment":"Sign into carry, then normalise"},
        "2905": {"synopsis":"INT for a double: below 2^15 through CINT_FN, exactly -32768 as an integer, 2^55 or more\nunchanged, otherwise DBL_FLOOR."},
        "2908": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "2909": {"comment":"Below 2^15: as an integer"},
        "2914": {"comment":"2^16 or more"},
        "2916": {"comment":"2^15 to 2^16: is it -32768?"},
        "2918": {"comment":"Top byte less the sign bit"},
        "2921": {"comment":"Six more bytes"},
        "2923": {"synopsis":"ORs the double's lower mantissa bytes together."},
        "2928": {"comment":"Any bit set?"},
        "2929": {"comment":"None: the integer -32768"},
        "2935": {"comment":"Back the exponent"},
        "2936": {"synopsis":"Returns for 2^55 or more (already whole), else goes on into DBL_FLOOR."},
        "2939": {"synopsis":"Rounds the double accumulator down to a whole number: a negative mantissa is lowered by one,\nthe mantissa is shifted by SHIFT8_RIGHT with A = $B8 - the exponent, and for a negative\nnumber DBL_INC_MANT runs; the exponent is set to $B8 and the byte at $411C cleared. With\ncarry set on entry (as from DBL_INT_RANGE's CP) it goes on at DBL_NORMALISE, else it\nreturns.","comment":"Keep the exponent and carry"},
        "2943": {"comment":"Implicit top bit"},
        "2946": {"comment":"A bit 7 = the sign"},
        "2948": {"comment":"Exponent for 56 bits"},
        "2950": {"comment":"Keep the sign"},
        "2951": {"comment":"Negative: lower by one"},
        "2957": {"comment":"Bits to shift off"},
        "2964": {"comment":"Negative: correct after the shift"},
        "2967": {"comment":"Clear the byte below the mantissa"},
        "2976": {"synopsis":"Lowers the double's mantissa by one from $411D up."},
        "2979": {"synopsis":"One byte; on into the next while the byte was zero (a borrow)."},
        "2986": {"synopsis":"Multiplies DE by BC, both unsigned 16-bit, into DE (the array code at $2768). Stops with\nerror code 16 (report BS) if the product needs more than 16 bits. HL is kept."},
        "2987": {"comment":"Product starts at zero"},
        "2990": {"comment":"BC zero: product zero"},
        "2994": {"comment":"16 bits"},
        "2996": {"synopsis":"Doubles the product, then adds BC if DE's next top bit is set.","comment":"Product doubled"},
        "2997": {"comment":"Too large: error code 16 (BS)"},
        "3000": {"comment":"Next bit of DE into carry"},
        "3005": {"comment":"Add BC"},
        "3009": {"synopsis":"One bit fewer."},
        "3012": {"synopsis":"DE = the product."},
        "3015": {"synopsis":"Integer subtraction, DE minus HL; the integer table at INT_OP_TABLE reaches it as its second\nentry. HL is negated (keeping a 17th bit in B), then INT_ADD_EXT adds.","comment":"B = HL's sign spread"},
        "3019": {"comment":"HL = -HL"},
        "3022": {"comment":"A = the 17th bit of -HL, into B"},
        "3026": {"synopsis":"Integer addition, DE plus HL; the integer table at INT_OP_TABLE reaches it as its first\nentry.\nExit: an integer result (INT_RESULT); on overflow both operands are made single precision\nand added by FP_ADD.","comment":"B = HL's sign spread"},
        "3029": {"synopsis":"Adds DE to HL with B as HL's 17th bit; a sum that fits 16 bits is the result."},
        "3030": {"comment":"Keep the second operand"},
        "3031": {"comment":"A = DE's sign spread"},
        "3034": {"comment":"17-bit sum, top in A"},
        "3036": {"comment":"Top bit and bit 15 agree?"},
        "3038": {"comment":"Yes: integer result"},
        "3041": {"comment":"Overflow: keep the 17th bit"},
        "3043": {"comment":"DE as single"},
        "3048": {"comment":"Pushed"},
        "3052": {"comment":"The second operand as single"},
        "3058": {"synopsis":"Integer multiplication, DE times HL; the integer table at INT_OP_TABLE reaches it as its\nthird entry. Zero gives zero; the magnitudes are multiplied (INT_ABS_PAIR takes the signs\noff) and the sign put back; a product past 16 bits is redone in single precision through\nFP_MUL. Continues to $0C44.","comment":"HL zero: zero result"},
        "3063": {"comment":"Keep both operands"},
        "3065": {"comment":"Signs off, product's sign in B"},
        "3069": {"comment":"BC = one magnitude"},
        "3074": {"comment":"16 multiplier bits"},
        "3076": {"synopsis":"The loop of the integer multiply at INT_MUL: doubles the product in HL, shifts the next\nmultiplier bit out of the top of DE and adds the multiplicand (BC) when it is set. A carry\nout of HL goes to the hidden entry $0C26 (inside the LD BC at $0C25), which redoes the\nmultiply in single precision.","comment":"Product doubled: past 16 bits?"},
        "3079": {"comment":"Next multiplier bit out of DE's top"},
        "3082": {"comment":"Bit clear: no add"},
        "3084": {"comment":"Add the multiplicand: past 16 bits?"},
        "3088": {"synopsis":"The integer multiply's loop count: 16 multiplier bits.","comment":"Bits left?"},
        "3091": {"comment":"B = the product's sign (from INT_ABS_PAIR)"},
        "3092": {"comment":"DE = the original second operand"},
        "3093": {"comment":"Product 32768 or more?"},
        "3098": {"comment":"Drop the original first operand"},
        "3099": {"comment":"Signed integer result"},
        "3103": {"synopsis":"The integer multiply's product is 32768 or more: exactly 32768 goes to IMUL_32768; anything\nlarger converts both original operands to single precision and multiplies them there\n(FP_MUL). The LD BC,$E1C1 at $0C25 hides the overflow entry $0C26 (POP BC, POP HL), which\nthe loop's carries reach with the sign and the original operands still on the stack.","comment":"Exactly $8000?"},
        "3108": {"comment":"HL = the original second operand"},
        "3109": {"comment":"Skips POP BC/POP HL, the overflow entry at $0C26"},
        "3112": {"comment":"Second operand as a single"},
        "3115": {"comment":"The original first operand"},
        "3116": {"comment":"The single onto the stack"},
        "3119": {"comment":"First operand as a single"},
        "3122": {"comment":"The stacked single into BCDE"},
        "3124": {"comment":"Multiply in single precision"},
        "3127": {"synopsis":"The integer multiply's product is exactly 32768: with a negative sign that is -32768, which\nfits an integer; with a positive one the result is +32768 as a single.","comment":"Product negative?"},
        "3129": {"comment":"Drop the original first operand"},
        "3130": {"comment":"Yes: -32768 is a valid integer"},
        "3134": {"comment":"$8000 as a single: -32768"},
        "3138": {"comment":"Sign flipped: +32768"},
        "3141": {"synopsis":"Makes the two integers in HL and DE positive for a multiply, and gives the product's sign.\nEntry: HL, DE = the operands.\nExit: B bit 7 = set when the signs differ; HL = |DE|, DE = |HL| (swapped); the accumulator\n($4121) is left holding |DE| as an integer, type 2. -32768 stays $8000, which the unsigned\nmultiply takes as 32768.","comment":"Bit 7 set when the signs differ"},
        "3144": {"comment":"One operand made positive"},
        "3147": {"comment":"Then the other"},
        "3148": {"synopsis":"Makes HL positive (negates it when bit 7 of H is set) and stores it as the integer result\n(INT_RESULT: $4121, type 2).","comment":"Negative?"},
        "3149": {"synopsis":"Stores HL as the integer result, negated first when bit 7 of A is set.\nEntry: A = the sign (bit 7)."},
        "3150": {"comment":"No: store it as it is"},
        "3153": {"synopsis":"Negates HL (HL = 0 - HL) and stores it as the integer result (INT_RESULT: $4121, type 2).\nExit: A = H; C = 0.","comment":"HL = 0 - HL"},
        "3160": {"comment":"Store as the integer result"},
        "3163": {"synopsis":"Negates the integer in the accumulator ($4121), for the negate at NEGATE_ANY when the type\nis integer. -32768 has no positive integer: it falls into UINT_TO_SNG and becomes +32768 as\na single.","comment":"The integer in the accumulator"},
        "3166": {"comment":"Negated"},
        "3169": {"comment":"Was it -32768 ($8000 again)?"},
        "3173": {"comment":"No: done"},
        "3174": {"synopsis":"Converts HL, taken as an unsigned 16-bit number (0-65535), to a single in the accumulator,\ntype 4. Used from $24E2 and $27F2.","comment":"DE = the value"},
        "3175": {"comment":"Result type single"},
        "3178": {"comment":"Top byte 0: a positive number"},
        "3179": {"synopsis":"Converts the 24-bit signed number A:D:E (A the top byte) to a single in the accumulator:\nexponent $98 (2^24), normalised by MANT_TO_FP/FP_NORM_SIGNED. Used at $0BEC.","comment":"Exponent for a 24-bit integer"},
        "3181": {"comment":"Build and normalise the single"},
        "3184": {"synopsis":"Double-precision subtract (from the operator table at $18AD): accumulator = accumulator -\nsecond operand. Flips the sign bit of the second operand ($412D) and falls into DBL_ADD.","comment":"Second operand's sign byte"},
        "3188": {"comment":"Sign flipped"},
        "3191": {"synopsis":"Double-precision add (from the operator table at DOUBLE_OP_TABLE): accumulator ($411D-$4124)\n+= second operand ($4127-$412E).\n1. A zero second operand (exponent $412E = 0) changes nothing; a zero accumulator takes the\n   second operand (ACC_FROM_ARG2).\n2. The number with the larger exponent goes into the accumulator (the two 8-byte numbers are\n   swapped), and a difference of 57 bits or more leaves it as it is.\n3. Both leading 1 bits are put back (FP_UNPACK_SIGNS, which leaves NOT the accumulator's\n   sign in $4125), the second operand is shifted right by the difference (its guard byte\n   $4126 cleared first, then copied to the accumulator's guard byte $411C).\n4. Same signs: the mantissas are added and a carry out shifts the sum right one bit and\n   raises the exponent. Different signs: subtracted, and a borrow negates the result and\n   flips the sign.\n5. Falls into DBL_NORMALISE / DBL_ROUND.\nExit: the sum in the accumulator. Stops with error code 10 (report OV) when the exponent\npasses $FF.","comment":"Second operand's exponent"},
        "3195": {"comment":"Zero: nothing to add"},
        "3197": {"comment":"B = its exponent"},
        "3199": {"comment":"C = its top byte, with the sign"},
        "3200": {"comment":"The accumulator's exponent"},
        "3204": {"comment":"Zero: the result is the second operand"},
        "3208": {"comment":"Accumulator the larger (or equal)?"},
        "3211": {"comment":"No: the difference made positive"},
        "3214": {"comment":"8 bytes to swap"},
        "3216": {"comment":"HL = $412E, the second exponent"},
        "3218": {"synopsis":"DBL_ADD's swap: exchanges the accumulator ($411D-$4124) and the second operand ($4127-$412E)\nbyte by byte, from the exponents down.","comment":"Exchange a pair of bytes"},
        "3223": {"comment":"Down to the next pair"},
        "3225": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3228": {"comment":"B, C = the new second operand's exponent and top"},
        "3232": {"comment":"A = the exponent difference"},
        "3233": {"synopsis":"DBL_ADD with the accumulator the larger: aligns the second operand's mantissa to its\nexponent, then adds or subtracts by the signs.","comment":"57 bits or more apart?"},
        "3235": {"comment":"Yes: the smaller one does not count"},
        "3237": {"comment":"Leading 1s back; A bit 7 set when signs equal"},
        "3240": {"comment":"The second operand's guard byte $4126"},
        "3243": {"comment":"B = the sign test"},
        "3245": {"comment":"Align the second operand's mantissa"},
        "3251": {"comment":"Its guard byte to the accumulator's"},
        "3257": {"comment":"Same signs?"},
        "3259": {"comment":"No: subtract"},
        "3262": {"comment":"Add the mantissas: carry out of the top?"},
        "3268": {"comment":"HL = $4124, the exponent"},
        "3269": {"comment":"One bit more: past $FF?"},
        "3273": {"comment":"Sum shifted right one bit"},
        "3279": {"synopsis":"DBL_ADD with different signs: subtracts the second operand's mantissa; a borrow means the\nsecond was the larger, so the result is negated and its sign flipped (DBL_NEG_MANT). Falls\ninto DBL_NORMALISE.","comment":"Subtract the mantissas"},
        "3282": {"comment":"The sign holder"},
        "3285": {"comment":"Borrow: negate the result and flip its sign"},
        "3288": {"synopsis":"Normalises the double in the accumulator: shifts the 8-byte mantissa ($411C-$4123, guard\nbyte included) left until bit 7 of $4123 is set, first by whole bytes, then by bits,\nlowering the exponent by the count; then rounds (DBL_ROUND). A mantissa that is still zero\nafter 64 bits, or an exponent that falls to zero or below, gives zero (FP_ZERO). Also\nreached from the INT/FIX code at $0B9D and from DBL_MUL.","comment":"B counts the shift"},
        "3289": {"synopsis":"DBL_NORMALISE's byte step: while the top byte $4123 is zero, moves the mantissa up a byte\nand counts 8 more in B."},
        "3290": {"comment":"Top byte zero?"},
        "3294": {"comment":"No: shift by bits"},
        "3296": {"comment":"Move the mantissa up a byte"},
        "3301": {"synopsis":"Moves the 8 bytes $411C-$4123 up one byte, a zero coming in at $411C.","comment":"Byte up one place"},
        "3305": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3308": {"comment":"8 more bits of shift"},
        "3311": {"comment":"64 bits: nothing left?"},
        "3315": {"comment":"The result is zero"},
        "3318": {"synopsis":"DBL_NORMALISE's bit step: shifts the mantissa left one bit and counts it in B.","comment":"One more bit of shift"},
        "3319": {"comment":"Mantissa left one bit"},
        "3326": {"synopsis":"DBL_NORMALISE: shifts again while bit 7 of the top byte is clear.","comment":"Leading bit still clear?"},
        "3329": {"comment":"No shift at all?"},
        "3333": {"comment":"Lower the exponent by the shift"},
        "3338": {"comment":"Below 1: the result is zero"},
        "3341": {"comment":"Exactly zero exponent: zero"},
        "3342": {"synopsis":"Rounds the double in the accumulator and puts its sign in: a guard byte ($411C) with bit 7\nset adds 1 to the 7-byte mantissa (DBL_INC_MANT), then bit 7 of $4123 (the leading 1) is\nreplaced by the sign, which is NOT bit 7 of $4125.","comment":"The guard byte"},
        "3345": {"synopsis":"DBL_ROUND with the rounding bit in bit 7 of A instead of the guard byte, for DBL_DIV.","comment":"Bit 7 set: round up"},
        "3349": {"comment":"The sign holder ($4125, NOT the sign)"},
        "3353": {"comment":"Its bit 7"},
        "3355": {"comment":"HL = $4123, the top byte"},
        "3357": {"comment":"Leading 1 replaced by the sign"},
        "3360": {"synopsis":"Adds 1 to the double's 7-byte mantissa ($411D-$4123). A carry out of the top raises the\nexponent and sets the mantissa to $80 00..00; stops with error code 10 (report OV) when the\nexponent passes $FF. Also used by the INT/FIX code at $0B94.","comment":"The mantissa's lowest byte"},
        "3363": {"comment":"7 bytes"},
        "3365": {"synopsis":"DBL_INC_MANT's loop: done at the first byte that does not wrap to zero.","comment":"No wrap: done"},
        "3367": {"comment":"Carry into the next byte"},
        "3369": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3371": {"comment":"Carry out of the top: exponent + 1"},
        "3372": {"comment":"Past $FF?"},
        "3375": {"comment":"Mantissa now 1000...: top byte $80"},
        "3379": {"synopsis":"Adds the second operand's 7-byte mantissa ($4127-$412D) to the accumulator's ($411D-$4123).\nExit: carry = a carry out of the top; DE = $4124, HL = $412E.","comment":"The second operand's mantissa"},
        "3382": {"synopsis":"Adds the 7 bytes at HL (low byte first) to the accumulator's mantissa ($411D-$4123). Used by\nthe number-to-digits code at $12D4.","comment":"The accumulator's mantissa"},
        "3385": {"synopsis":"Adds the 7 bytes at HL to the 7 bytes at DE (low bytes first).\nExit: carry = a carry out of the top; DE and HL just past them.","comment":"7 bytes"},
        "3387": {"comment":"Carry clear"},
        "3388": {"synopsis":"MANT7_ADD's loop: one byte, with the carry.","comment":"Add a byte with carry"},
        "3393": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3397": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the second operand's 7-byte mantissa ($4127-$412D) from the accumulator's\n($411D-$4123).\nExit: carry = a borrow out of the top.","comment":"The second operand's mantissa"},
        "3400": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the 7 bytes at HL from the accumulator's mantissa ($411D-$4123). Used by the\nnumber-to-digits code at $12CE.","comment":"The accumulator's mantissa"},
        "3403": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the 7 bytes at HL from the 7 bytes at DE (low bytes first).\nExit: carry = a borrow out of the top; DE and HL just past them.","comment":"7 bytes"},
        "3405": {"comment":"Borrow clear"},
        "3406": {"synopsis":"MANT7_SUB's loop: one byte, with the borrow.","comment":"Subtract a byte with borrow"},
        "3411": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3415": {"synopsis":"Negates the double's result: complements the sign holder at HL ($4125) and makes the 8 bytes\n$411C-$4123 their two's complement (0 minus each, with borrow).","comment":"Sign holder complemented"},
        "3418": {"comment":"The 8 bytes from the guard byte"},
        "3423": {"comment":"Borrow clear, C = 0"},
        "3425": {"synopsis":"DBL_NEG_MANT's loop: one byte of 0 - (HL) with the borrow.","comment":"0 - byte - borrow"},
        "3429": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3433": {"synopsis":"Shifts an 8-byte number right by A bits.\nEntry: HL = its top byte, which first gets C (the top byte with its leading 1 put back); A =\nthe count. Whole bytes go first (8 bits at a time, a zero coming in at the top), then the\nbits left. Used for DBL_ADD's alignment and by the INT/FIX code at $0B90.","comment":"Top byte with its leading 1"},
        "3435": {"synopsis":"SHIFT8_RIGHT: 8 bits or more left? Then one whole byte.","comment":"8 bits or more left?"},
        "3437": {"comment":"No: shift by bits"},
        "3440": {"synopsis":"Moves the 8 bytes ending at HL down one byte, a zero coming in at the top, the lowest byte\nlost. DBL_MUL uses it for a multiplier byte of zero.","comment":"A whole byte down"},
        "3441": {"comment":"D = 8 bytes, E = 0 coming in"},
        "3444": {"synopsis":"The byte move's loop: each byte takes the one above it.","comment":"Byte takes the one above it"},
        "3447": {"comment":"Down to the next"},
        "3448": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3451": {"comment":"Again for the next 8 bits"},
        "3453": {"synopsis":"SHIFT8_RIGHT's bit step: D = the bits left + 1.","comment":"D = the bits left + 1"},
        "3456": {"synopsis":"SHIFT8_RIGHT: one more bit? Returns when D runs out.","comment":"Carry clear for each bit"},
        "3458": {"comment":"Bits left?"},
        "3460": {"synopsis":"Shifts the 8 bytes ending at HL right one bit (a zero into the top), D times in all."},
        "3461": {"comment":"8 bytes"},
        "3463": {"synopsis":"The one-bit shift's loop: RRA down the 8 bytes.","comment":"Byte right one bit, through the carry"},
        "3466": {"comment":"Down to the next"},
        "3467": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3470": {"comment":"Next bit"},
        "3472": {"synopsis":"Shifts the accumulator's mantissa with its guard byte ($411C-$4123) right one bit.","comment":"The top of the accumulator's mantissa"},
        "3475": {"comment":"One bit"},
        "3479": {"synopsis":"Shifts the 8 bytes from HL up (low byte first) left one bit, the carry coming in at the\nbottom.\nExit: carry = the bit out of the top.","comment":"8 bytes"},
        "3481": {"synopsis":"Shifts C bytes from HL up left one bit, through the carry.\nEntry: carry = the bit coming in at the bottom.","comment":"Byte left one bit, through the carry"},
        "3484": {"comment":"Up to the next"},
        "3485": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3489": {"synopsis":"Double-precision multiply (from the operator table at $18AF): accumulator = accumulator x\nsecond operand. Also used by the scaling code at $1219.\n1. A zero accumulator stays zero; $090A adds the exponents (errors on overflow, zero on\n   underflow).\n2. DBL_TAKE_MANT moves the accumulator's mantissa to $414A-$4150 and clears the accumulator.\n3. For each of those 7 bytes, low byte first, each bit low first: a set bit adds the second\n   operand's mantissa (DBL_MANT_ADD); then the partial product is shifted right one bit\n   (DBL_SHR1). A zero byte shifts it a whole byte (SHIFT8_BYTE).\n4. Ends through DBL_NORMALISE and DBL_ROUND.","comment":"Accumulator zero?"},
        "3492": {"comment":"Yes: the product is zero"},
        "3493": {"comment":"Add the exponents"},
        "3496": {"comment":"Multiplicand to $414A-$4150, accumulator cleared"},
        "3499": {"comment":"Guard byte cleared"},
        "3500": {"comment":"DE = $414A, the multiplier's low byte"},
        "3501": {"comment":"7 multiplier bytes"},
        "3503": {"synopsis":"DBL_MUL: the next multiplier byte; a zero one takes the short way (DBL_MUL_ZERO).","comment":"Next multiplier byte"},
        "3505": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "3507": {"comment":"Yes: shift a whole byte"},
        "3509": {"comment":"8 bits"},
        "3511": {"synopsis":"DBL_MUL: one multiplier bit, low first: add the second operand when set, then shift right."},
        "3512": {"comment":"Next bit, low first: set?"},
        "3514": {"comment":"Yes: add the second operand"},
        "3517": {"comment":"Partial product right one bit"},
        "3522": {"comment":"Bits left?"},
        "3525": {"synopsis":"DBL_MUL: the multiplier bytes left? Then normalise."},
        "3526": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3529": {"comment":"Normalise and round"},
        "3532": {"synopsis":"DBL_MUL's zero multiplier byte: the partial product moves down one byte (SHIFT8_BYTE).","comment":"Partial product down one byte"},
        "3540": {"synopsis":"The number 10 as a double: 00 00 00 00 00 00 20 84 (exponent $84, top byte $20, which with\nits leading 1 put back is $A0: 0.625 x 2^4). Its last four bytes, $0DD8, are 10 as a single,\nwhich the single divide-by-10 at FP_DIV10 loads."},
        "3548": {"synopsis":"Divides the double in the accumulator by 10: copies DBL_TEN to the second operand (8 bytes,\nthe length taken from the type byte $40AF) and falls into DBL_DIV.","comment":"Copy DBL_TEN to the second operand"},
        "3557": {"synopsis":"Double-precision divide (from the operator table at $18B1): accumulator = accumulator /\nsecond operand. Stops with error code 20 (report /0) for a zero divisor.\n1. DBL_EXP_DIV subtracts the exponents; +2 more for the quotient's place.\n2. DBL_TAKE_MANT moves the dividend's mantissa to $414A-$4150 (with $4151 = 0 above it),\n   clearing the accumulator for the quotient.\n3. Each step subtracts the divisor's mantissa from the remainder: no borrow keeps it and\n   gives a quotient bit of 1, a borrow adds it back and gives 0. The bit is shifted into the\n   accumulator from the bottom, the remainder shifted left.\n4. Until a first 1 has been found (B = 0), each step lowers the exponent instead; an\n   exponent run down to zero stops with error code 10 (report OV).\n5. When the quotient's top bit is set, the next quotient bit rounds it (DBL_ROUND_A).","comment":"Divisor's exponent"},
        "3561": {"comment":"Zero divisor: report /0"},
        "3564": {"comment":"Subtract the exponents"},
        "3567": {"comment":"Exponent + 2 for the quotient's place"},
        "3569": {"comment":"Dividend to $414A-$4150, accumulator cleared"},
        "3572": {"comment":"$4151, above the remainder, cleared"},
        "3576": {"comment":"B = 0: no 1 in the quotient yet"},
        "3577": {"synopsis":"DBL_DIV's step: try to subtract the divisor from the remainder. The JP C,$0412 at $0E11 is\nnever taken (XOR A clears the carry): it hides the entry DBL_DIV_KEEP (LD (DE),A; INC B),\nwhich keeps the remainder's top byte and counts the quotient as started.","comment":"Remainder minus the divisor"},
        "3586": {"comment":"Borrow from the top byte"},
        "3588": {"comment":"Carry set: no borrow, quotient bit 1"},
        "3591": {"comment":"Borrow: add the divisor back"},
        "3600": {"comment":"Quotient bit 0 (carry clear)"},
        "3601": {"synopsis":"$DA, the first byte of a JP C,$0412 that is never taken: run from $0E10, whose XOR A clears\nthe carry, the three bytes $DA $12 $04 are that jump and the step goes on at $0E14. Reached\nby the JR C at $0E05, DBL_DIV_KEEP is code: DBL_DIV_KEEP. Listed as data so that both\nreadings show."},
        "3602": {"synopsis":"DBL_DIV's step when the divisor went into the remainder (from $0E05): keeps the new top byte\nof the remainder and counts the quotient as started, then joins the step at $0E14.","comment":"Keep the remainder's new top byte"},
        "3603": {"comment":"The quotient has started"},
        "3604": {"comment":"Quotient's top byte"},
        "3609": {"comment":"Bit into A's top; S from the top byte"},
        "3610": {"comment":"Quotient full: round with that bit"},
        "3613": {"comment":"Carry = the quotient bit again"},
        "3614": {"comment":"Quotient left one bit, the bit in"},
        "3622": {"comment":"Remainder left one bit"},
        "3628": {"comment":"Quotient started?"},
        "3630": {"comment":"Yes: next step"},
        "3632": {"comment":"No: exponent - 1 instead"},
        "3636": {"comment":"Not zero: next step"},
        "3638": {"comment":"Exponent run down: report OV"},
        "3641": {"synopsis":"Moves the accumulator's 7-byte mantissa ($411D-$4123) to $414A-$4150, clearing it, for\nDBL_MUL and DBL_DIV.\nEntry: C = the second operand's top byte with its leading 1 (put back into $412D); HL =\n$4124.\nExit: HL = $411C; DE = $4149; C = 0.","comment":"Second operand's top byte with its leading 1"},
        "3645": {"comment":"HL = $4123"},
        "3646": {"comment":"To $4150 down"},
        "3649": {"comment":"7 bytes, C = 0 to clear them"},
        "3652": {"synopsis":"DBL_TAKE_MANT's loop: one byte moved, its place cleared.","comment":"Byte moved"},
        "3654": {"comment":"Its place cleared"},
        "3655": {"comment":"Down to the next"},
        "3657": {"comment":"Bytes left?"},
        "3661": {"synopsis":"Multiplies the double in the accumulator by 10: copies it to the second operand\n(ACC_TO_ARG2), multiplies the accumulator by 4 (exponent + 2), adds the copy (x 5) and\ndoubles (exponent + 1). A zero stays zero. Stops with error code 10 (report OV) when the\nexponent passes $FF.","comment":"Copy to the second operand"},
        "3664": {"comment":"HL = past the accumulator's copy"},
        "3665": {"comment":"HL = $4124, the exponent"},
        "3667": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "3669": {"comment":"x 4: past $FF?"},
        "3676": {"comment":"+ the copy: x 5"},
        "3680": {"comment":"x 10: past $FF?"},
        "3685": {"synopsis":"Reads a number from the text at HL into the accumulator, kept in double precision from the\nstart (VAL at $2AD8, and the code at $2257). The OR $AF at $0E6B hides the general entry\n$0E6C (XOR A), where the number starts as the integer 0 (from $2254, $24A5 and $24B4, the\nexpression evaluator).\n1. An optional '-' or '+' (the '-' remembered on the stack).\n2. Digits (NUM_IN_DIGIT): kept as an integer while it fits (below 32768), then as a single\n   while below 10 million, then as a double. B counts the digits after the point.\n3. '.' (NUM_IN_POINT) makes the number single; 'E' or 'D' (NUM_IN_EXP) makes it single or\n   double and reads a signed exponent into E; the suffixes '%' (integer, else error code 2,\n   report SN), '!' (single) and '#' (double) fix the type and end the number.\n4. The number is scaled by 10 to the power E - B (NUM_IN_SCALE), negated for a '-', and a\n   single that came out as exactly -32768 becomes the integer -32768.\nExit: the value in the accumulator, its type in $40AF; HL = the character after the number.","comment":"The accumulator is zero"},
        "3688": {"comment":"Type double"},
        "3691": {"comment":"NZ; hides XOR A, the entry $0E6C"},
        "3693": {"comment":"HL free; DE = the text"},
        "3694": {"comment":"B = 0 digits after the point, C = $FF: no point"},
        "3697": {"comment":"HL = 0"},
        "3699": {"comment":"From $0E6C: the integer 0"},
        "3702": {"comment":"HL = the text"},
        "3703": {"comment":"A minus sign?"},
        "3706": {"comment":"Remembered (Z) on the stack"},
        "3710": {"comment":"Or a plus sign?"},
        "3714": {"comment":"Neither: back one for NEXT_CHAR"},
        "3715": {"synopsis":"Reads the number's next character (NEXT_CHAR) and decides by it: a digit, '.', 'E', '%',\n'#', '!', 'D', or anything else, which ends the number.","comment":"Next character: a digit?"},
        "3719": {"comment":"The point?"},
        "3724": {"comment":"'E', a single's exponent?"},
        "3728": {"comment":"'%', an integer?"},
        "3733": {"comment":"'#', a double?"},
        "3738": {"comment":"'!', a single?"},
        "3743": {"comment":"'D', a double's exponent?"},
        "3745": {"comment":"None of them: the number ends"},
        "3747": {"comment":"Z clear for 'D'"},
        "3748": {"synopsis":"The number's exponent part ('E' with Z set, 'D' with Z clear): makes the number single or\ndouble, then reads an optional sign - '-', '+' or their tokens $CE / $CD - into D ($FF for\nminus) and the digits through NUM_EXP_DIGIT.","comment":"Single for 'E', double for 'D'"},
        "3751": {"comment":"Return through NUM_EXP_DIGIT"},
        "3756": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "3757": {"comment":"D = $FF: minus"},
        "3758": {"comment":"The '-' token?"},
        "3761": {"comment":"Or '-'?"},
        "3764": {"comment":"D = 0: plus"},
        "3765": {"comment":"The '+' token?"},
        "3768": {"comment":"Or '+'?"},
        "3771": {"comment":"No sign: back one, no return through the stack"},
        "3773": {"synopsis":"The exponent's digits: each goes through NUM_EXP_ACC into E; at the first non-digit a minus\nsign (D = $FF) negates E.","comment":"Next character: a digit?"},
        "3777": {"comment":"Exponent negative (D was $FF)?"},
        "3780": {"comment":"Yes: E = -E"},
        "3783": {"synopsis":"Ends a number: scales it by 10 to the power E - B (E the exponent, B the digits after the\npoint), negates it for a leading '-', and turns a single -32768 into the integer -32768\n(CINT_LIMIT). The single case returns through POP_HL_RET (POP HL, RET), so HL is the text\nposition either way.","comment":"Keep the text position"},
        "3784": {"comment":"A = the exponent - the decimals"},
        "3786": {"synopsis":"NUM_IN_SCALE's loop: one x 10 (A positive) or / 10 (A negative), until A is 0.","comment":"Positive: x 10"},
        "3789": {"comment":"Negative: / 10"},
        "3792": {"comment":"Until A is 0"},
        "3795": {"comment":"Z: there was a minus sign"},
        "3797": {"comment":"Negate"},
        "3801": {"comment":"Not a single?"},
        "3802": {"comment":"Then done"},
        "3804": {"comment":"Return through POP HL, RET"},
        "3808": {"comment":"Single -32768 to an integer"},
        "3812": {"synopsis":"The number's '.': a second point ends the number (C was $FF before the first, 0 after); the\nfirst makes an integer a single, so the digits after it count in B.","comment":"C from $FF to 0: the first point?"},
        "3814": {"comment":"A second point ends the number"},
        "3816": {"comment":"An integer becomes a single"},
        "3822": {"synopsis":"The number's '%' suffix: the number must still be an integer, else error code 2 (report SN).\nEnds the number past the suffix.","comment":"Still an integer?"},
        "3823": {"comment":"No: report SN"},
        "3826": {"synopsis":"Steps past a type suffix and ends the number.","comment":"Past the suffix"},
        "3829": {"synopsis":"The number's '#' suffix: makes it a double (NZ to TO_SNG_OR_DBL) and ends it.","comment":"Z clear: double"},
        "3830": {"synopsis":"The number's '!' suffix (Z set from the compare): makes it a single and ends it. NUM_IN_HASH\ncomes here with Z clear.","comment":"Single (Z) or double"},
        "3835": {"synopsis":"Converts the accumulator to single when Z is set, to double when Z is clear (CSNG_FN /\nCDBL_FN, the CSNG and CDBL functions). Keeps AF, BC, DE and HL."},
        "3839": {"comment":"Z: CSNG"},
        "3843": {"comment":"NZ: CDBL"},
        "3850": {"synopsis":"One step of a decimal scaling: returns at once when A is 0, else multiplies the accumulator\nby 10 (single at FP_MUL10, double DBL_MUL10) and decrements A.","comment":"A = 0: nothing to do"},
        "3851": {"synopsis":"NUM_TIMES10 without the zero test (from the scaling code at $123D)."},
        "3852": {"comment":"Single or double?"},
        "3854": {"comment":"Single: x 10"},
        "3858": {"comment":"Double: x 10"},
        "3862": {"synopsis":"Decrements A and returns (flags from the DEC).","comment":"One step done"},
        "3864": {"synopsis":"Divides the accumulator by 10 (single at FP_DIV10, double DBL_DIV10) and increments A. Keeps\nDE and HL."},
        "3867": {"comment":"Single or double?"},
        "3869": {"comment":"Single: / 10"},
        "3873": {"comment":"Double: / 10"},
        "3879": {"comment":"One step done"},
        "3881": {"synopsis":"Adds a digit to the number being read.\n1. B counts it when it is after the point (B += C + 1, C being $FF before the point, 0\n   after).\n2. Integer: HL x 10 + digit while the integer is below 3277 and the result below 32768; else\n   the integer is made a single.\n3. Single: x 10 + digit while it is below 1000000 ($9474 2400); else it is made a double.\n4. Double: x 10 (DBL_MUL10) + digit (as a double, through DBL_ADD).\nThen on with the next character (NUM_IN_CHAR)."},
        "3882": {"comment":"B + 1 after the point, + 0 before"},
        "3887": {"comment":"The digit's value"},
        "3891": {"comment":"Not an integer?"},
        "3895": {"comment":"The integer so far"},
        "3898": {"comment":"3277: would x 10 pass 32767?"},
        "3902": {"comment":"Yes: go to single"},
        "3904": {"comment":"HL x 5"},
        "3909": {"comment":"x 10"},
        "3911": {"comment":"BC = the digit (B = 0 for an integer)"},
        "3912": {"comment":"+ the digit"},
        "3913": {"comment":"32768 or more?"},
        "3918": {"comment":"No: the new integer"},
        "3921": {"synopsis":"A digit added: restores HL, BC and DE and reads the next character.","comment":"Restore and on with the number"},
        "3927": {"synopsis":"The digit made the integer 32768 or more: keep the digit and redo it in single precision.","comment":"Keep the digit"},
        "3929": {"synopsis":"The integer cannot take another digit: it becomes a single (INT_TO_SNG), and the digit is\nadded there.","comment":"The integer as a single"},
        "3932": {"comment":"Carry set: a single"},
        "3933": {"synopsis":"Adds a digit to a single, or (carry clear: a double) goes to NUM_DBL_DIGIT. A single of\n1000000 or more becomes a double first.","comment":"A double?"},
        "3935": {"comment":"1000000 as a single"},
        "3941": {"comment":"Compared with the accumulator"},
        "3944": {"comment":"1000000 or more: go to double"},
        "3947": {"comment":"Single x 10"},
        "3951": {"comment":"+ the digit"},
        "3956": {"synopsis":"The single is too large for another digit: it becomes a double (DBL_CLEAR_LOW).","comment":"The single as a double"},
        "3959": {"synopsis":"Adds a digit to a double: x 10, the digit made a double, added.","comment":"Double x 10"},
        "3962": {"comment":"Copied to the second operand"},
        "3965": {"comment":"The digit as a single"},
        "3969": {"comment":"Then as a double"},
        "3972": {"comment":"+ the copy"},
        "3977": {"synopsis":"Adds the digit in A to the single in the accumulator: pushes the accumulator, makes A a\nsingle (BYTE_TO_FP) and adds the pushed value (SNG_ADD_STACK). Also used by LOG at $083E.","comment":"The accumulator onto the stack"},
        "3980": {"comment":"The digit as a single"},
        "3983": {"synopsis":"Pops a single into BCDE and adds it to the accumulator (FP_ADD).\nEntry: a single on the top of the stack, as FP_PUSH pushes it. Used by the integer add at\n$0BEF.","comment":"The stacked single into BCDE"},
        "3985": {"comment":"Single add"},
        "3988": {"synopsis":"E = E x 10 + the digit at HL, the exponent being read. From 10 on it stops accumulating: the\nLD E,$32 hidden at $0FA2 (inside the JP M at $0FA1, never taken) sets E to 50.","comment":"Exponent already 10 or more?"},
        "3993": {"comment":"E x 4"},
        "3995": {"comment":"x 5"},
        "3996": {"comment":"x 10"},
        "3997": {"comment":"+ the digit's character"},
        "3998": {"comment":"- '0'"},
        "4001": {"comment":"Never taken: hides LD E,50 at $0FA2"},
        "4004": {"comment":"Next exponent digit"},
        "4007": {"synopsis":"Prints \" in \" (the text at IN_TEXT) and then the line number in HL; from the error report at\n$1A14 when HL is not $FFFF."},
        "4008": {"comment":"\" in \""},
        "4011": {"comment":"Printed"},
        "4015": {"synopsis":"Prints HL as a number: stores it as the integer result, makes its text (NUM_TO_TEXT's\ninteger path, no format flags) and prints it from the character after the sign position\n(PRINT_TEXT_NEXT). Used for line numbers ($1A43, $1D50, $2B5E, $2E7E).","comment":"HL as the integer result"},
        "4018": {"comment":"No format flags"},
        "4022": {"comment":"NZ: not zero, a positive number"},
        "4023": {"comment":"Make the text"},
        "4026": {"comment":"Print it from past the sign position"},
        "4029": {"synopsis":"Makes the text of the number in the accumulator, with no format (flags 0), in the buffer\nfrom $4130. Used by PRINT ($20C0), STR$ (STR_FN) and the formatted output at $111D.\n1. $4130 gets the sign: a space, '+' when flag bit 3 is set, '-' for a negative number\n   (which is negated).\n2. A zero gives \"0\" (NUM_TEXT_ZERO).\n3. Flag bit 7 set: formatted output (FMT_NUMBER). An integer: 5 digits (PUT_INT_DIGITS) with\n   leading zeros taken out (NUM_TEXT_LEAD). A single or a double: NUM_TEXT_FLOAT.\nExit: HL = the start of the text (the sign position), ended by $00; for a single or double\nHL = $4130 and DE = the $00.","comment":"No format flags"},
        "4030": {"synopsis":"NUM_TO_TEXT with the format flags in A (from the formatted output at $2DCD).","comment":"Flags set, a space at $4130"},
        "4033": {"comment":"Flag bit 3: a '+' sign?"},
        "4037": {"comment":"Yes: '+' instead of the space"},
        "4039": {"synopsis":"NUM_TO_TEXT: the sign of the number decides between a positive and a '-'."},
        "4040": {"comment":"The number's sign: A = $FF/0/1"},
        "4044": {"comment":"Positive or zero?"},
        "4047": {"comment":"Negative: '-'"},
        "4051": {"comment":"Made positive"},
        "4056": {"comment":"NZ: not zero"},
        "4057": {"synopsis":"NUM_TO_TEXT after the sign: picks the layout by the format flags and the type.","comment":"A '0' after the sign"},
        "4060": {"comment":"The format flags"},
        "4064": {"comment":"Carry = bit 7, formatted"},
        "4065": {"comment":"The type"},
        "4068": {"comment":"Formatted?"},
        "4071": {"comment":"Zero: the text is \"0\""},
        "4074": {"comment":"Single or double?"},
        "4079": {"comment":"An integer: 5 digits"},
        "4085": {"synopsis":"Tidies the start of a number's text in the buffer at $4130: each leading '0' (and ',')\nbecomes the fill character, a space or '*' (flag bit 5); a zero is put back before a '.',\n'E', 'D' or the end; a '$' is put before the number when flag bit 4 is set; then the sign\nbefore that unless flag bit 2 (sign after the number) is set. With '*' fill a space sign\nbecomes '*'.\nExit: B = the sign character; Z clear when the sign is still to be written after the number;\nHL = the text's start.","comment":"The sign position"},
        "4088": {"comment":"B = the sign"},
        "4089": {"comment":"C = the fill, a space"},
        "4091": {"comment":"The format flags"},
        "4095": {"comment":"Bit 5: '*' fill?"},
        "4099": {"comment":"Sign a space?"},
        "4101": {"comment":"Fill '*'"},
        "4105": {"comment":"Yes: the sign is '*' too"},
        "4106": {"synopsis":"NUM_TEXT_LEAD's loop: fills the position, then looks at the next character.","comment":"Position filled"},
        "4107": {"comment":"Next character: the end?"},
        "4110": {"comment":"An exponent letter?"},
        "4118": {"comment":"A leading zero?"},
        "4122": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "4126": {"comment":"The point?"},
        "4128": {"comment":"No: a digit, the number starts here"},
        "4130": {"synopsis":"Puts a '0' back before the point, the exponent or the end.","comment":"A '0' before it"},
        "4133": {"synopsis":"Flag bit 4 set? Then a '$' before the number.","comment":"Flag bit 4: '$'?"},
        "4138": {"comment":"Yes: '$' before the number"},
        "4141": {"synopsis":"Flag bit 2 clear? Then the sign before the number; set, it is left in B for after.","comment":"Flag bit 2: sign after the number?"},
        "4144": {"comment":"Yes: NZ, the sign left in B"},
        "4145": {"comment":"The sign before the number"},
        "4148": {"synopsis":"Starts a number's text: the format flags from A into $40D8, a space at $4130.\nExit: HL = $4130.","comment":"The format flags"},
        "4151": {"comment":"The number's text buffer"},
        "4154": {"comment":"Sign position: a space"},
        "4157": {"synopsis":"The text of a single or double without a format: up to 7 (single) or 17 (double) positions\nof digits.\n1. SCALE_TO_DIGITS scales the number and gives its decimal exponent.\n2. A number that fits is written in plain form with the point in its place; otherwise one\n   digit before the point and an exponent (NUM_PUT_EXP).\n3. The digits (PUT_DIGITS), then trailing zeros are taken off, and the point when nothing\n   follows it.\nExit: as NUM_TO_TEXT (HL = $4130, DE = the $00).","comment":"Single (4) or double (8)?"},
        "4160": {"comment":"A = 3 or 8"},
        "4162": {"comment":"6 or 16"},
        "4163": {"comment":"D = 7 or 17 positions"},
        "4165": {"comment":"Scale it; A = the decimal exponent"},
        "4168": {"comment":"B = 3, C = 0"},
        "4171": {"comment":"Digits before the point: negative?"},
        "4175": {"comment":"Wider than the positions?"},
        "4179": {"comment":"No: B = where the point goes"},
        "4181": {"comment":"No exponent (A = 2 - 2)"},
        "4183": {"synopsis":"NUM_TEXT_FLOAT: A = the exponent to write (0 for none), B = the digits before the point;\nwrites the digits.","comment":"A = the exponent to write"},
        "4185": {"comment":"The text position"},
        "4186": {"comment":"Exponent kept"},
        "4187": {"comment":"The point here when B runs out (DIGIT_PUNCT)"},
        "4190": {"comment":"A '0'"},
        "4192": {"comment":"Kept when the point came first"},
        "4195": {"comment":"The digits"},
        "4198": {"synopsis":"Takes off the trailing zeros, back to the last other digit (a '.' left alone is dropped\ntoo).","comment":"Back over the trailing zeros"},
        "4204": {"comment":"A point left alone?"},
        "4206": {"comment":"No: keep the last digit"},
        "4209": {"comment":"An exponent?"},
        "4210": {"comment":"No: end the text"},
        "4212": {"synopsis":"Writes a number's exponent: 'E' for a single, 'D' for a double (ADC A,A on $22 with the\nTYPE_FLAGS carry), a '+' or '-', two digits, and the $00.\nEntry: A = the exponent, flags from OR A; HL = the position.\nExit: HL = $4130; DE = the $00."},
        "4213": {"comment":"C: single, NC: double"},
        "4214": {"comment":"'E' for a single, 'D' for a double"},
        "4220": {"comment":"The exponent's sign: '+'"},
        "4222": {"comment":"Positive?"},
        "4225": {"comment":"No: '-' and the exponent negated"},
        "4229": {"synopsis":"The exponent's two digits: tens counted in B from '0', units left in A.","comment":"Tens counted from '0'"},
        "4231": {"synopsis":"Counts the tens of the exponent."},
        "4232": {"comment":"Another ten?"},
        "4236": {"comment":"Units as a digit"},
        "4238": {"comment":"Tens and units"},
        "4242": {"synopsis":"Ends the text after the '0' already written (the number is zero).","comment":"Past the last character"},
        "4243": {"synopsis":"Ends the number's text with $00.\nExit: HL = $4130 (the start); DE = the $00.","comment":"End of the text"},
        "4246": {"comment":"HL = the text's start"},
        "4250": {"synopsis":"Formatted number text (format flags at $40D8, bit 7 set; from the formatted output at\n$2DCD).\nEntry: B = the digit positions before the point, C = those after (pushed for the layouts); D\n= the flags.\nFlag bit 0 asks for an exponent form (FMT_INT_EXP / FMT_EXP), bit 6 for commas every 3\ndigits (COMMA_OPTION), bit 5 '*' fill, bit 4 '$', bit 2 the sign after the number, bit 3 a\n'+' for positive numbers.\nAn integer is written as 5 digits (PUT_INT_DIGITS) padded to the field; a single or double\nthrough FMT_FLOAT. Then FMT_FINISH.","comment":"Past the sign position"},
        "4251": {"comment":"The field's sizes kept"},
        "4252": {"comment":"Single or double?"},
        "4254": {"comment":"The format flags"},
        "4258": {"comment":"Bit 0: exponent form?"},
        "4262": {"comment":"Point after 5 digits, commas every 3"},
        "4265": {"comment":"No commas unless bit 6"},
        "4268": {"comment":"D = positions before the point, E = after"},
        "4269": {"comment":"More than 5 positions?"},
        "4272": {"comment":"Pad with zeros"},
        "4275": {"comment":"The integer's 5 digits"},
        "4278": {"synopsis":"Formatted text: E = the positions after the point; none drops the '.' (DEC_HL_RET), and the\nrest are filled with zeros (PUT_ZEROS).","comment":"Positions after the point?"},
        "4280": {"comment":"None: drop the point"},
        "4283": {"comment":"The rest are zeros"},
        "4287": {"synopsis":"Finishes formatted text: the leading fill, '$' and sign (NUM_TEXT_LEAD), a sign after the\nnumber when asked, the $00. Then, if the part before the point is wider than the field (D),\ntakes out a leading zero after the signs or else puts '%' in front."},
        "4288": {"comment":"Fill, '$' and sign"},
        "4292": {"comment":"Sign after the number?"},
        "4294": {"comment":"Yes: the sign"},
        "4296": {"synopsis":"Ends the formatted text with $00, then checks its width.","comment":"End of the text"},
        "4298": {"comment":"Just before the text"},
        "4301": {"synopsis":"Steps past the leading spaces and '*'s."},
        "4302": {"synopsis":"Does the text up to the point ($40F3 holds its address) fit the field's D positions? Then\ndone.","comment":"The point's address, low byte"},
        "4305": {"comment":"Minus the position and the field width"},
        "4307": {"comment":"Fits: done"},
        "4308": {"comment":"A leading space?"},
        "4313": {"comment":"Or a leading '*'?"},
        "4317": {"comment":"Back to the first other character"},
        "4319": {"comment":"Saved, with Z clear as the stop mark"},
        "4320": {"comment":"Each sign character loops back to $10DF"},
        "4324": {"comment":"Next character: a '-'?"},
        "4328": {"comment":"A '+'?"},
        "4331": {"comment":"A '$'?"},
        "4334": {"comment":"None: drop the loop's return"},
        "4335": {"comment":"A leading '0'?"},
        "4339": {"comment":"Followed by a digit?"},
        "4343": {"comment":"Yes: the zero can go"},
        "4344": {"comment":"Skips DEC HL/LD (HL),A, the entry $10F9"},
        "4347": {"comment":"A saved character: Z for a sign"},
        "4348": {"comment":"Written back one place on"},
        "4350": {"comment":"Drop the saved text position"},
        "4351": {"comment":"Check the width again"},
        "4354": {"synopsis":"The number is wider than its field: drops the saved characters and puts '%' before it.","comment":"Drop the saved characters"},
        "4357": {"comment":"The first non-blank position"},
        "4358": {"comment":"'%': the number does not fit"},
        "4361": {"synopsis":"Formatted text of a single or double: the exponent form for flag bit 0 (FMT_EXP); else a\nfixed-point layout when the number is below about 10^16 (single: $B60E 1BCA; double: the\nconstant at DBL_1E16), otherwise its unformatted text (NUM_TO_TEXT) with '%' in front.","comment":"The text position kept"},
        "4362": {"comment":"Bit 0: exponent form?"},
        "4366": {"comment":"A single?"},
        "4368": {"comment":"A double: compared with the limit at DBL_1E16"},
        "4374": {"comment":"16 digits"},
        "4376": {"comment":"Below the limit: fixed layout"},
        "4379": {"synopsis":"Too large for a fixed layout: the plain text (NUM_TO_TEXT) with '%' before it.","comment":"The text position"},
        "4380": {"comment":"The field's sizes"},
        "4381": {"comment":"The plain text"},
        "4384": {"comment":"'%' before it"},
        "4388": {"synopsis":"A single: below the limit for a fixed layout? D = 6 digits of precision.","comment":"A single's limit, about 10^16"},
        "4394": {"comment":"Compared with the accumulator"},
        "4397": {"comment":"Not below: too large"},
        "4400": {"comment":"6 digits"},
        "4402": {"synopsis":"Fixed-point layout with D digits of precision: SCALE_TO_DIGITS scales the number (A = its\ndecimal exponent). A whole number: zeros to pad the field (PUT_ZEROS), comma counting\n(DIGIT_GROUPS), the digits (PUT_DIGITS), the zeros for the exponent (PUT_ZP_DIGIT), the\npoint (DIGIT_PUNCT), then FMT_DECIMALS.","comment":"The number zero?"},
        "4405": {"comment":"No: scale it; A = its decimal exponent"},
        "4408": {"comment":"The text position"},
        "4409": {"comment":"B, C = the field's positions"},
        "4410": {"comment":"Negative decimal exponent?"},
        "4414": {"comment":"E = the exponent"},
        "4415": {"comment":"Positions beyond the digits"},
        "4418": {"comment":"Padded with zeros"},
        "4421": {"comment":"Comma counting"},
        "4424": {"comment":"The digits"},
        "4427": {"comment":"Zeros for the exponent?"},
        "4431": {"comment":"Then the point?"},
        "4435": {"comment":"D, E = the field's positions"},
        "4439": {"synopsis":"Fixed-point layout of a number with digits after the point (a negative decimal exponent):\nfirst divides away the digits the field's decimals cannot hold.","comment":"E = the (negative) exponent"},
        "4440": {"comment":"Positions after the point?"},
        "4442": {"comment":"Less one"},
        "4445": {"comment":"Digits too many for them?"},
        "4449": {"comment":"No: none to drop"},
        "4450": {"synopsis":"FMT_FIXED_SMALL: A = the digits to drop (negative), 0 when none.","comment":"The field and the count kept"},
        "4452": {"synopsis":"Divides by 10 (NUM_DIV10) until A is no longer negative.","comment":"Drop a digit: / 10"},
        "4455": {"comment":"More?"},
        "4458": {"comment":"B = the count left"},
        "4459": {"comment":"The digits dropped"},
        "4462": {"comment":"E = the new exponent"},
        "4463": {"comment":"Below 1?"},
        "4468": {"comment":"Positions before the digits"},
        "4470": {"comment":"Padded with zeros"},
        "4473": {"comment":"The field kept"},
        "4474": {"comment":"Comma counting"},
        "4479": {"synopsis":"A number below 1 in a fixed layout: zeros before the point, the point, zeros after it, then\nthe digits.","comment":"Zeros before the point"},
        "4482": {"comment":"Then the point"},
        "4487": {"comment":"Zeros after it, before the digits"},
        "4494": {"comment":"No commas"},
        "4496": {"synopsis":"Writes the digits (PUT_DIGITS); with nothing written after them HL goes back to the point's\naddress ($40F3).","comment":"The digits"},
        "4500": {"comment":"Anything after them?"},
        "4503": {"comment":"No: back to the point"},
        "4506": {"synopsis":"The zeros after the digits, then FMT_FINISH.","comment":"Zeros to the field's end"},
        "4511": {"comment":"D = the field's positions before the point"},
        "4515": {"synopsis":"Exponent form of an integer: makes it a single (INT_TO_SNG) first, then as FMT_EXP. The JP\nZ,$11B0 at FMT_EXP picks 6 digits: $11B0 is a LD E,$06 hidden inside the LD BC at $11AF,\nafter the LD E,$10 (16 digits, a double) at $11AD.","comment":"The text position"},
        "4517": {"comment":"The integer as a single"},
        "4521": {"comment":"Z: 6 digits"},
        "4522": {"synopsis":"Exponent form of a single (Z set: 6 digits) or double (16): scales the number\n(SCALE_TO_DIGITS), rounds it to the positions the field has (NUM_DIV10), writes the digits\nwith the point after B of them, the zeros, and the exponent (NUM_PUT_EXP).","comment":"Single? E = 6 at the hidden $11B0"},
        "4525": {"comment":"Double: 16 digits"},
        "4527": {"comment":"Skips LD E,6 (the entry $11B0)"},
        "4530": {"comment":"The number zero?"},
        "4533": {"comment":"Carry set for a zero"},
        "4534": {"comment":"No: scale it, A = the exponent"},
        "4537": {"comment":"The text position"},
        "4538": {"comment":"B, C = the field's positions"},
        "4539": {"comment":"The exponent kept"},
        "4540": {"comment":"Positions after the point?"},
        "4543": {"comment":"Less one for the point"},
        "4546": {"comment":"+ those before"},
        "4548": {"comment":"Flag bit 2: sign after the number?"},
        "4551": {"comment":"No: A = $FF, one position for the sign"},
        "4555": {"comment":"C = the digit positions"},
        "4557": {"comment":"More than the digits there are?"},
        "4560": {"synopsis":"Divides by 10 until the digits fit the field.","comment":"Round off a digit: / 10"},
        "4563": {"comment":"Until they fit"},
        "4570": {"comment":"Fewer positions than digits?"},
        "4573": {"comment":"No: none"},
        "4574": {"synopsis":"B = where the point goes; writes the digits (PUT_DIGITS) and the zeros after\n(PUT_ZEROS_PUNCT).","comment":"Negated"},
        "4579": {"comment":"B = where the point goes"},
        "4580": {"comment":"No commas"},
        "4582": {"comment":"The digits"},
        "4586": {"comment":"Zeros after them"},
        "4590": {"comment":"No positions after the point?"},
        "4591": {"comment":"Then no point"},
        "4594": {"comment":"The exponent (carry: the number is zero)"},
        "4597": {"comment":"Adjusted for the point's place"},
        "4600": {"synopsis":"Writes the exponent (NUM_PUT_EXP), then FMT_FINISH."},
        "4601": {"comment":"Exponent written"},
        "4604": {"comment":"HL = the end"},
        "4606": {"comment":"Finish"},
        "4609": {"synopsis":"Scales the number in FAC by powers of ten until its whole part has exactly the digits its\ntype prints: 6 for a single (99999.95 up to 999999.5), 16 for a double (just under 10^15 up\nto 9999999999999999.5), counting the powers of ten taken out.\n1. A double below 2^16 (exponent byte under $91) is multiplied by DBL_1E10 at a time, the\n   count dropping by 10 each time.\n2. SCALE_TOP_TEST divides by 10 (count up by one) while FAC is at or above the top limit.\n3. Otherwise FAC is multiplied by 10 (count down by one) while it is below the lower limit.\nEntry: FAC = a nonzero single or double; $40AF its type.\nExit: A = the count, the decimal exponent taken out (negative when FAC was multiplied up),\nflags from OR A; DE kept.\nCalled by the number formatter at $1045, $1135 and $11B6.","comment":"Kept for the exit"},
        "4610": {"comment":"The count starts at 0"},
        "4612": {"comment":"A single?"},
        "4616": {"synopsis":"The loop that multiplies a double below 2^16 by 10^10 until it is not.","comment":"The exponent byte of the double"},
        "4619": {"comment":"2^16 or more? Done with big steps"},
        "4624": {"comment":"DE = DBL_1E10"},
        "4627": {"comment":"Copied to the second operand at $4127"},
        "4633": {"comment":"FAC = FAC * 10^10 (double)"},
        "4636": {"comment":"The count"},
        "4637": {"comment":"Ten powers taken"},
        "4640": {"comment":"Test again"},
        "4642": {"synopsis":"Divides by 10 while FAC is too big (SCALE_TOP_TEST), then falls into the lower test.","comment":"Does not return here when FAC was too big"},
        "4645": {"synopsis":"The loop that multiplies FAC by 10 while it is below the lower limit.","comment":"A double?"},
        "4648": {"comment":"BCDE = 99999.95, the single lower limit"},
        "4654": {"comment":"Compare FAC with it"},
        "4659": {"synopsis":"The double lower limit: compares FAC with DBL_1E15_LESS.","comment":"DE = just under 10^15"},
        "4662": {"comment":"Compare the double FAC with it"},
        "4665": {"synopsis":"Done when FAC is at or above the lower limit; otherwise times 10 and test again.","comment":"At or above the limit? Done"},
        "4668": {"comment":"The count"},
        "4669": {"comment":"FAC = FAC * 10, count down by one"},
        "4673": {"comment":"Test again"},
        "4675": {"synopsis":"Divides FAC by 10, counts up by one and tests the top limit again.","comment":"The count"},
        "4676": {"comment":"FAC = FAC / 10, count up by one"},
        "4680": {"comment":"Still too big?"},
        "4683": {"synopsis":"The end of SCALE_TO_DIGITS: A = the count with flags from OR A, DE restored.","comment":"A = the count"},
        "4685": {"comment":"DE as at entry"},
        "4687": {"synopsis":"Compares FAC with the top limit of its type: 999999.5 for a single, DBL_1E16_LESS for a\ndouble. When FAC is at or above it, drops the return address and goes on at SCALE_DOWN,\nwhich calls this again; when below, returns (to SCALE_DONE once SCALE_DOWN has run).\nEntry: FAC = the number; the count under the return address on the stack.","comment":"A double?"},
        "4691": {"comment":"BCDE = 999999.5"},
        "4697": {"comment":"Compare FAC with it"},
        "4702": {"synopsis":"The double top limit: compares FAC with DBL_1E16_LESS.","comment":"DE = 9999999999999999.5"},
        "4705": {"comment":"Compare the double FAC with it"},
        "4708": {"synopsis":"Takes the return address off; too big goes to SCALE_DOWN, else back to the caller.","comment":"HL = where to return"},
        "4709": {"comment":"At or above the limit? Divide by 10"},
        "4712": {"comment":"Return to the caller"},
        "4713": {"synopsis":"Writes A \"0\" characters at HL, none when A = 0.\nEntry: A = how many; HL = the output.\nExit: HL past them, A = 0, Z set.","comment":"None at all?"},
        "4714": {"synopsis":"The loop of PUT_ZEROS: one \"0\" per pass until A is 0.","comment":"All written?"},
        "4716": {"comment":"\"0\""},
        "4721": {"synopsis":"Writes A \"0\" digits at HL with DIGIT_PUNCT between them, so a point or comma falls among\nthem where the counters say. With Z set at entry writes nothing; with Z clear the first \"0\"\ngoes out at once.\nEntry: A = how many (1 or more when Z is clear); HL = the output; B, C as for DIGIT_PUNCT.\nExit: HL past what was written; A = 0.","comment":"Z clear: the first \"0\" without a check"},
        "4723": {"synopsis":"The loop of PUT_ZEROS_PUNCT: stops when A has counted down to 0.","comment":"All written?"},
        "4724": {"comment":"A point or a comma due here?"},
        "4727": {"synopsis":"Writes one \"0\" of PUT_ZEROS_PUNCT and counts it.","comment":"\"0\""},
        "4730": {"comment":"One fewer to write"},
        "4733": {"synopsis":"Sets the counters DIGIT_PUNCT works with for a number with E + D digits before the point: B\n= E + D + 1, and C = 1 + the digits before the first thousands comma (the digit count modulo\n3, with 3 for 0). Without commas (bit 6 of $40D8 clear) C = 0.\nEntry: D, E = the two parts of the digit count before the point.\nExit: B, C as above; Z set when there are no commas.\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"Digits before the point"},
        "4735": {"comment":"Plus one: DIGIT_PUNCT counts to 0"},
        "4737": {"comment":"A = the digits + 2"},
        "4738": {"synopsis":"Subtracts 3 until A goes below 0: what is left picks the first group's length.","comment":"Another 3?"},
        "4742": {"comment":"2-4: digits of the first group + 1"},
        "4744": {"comment":"C = counts to the first comma"},
        "4745": {"synopsis":"Keeps C only when bit 6 of $40D8, the format's comma flag, is set; clears it otherwise. Also\nentered from $10A9.\nExit: Z set (and C = 0) when no commas are wanted.","comment":"The format flags"},
        "4748": {"comment":"Commas wanted? Keep C"},
        "4751": {"comment":"C = 0: DIGIT_PUNCT writes no comma"},
        "4753": {"synopsis":"Called before each digit and once after the last, so its n-th call comes after n - 1 digits.\nCounts B down: where it reaches 0, writes \".\" at HL, keeps the point's address at $40F3 and\nclears C so that no comma follows. Otherwise counts C down: where it reaches 0, writes \",\"\nand sets C = 3 for the next group.\nEntry: HL = the output; B = 1 + the digits before the point; C = 1 + the digits before the\nnext comma, 0 for none (it wraps to 255).\nExit: HL past anything written.\nChanges: B, C, flags except carry.","comment":"The point due here?"},
        "4756": {"synopsis":"Writes the decimal point: entered here from $1183 to put it at HL directly.","comment":"\".\""},
        "4758": {"comment":"$40F3 = where the point is"},
        "4762": {"comment":"C = 0: no commas after the point"},
        "4764": {"synopsis":"Counts C down; at 0 writes \",\" at HL and starts the next group of 3.","comment":"A comma due here?"},
        "4766": {"comment":"\",\""},
        "4769": {"comment":"Three digits to the next one"},
        "4772": {"synopsis":"Writes the whole part of FAC, which SCALE_TO_DIGITS has scaled, as decimal digits at HL: 6\nfor a single, 16 for a double, with leading zeros, calling DIGIT_PUNCT before each digit and\nafter the last so the point and thousands commas fall in place, then a $00.\n1. A double: FAC + DBL_HALF rounds it; DBL_FLOOR makes it a whole number in the 7 bytes from\n   $411D; 10 digits come from subtracting the powers 10^15 to 10^6 of POW10_7BYTE; the 3\n   bytes left move into FAC as a 24-bit number.\n2. A single (PUT_DIGITS_SNG): adding a half (FP_ADD_HALF) rounds it; FP_WHOLE_PART makes it\n   a 24-bit whole number.\n3. Both: 2 digits from POW10_3BYTE (PUT_24BIT), the last 4 from POW10_WORDS (the loop of\n   PUT_INT_DIGITS).\nEntry: FAC = the scaled number; HL = the output; B, C as DIGIT_GROUPS sets them.\nExit: HL at the $00 after the digits; DE kept.","comment":"Kept for the exit"},
        "4773": {"comment":"A single?"},
        "4779": {"comment":"Copy FAC to the second operand at $4127"},
        "4782": {"comment":"FAC = DBL_HALF"},
        "4788": {"comment":"FAC = the number + 0.5 (double)"},
        "4791": {"comment":"Whole number in $411D-$4123"},
        "4797": {"comment":"DE = POW10_7BYTE"},
        "4800": {"comment":"10 digits from the 7-byte powers"},
        "4802": {"synopsis":"One digit of a double: counts how often the 7-byte power goes into the number at $411D.","comment":"A point or a comma due here?"},
        "4808": {"comment":"The power's address"},
        "4809": {"comment":"\"0\" - 1"},
        "4811": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the power from the 7 bytes at $411D until it goes below 0.","comment":"The digit one higher"},
        "4814": {"comment":"$411D -= the power (7 bytes)"},
        "4817": {"comment":"Not below 0? Again"},
        "4820": {"comment":"One too many: add it back"},
        "4823": {"comment":"DE = the next power"},
        "4825": {"comment":"The digit"},
        "4829": {"comment":"All 10 digits?"},
        "4834": {"comment":"FAC = the 3 bytes left (from $411D)"},
        "4840": {"comment":"On to the last 6 digits"},
        "4842": {"synopsis":"A single: rounded and made a 24-bit whole number in FAC."},
        "4844": {"comment":"FAC = FAC + 0.5"},
        "4848": {"comment":"A 24-bit whole number in CDE"},
        "4851": {"comment":"Into FAC"},
        "4854": {"synopsis":"Writes the first 2 of the last 6 digits from the 24-bit whole number in $4121-$4123."},
        "4856": {"comment":"Carry clear: CCF makes two passes"},
        "4857": {"comment":"DE = POW10_3BYTE"},
        "4860": {"synopsis":"One digit from a 3-byte power; carry, flipped each pass, says whether another follows.","comment":"Set on the first pass, clear on the second"},
        "4861": {"comment":"A point or a comma due here?"},
        "4868": {"comment":"CDE = the number"},
        "4872": {"comment":"\"0\" - 1"},
        "4874": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the 3-byte power from CDE until it goes below 0.","comment":"The digit one higher"},
        "4875": {"comment":"CDE -= the power"},
        "4888": {"comment":"Not below 0? Again"},
        "4890": {"comment":"One too many: add it back"},
        "4893": {"comment":"Past the power"},
        "4894": {"comment":"FAC = what is left"},
        "4897": {"comment":"DE = the next power"},
        "4899": {"comment":"The digit"},
        "4903": {"comment":"The second pass?"},
        "4905": {"comment":"Past 10000 of POW10_WORDS: 3-byte table did it"},
        "4907": {"comment":"4 digits left"},
        "4909": {"comment":"On to the word loop"},
        "4911": {"synopsis":"Writes the 16-bit number in $4121 (an integer's value, unsigned here) as five digits at HL\nwith leading zeros, calling DIGIT_PUNCT before each and after the last, then a $00. Its loop\n(PUT_16_DIGIT) also gives the last four digits of PUT_DIGITS.\nEntry: HL = the output; B, C as for DIGIT_PUNCT.\nExit: HL at the $00 after the digits; DE kept.\nCalled from $0FF2 and $10B3.","comment":"Kept for the exit"},
        "4912": {"comment":"DE = POW10_WORDS, from 10000"},
        "4915": {"comment":"5 digits"},
        "4917": {"synopsis":"One digit from a word power: counts how often it goes into the number at $4121.","comment":"A point or a comma due here?"},
        "4924": {"comment":"BC = the power"},
        "4929": {"comment":"Stack: the next power's address"},
        "4931": {"comment":"HL = the number"},
        "4934": {"comment":"\"0\" - 1"},
        "4936": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the power (DE) from HL until it goes below 0.","comment":"The digit one higher"},
        "4937": {"comment":"HL -= the power"},
        "4943": {"comment":"Not below 0? Again"},
        "4945": {"comment":"One too many: add it back"},
        "4946": {"comment":"What is left, for the next digit"},
        "4949": {"comment":"DE = the next power"},
        "4951": {"comment":"The digit"},
        "4955": {"comment":"All digits?"},
        "4958": {"comment":"A point after the last digit?"},
        "4961": {"comment":"$00 ends the digits"},
        "4962": {"comment":"DE as at entry"},
        "4964": {"synopsis":"The double 10^10 (bytes low first, exponent byte $A2 last). SCALE_TO_DIGITS copies it to\n$4127 to multiply a small double up by 10^10 at a time."},
        "4972": {"synopsis":"The double just under 10^15 (999999999999999.95): the lower limit SCALE_TO_DIGITS multiplies\na double up to."},
        "4980": {"synopsis":"The double 9999999999999999.5: the top limit SCALE_TOP_TEST divides a double down below."},
        "4988": {"synopsis":"The double 0.5 (seven zero bytes, then exponent $80), added by PUT_DIGITS to round. Its last\nfour bytes are SNG_HALF."},
        "4992": {"synopsis":"The single 0.5 ($00 $00 $00 $80), and the end of DBL_HALF. Read by FP_ADD_HALF (FAC + 0.5)\nand by SQR_FN."},
        "4996": {"synopsis":"The double 10^16, compared with FAC by the number formatter at $1110."},
        "5004": {"synopsis":"Ten 7-byte whole numbers, low byte first: 10^15, 10^14, ... 10^6. PUT_DIGITS subtracts each\nfrom the 7-byte number at $411D to get a double's first 10 digits."},
        "5074": {"synopsis":"Two 3-byte whole numbers, low byte first: 100000 and 10000, for the first two of the last\nsix digits (PUT_24BIT)."},
        "5080": {"synopsis":"Five words: 10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1. PUT_INT_DIGITS starts at the first for five digits;\nPUT_DIGITS starts at the second for its last four."},
        "5090": {"synopsis":"Puts FP_NEGATE (which changes the sign of FAC) under the caller's return address, so that\nFAC's sign is changed when the calling routine returns.\nChanges: HL.","comment":"FP_NEGATE: change the sign of FAC"},
        "5093": {"comment":"Under the caller's return address"},
        "5094": {"comment":"Back to the caller"},
        "5095": {"synopsis":"The SQR function: SQR(x) as x raised to the power 0.5. Pushes x, loads SNG_HALF into FAC and\njoins POWER_FAC.\nEntry: FAC = x (single).\nExit: FAC = the square root.","comment":"The base, x, on the stack"},
        "5098": {"comment":"FAC = 0.5, the exponent"},
        "5106": {"synopsis":"The power operator (↑, token $D1), whose address $23DA passes on (with D = $7F) after making\nthe left operand a single and pushing it: makes the exponent in FAC a single (CSNG) and\nfalls into POWER_FAC with the base on the stack."},
        "5109": {"synopsis":"Raises the single on the stack (the base) to the power in FAC (the exponent):\n1. Exponent 0: EXP_FN of 0, which is 1.\n2. Base 0: a negative exponent stops with error code 20 (report /0), a positive one gives 0.\n3. A negative base: the exponent is made whole (SNG_FLOOR) and compared with itself. A whole\n   exponent makes the base positive, and an odd one also sets NEGATE_ON_RET; a base left\n   negative makes LOG stop with error code 8 (report FC).\n4. EXP(exponent * LOG(base)): LOG (LOG_FN), the product, then into EXP_FN.\nEntry: FAC = the exponent; the base on the stack (popped into BCDE).\nExit: FAC = the result.","comment":"BCDE = the base"},
        "5111": {"comment":"The exponent's sign"},
        "5114": {"comment":"The base's exponent byte"},
        "5115": {"comment":"Exponent 0? EXP(0) = 1"},
        "5117": {"comment":"A positive exponent?"},
        "5120": {"comment":"Base 0 to a negative power?"},
        "5121": {"comment":"Report /0"},
        "5124": {"synopsis":"A positive exponent: a zero base gives 0; otherwise the sign of the base decides.","comment":"Base 0?"},
        "5125": {"comment":"0 to a positive power is 0"},
        "5128": {"comment":"Keep the base"},
        "5130": {"comment":"$7F if the base is positive, $FF if negative"},
        "5133": {"comment":"BCDE = the exponent"},
        "5136": {"comment":"A positive base? Straight on"},
        "5139": {"comment":"Keep the exponent"},
        "5141": {"comment":"FAC = the exponent made whole"},
        "5144": {"comment":"BCDE = the exponent again"},
        "5146": {"comment":"Keep its low byte"},
        "5147": {"comment":"Z: the exponent is whole"},
        "5151": {"comment":"Carry = bit 0: an odd exponent"},
        "5153": {"synopsis":"FAC = the base again; for a negative base a whole exponent fixes the sign.","comment":"Sign and exponent byte of the base"},
        "5157": {"comment":"Its low bytes"},
        "5161": {"comment":"Odd: negate the result at the end"},
        "5164": {"comment":"Whole: the base made positive"},
        "5167": {"comment":"Keep the exponent"},
        "5169": {"comment":"FAC = LOG(base)"},
        "5172": {"comment":"BCDE = the exponent"},
        "5174": {"comment":"FAC = exponent * LOG(base), then EXP"},
        "5177": {"synopsis":"The EXP function, e^x, single precision:\n1. t = x * 1.4427 (1 / ln 2). With an exponent byte of $88 or more (|t| of 128 or more) goes\n   to EXP_RANGE_OUT: 0 for a negative x, error code 10 (report OV) for a positive one.\n2. n = INT(t) through SNG_FLOOR, the single-precision part of INT; n + $82 is kept for the\n   result's exponent byte (a carry out of it also goes to EXP_RANGE_OUT).\n3. r = (n + 1) * ln 2 - x (FP_ONE is 1; FP_MUL_LN2 multiplies by ln 2), between 0 and ln 2.\n4. FAC = the polynomial EXP_COEFFS in r (POLY_EVAL, close to e^-r), times 2^(n + 1): BCDE is\n   exponent byte n + $82 with a zero mantissa.\nEntry: FAC = x (single).\nExit: FAC = e^x.","comment":"Keep x"},
        "5180": {"comment":"BCDE = 1.4427, 1 / ln 2"},
        "5186": {"comment":"FAC = t"},
        "5189": {"comment":"Its exponent byte"},
        "5192": {"comment":"|t| 128 or more? 0 or report OV"},
        "5197": {"comment":"FAC = n, A = its low byte"},
        "5200": {"comment":"A = n + $82, the result's exponent byte"},
        "5204": {"comment":"Out of range? 0 or report OV"},
        "5207": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "5208": {"comment":"FP_ONE: the single 1"},
        "5211": {"comment":"FAC = n + 1"},
        "5214": {"comment":"FAC = (n + 1) * ln 2"},
        "5217": {"comment":"A = the exponent byte"},
        "5218": {"comment":"BCDE = x"},
        "5221": {"comment":"FAC = x - (n + 1) * ln 2"},
        "5224": {"comment":"FAC = r, from 0 to ln 2"},
        "5227": {"comment":"HL = EXP_COEFFS"},
        "5230": {"comment":"FAC = the polynomial in r"},
        "5233": {"comment":"A zero mantissa"},
        "5236": {"comment":"B = the exponent byte n + $82"},
        "5237": {"comment":"C = 0: BCDE = 2^(n + 1)"},
        "5238": {"comment":"FAC = FAC * 2^(n + 1)"},
        "5241": {"synopsis":"EXP_FN's polynomial for POLY_EVAL: a count (8), then 8 singles, highest power first:\n-0.000141, 0.00133, -0.00830, 0.04166, -0.16667, 0.5, -1, 1 (close to the series of e^-r)."},
        "5274": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an odd polynomial: x * P(x^2), with P's table at HL (as POLY_EVAL reads it).\nPushes x, leaves $0C32 (which pops x and multiplies FAC by it) as the return, squares FAC\nand goes on at POLY_EVAL.\nEntry: FAC = x; HL = the table.\nExit: FAC = x * P(x^2).\nUsed by SIN_FN, ATN_FN and $0831.","comment":"Keep x"},
        "5277": {"comment":"$0C32: FAC = FAC * x, at the end"},
        "5281": {"comment":"Keep the table's address"},
        "5282": {"comment":"BCDE = x"},
        "5285": {"comment":"FAC = x * x"},
        "5288": {"comment":"HL = the table"},
        "5289": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a polynomial in FAC by Horner's rule from the table at HL: a count byte n, then n\nsingles, the highest power's coefficient first. Keeps the variable on the stack, starts FAC\nat the first coefficient and, n - 1 times, multiplies by the variable and adds the next\ncoefficient.\nEntry: FAC = the variable; HL = the table.\nExit: FAC = the polynomial's value.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Keep the variable"},
        "5292": {"comment":"A = the count"},
        "5294": {"comment":"FAC = the first coefficient"},
        "5297": {"synopsis":"LD B,$F1 hides POP AF at $14B2: the first pass skips it, and the loop's JR goes back to it\nto get the count again.","comment":"Hides POP AF at $14B2, the loop's start"},
        "5299": {"comment":"BCDE = the variable"},
        "5301": {"comment":"All coefficients used?"},
        "5303": {"comment":"Keep the variable, count and pointer"},
        "5307": {"comment":"FAC = FAC * the variable"},
        "5311": {"comment":"BCDE = the next coefficient"},
        "5315": {"comment":"FAC = FAC + it"},
        "5319": {"comment":"Back to the POP AF at $14B2"},
        "5321": {"synopsis":"The RND function. The argument is made an integer (CINT, CINT_FN); a negative one stops with\nerror code 8 (report FC). RND(0) is RND_NEXT's fraction from 0 to just under 1; RND(n) is\nINT(r * n + 1), a whole number from 1 to n.\nEntry: FAC = the argument.\nExit: FAC = the result (single).","comment":"HL = the argument as an integer"},
        "5325": {"comment":"Negative? Report FC"},
        "5329": {"comment":"RND(0): just the fraction"},
        "5333": {"comment":"Keep n"},
        "5334": {"comment":"FAC = r, a new fraction"},
        "5337": {"comment":"BCDE = r"},
        "5340": {"comment":"Stack r's DE, HL = n"},
        "5342": {"comment":"Keep r's BC"},
        "5343": {"comment":"FAC = n as a single"},
        "5346": {"comment":"BCDE = r"},
        "5348": {"comment":"FAC = r * n"},
        "5351": {"comment":"FP_ONE: the single 1"},
        "5354": {"comment":"FAC = r * n + 1"},
        "5357": {"comment":"Made whole: 1 to n"},
        "5360": {"synopsis":"Steps the 24-bit random seed at $40AA-$40AC and returns it as a fraction:\n1. CDE = seed * the 24-bit multiplier at $4090-$4092 ($4090 most significant), low 24 bits,\n   bit by bit; each multiplier byte is rotated in place eight times, so it is unchanged\n   afterwards.\n2. seed = CDE + $05B065, stored back.\n3. FAC = seed / 2^24: type single ($0AEF), $4124 and $4125 = $80, normalised by\n   FP_NORMALIZE.\nExit: FAC = a number from 0 to just under 1.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The multiplier's first byte"},
        "5364": {"comment":"CDE = 0, the product"},
        "5368": {"comment":"3 multiplier bytes"},
        "5370": {"synopsis":"The loop over the three multiplier bytes: 8 bits each.","comment":"8 bits"},
        "5372": {"synopsis":"One bit: the product doubled, then the seed added if the multiplier bit is set.","comment":"DE doubled"},
        "5375": {"comment":"C doubled with DE's carry"},
        "5378": {"comment":"HL = the multiplier byte's address"},
        "5379": {"comment":"Rotate the byte, carry = its top bit"},
        "5383": {"comment":"Bit clear? Nothing to add"},
        "5387": {"comment":"Add the seed's low word"},
        "5392": {"comment":"And its high byte"},
        "5398": {"synopsis":"Counts the bits of a multiplier byte, then steps to the next byte.","comment":"More bits?"},
        "5402": {"comment":"The next multiplier byte"},
        "5405": {"comment":"More bytes?"},
        "5410": {"comment":"Plus $B065"},
        "5414": {"comment":"The seed's new low word"},
        "5417": {"comment":"Type single; keeps the carry"},
        "5420": {"comment":"Plus $05 and the carry: the high byte"},
        "5423": {"comment":"The seed's new high byte"},
        "5427": {"comment":"$80 in $4125 and in the exponent byte $4124"},
        "5435": {"comment":"C = the high byte"},
        "5436": {"comment":"B = 0"},
        "5438": {"comment":"Normalise CDE into FAC: 0 to under 1"},
        "5441": {"synopsis":"The COS function: COS(x) as SIN(x + pi/2), adding SNG_HALF_PI and falling into SIN_FN.\nEntry: FAC = x (single).\nExit: FAC = the cosine.","comment":"pi / 2"},
        "5444": {"comment":"FAC = x + pi / 2"},
        "5447": {"synopsis":"The SIN function, single precision:\n1. u = x / 2pi, then u - INT(u) (SNG_FLOOR): the fraction of a turn.\n2. A fraction above a quarter is folded back into -0.25..0.25 with the same sine: 0.5 - u up\n   to three quarters, u - 1 above.\n3. FAC = the odd polynomial SIN_COEFFS in it (ODD_POLY), about 2pi u - (2pi u)^3 / 6 + ...\nEntry: FAC = x.\nExit: FAC = the sine.","comment":"Keep x"},
        "5450": {"comment":"BCDE = 6.2832, 2pi"},
        "5456": {"comment":"Into FAC"},
        "5459": {"comment":"BCDE = x"},
        "5461": {"comment":"FAC = x / 2pi, in turns"},
        "5464": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "5467": {"comment":"FAC = INT of it"},
        "5470": {"comment":"BCDE = the turns"},
        "5472": {"comment":"FAC = the fraction of a turn"},
        "5475": {"comment":"SNG_QUARTER"},
        "5478": {"comment":"FAC = 0.25 - the fraction"},
        "5481": {"comment":"Its sign"},
        "5484": {"comment":"Carry: a quarter turn or less"},
        "5485": {"comment":"Up to a quarter? Not folded"},
        "5488": {"comment":"FAC = 0.75 - the fraction"},
        "5491": {"comment":"Its sign"},
        "5494": {"comment":"Carry clear: negate at the end"},
        "5495": {"synopsis":"Turns the value back into the folded fraction, then the polynomial.","comment":"Keep the sign and the carry"},
        "5496": {"comment":"Positive? Negate it"},
        "5499": {"comment":"SNG_QUARTER"},
        "5502": {"comment":"FAC = FAC + 0.25"},
        "5505": {"comment":"The flags again"},
        "5506": {"comment":"Folded past a quarter: negate"},
        "5509": {"comment":"HL = SIN_COEFFS"},
        "5512": {"comment":"FAC = the odd polynomial"},
        "5515": {"synopsis":"The single pi/2 (1.5708), added by COS_FN and taken away from by ATN_FN."},
        "5519": {"synopsis":"The single 0.25, a quarter turn: SIN_FN folds the fraction of a turn with it."},
        "5523": {"synopsis":"SIN_FN's odd polynomial for ODD_POLY: a count (5), then 5 singles, highest power first:\n39.711, -76.575, 81.602, -41.342, 6.2832 (2pi)."},
        "5544": {"synopsis":"The TAN function: SIN(x) / COS(x).\nEntry: FAC = x (single).\nExit: FAC = the tangent.","comment":"Keep x"},
        "5547": {"comment":"FAC = SIN(x)"},
        "5550": {"comment":"BCHL = x"},
        "5552": {"comment":"Keep SIN(x)"},
        "5555": {"comment":"BCDE = x"},
        "5556": {"comment":"Into FAC"},
        "5559": {"comment":"FAC = COS(x)"},
        "5562": {"comment":"FAC = SIN(x) / COS(x)"},
        "5565": {"synopsis":"The ATN function, single precision:\n1. A negative x is made positive, with NEGATE_ON_RET to change the result's sign.\n2. x of 1 or more (exponent byte $81 or above) is replaced by 1 / x, with FP_MEM_SUB left as\n   the return so that the result becomes pi/2 minus it (HL = SNG_HALF_PI on the way out).\n3. FAC = the odd polynomial ATN_COEFFS (ODD_POLY), about x - x^3/3 + x^5/5 ...\nEntry: FAC = x.\nExit: FAC = the angle in radians.","comment":"The sign of x"},
        "5568": {"comment":"Negative: negate the result at the end"},
        "5571": {"comment":"And make x positive"},
        "5574": {"comment":"The exponent byte"},
        "5577": {"comment":"Below 1? Straight to the series"},
        "5581": {"comment":"BCDE = 1"},
        "5586": {"comment":"FAC = 1 / x"},
        "5589": {"comment":"FP_MEM_SUB: (HL) - FAC, at the end"},
        "5593": {"synopsis":"The series for x below 1, then HL = SNG_HALF_PI for FP_MEM_SUB if it is waiting.","comment":"HL = ATN_COEFFS"},
        "5596": {"comment":"FAC = the odd polynomial"},
        "5599": {"comment":"For FP_MEM_SUB: pi/2 - FAC"},
        "5603": {"synopsis":"ATN_FN's odd polynomial for ODD_POLY: a count (9), then 9 singles, highest power first:\n0.00287, -0.01617, 0.04291, -0.07529, 0.10656, -0.14209, 0.19994, -0.33333, 1."},
        "5640": {"synopsis":"The functions' routine addresses: 36 words, one per function token from $D7 (SGN) to $FA\n(MID$), in token order (SGN, INT, ABS, FRE, INP, POS, SQR, RND, LOG, EXP, COS, SIN, TAN,\nATN, PEEK, CVI ... MID$). $2527 takes $D7 from the token and $2550 doubles it; FN_TABLE_JP\nadds FN_DISPATCH and jumps to the word, with $253E as the return. Entries 6-13 (SQR to ATN)\nget their argument made a single (CSNG, $257B) first. CVI to MKD$ point at RAM hooks\n$4152-$4170 (JP $012D at power-on)."},
        "5712": {"synopsis":"The keywords of tokens $80 (END) to $FB (the apostrophe), in token order, each one's first\ncharacter with bit 7 set and the rest plain ASCII; a lone $80 at $1821 ends the table. The\ntokeniser at $1BF6 walks it from $164F, counting the bit-7 bytes in B from $7F, so a match's\ntoken is $80 + its place; the lone $80 (0 once bit 7 is masked) ends the search. LIST's\nexpansion at $2BA9 counts token - $7F first characters and copies that keyword out with bit\n7 cleared."},
        "6177": {"comment":"$80: the end of the keyword table"},
        "6178": {"synopsis":"The statement dispatch table: 60 words, the routine of each statement token from $80 (END)\nto $BB (NEW), in token order. EXEC_STATEMENT takes token - $80, doubles it and jumps to the\nword at STMT_TABLE plus that, with the next character read. Words of $41xx are RAM hooks\n(CMD, LINE, OPEN ... SAVE, DEF), which stop with error code 44 at power-on."},
        "6298": {"synopsis":"Seven precedence bytes for the operator tokens $CD-$D3 (+, -, *, /, the power arrow, AND,\nOR), read by the expression code at $237D indexed by token - $CD: $79 for + and -, $7C for *\nand /, $7F for the power, $50 for AND, $46 for OR. A higher byte binds tighter."},
        "6305": {"synopsis":"Five words read by CONVERT_TYPE for LET ($1F35), indexed by (type byte AND 7) x 2: the\nroutine that brings the value to the variable's type. 0 (double, type 8) CDBL_FN, 1 unused\n($0000), 2 (integer) CINT_FN, 3 (string) NEED_STRING, which stops with code 24 (report TM)\nunless it is a string, 4 (single) CSNG_FN."},
        "6315": {"synopsis":"Five words read at $244E, indexed by the operator number kept at $40B0 (+ 0, - 1, * 2, / 3,\ncomparison 4, set at $2408): the double-precision routines DBL_ADD, DBL_SUB, DBL_MUL,\nDBL_DIV and DBL_CMP, the comparison the relational operators use."},
        "6325": {"synopsis":"Five words read at ARITH_SNG_OP the same way: the single-precision routines FP_ADD, FP_SUB,\nFP_MUL, FP_DIV and FP_CMP (operator number 4, the comparison)."},
        "6335": {"synopsis":"Five words read at $2428, indexed by the operator number: the integer routines INT_ADD,\nINT_SUB, INT_MUL, INT_DIVIDE for / and INT_CMP (operator number 4, the comparison)."},
        "6345": {"synopsis":"The errors' two-letter reports, two bytes each: ERROR_E prints the pair at ERROR_CODES + E\n($19E9), so the error code is twice the pair's index: 0 NF, 2 SN, 4 RG, 6 OD, 8 FC, 10 OV,\n12 OM, 14 UL, 16 BS, 18 DD, 20 /0, 22 ID, 24 TM, 26 OS, 28 LS, 30 ST, 32 CN, 34 NR, 36 RW,\n38 UE, 40 MO, 42 FD, 44 SN (the code the RAM hooks give)."},
        "6391": {"synopsis":"39 bytes that BASIC_INIT copies to $4080-$40A6: code that runs in RAM, with immediates the\nROM patches, mixed with the initial values of BASIC variables.\n- RAM_IMAGE_4080-$1904 ($4080-$408D): the division's trial subtraction, code run in RAM. It\n  subtracts three bytes from L, H and B and returns A = the immediate of LD A,n at $408C;\n  the single division at FP_DIV patches the divisor's bytes into $4081, $4085 and $4089 and\n  keeps a fourth byte at $408C. Called at $08CA.\n- USR_RND_IMAGE-$1909 ($408E-$4092): data (see there).\n- IN_PORT_IMAGE-$190F ($4093-$4098): IN A,(n) / RET and OUT (n),A / RET, code run in RAM.\n- VARS_IMAGE-ERROR_TEXT ($4099-$40A6): data (see there).","comment":"$4081: patched by $08BB"},
        "6393": {"comment":"Result into L"},
        "6395": {"comment":"$4085: patched by $08B6"},
        "6398": {"comment":"Then B"},
        "6399": {"comment":"$4089: patched by $08B1"},
        "6402": {"comment":"$408C: kept and patched by the division"},
        "6404": {"comment":"Back to $08CD with the borrow"},
        "6405": {"synopsis":"Five data bytes of the RAM image. The first two go to $408E-$408F: the address of ERROR_FC,\nwhich the USR function jumps through (USR_FN), so USR stops with error code 8 (report FC)\nuntil a program stores an address there. The last three ($40 $E6 $4D) go to $4090-$4092: the\nmultiplier RND_NEXT steps the random seed with."},
        "6410": {"synopsis":"IN A,(n) / RET, run in RAM at $4093: the INP function (INP_FN) patches the port into $4094\nand calls it.","comment":"$4094: the port, patched by $2AF2 and $2B11"},
        "6413": {"synopsis":"OUT (n),A / RET, run in RAM at $4096: the OUT statement (OUT_CMD) patches the port into\n$4097 ($2B14) and jumps to it.","comment":"$4097: the port, patched by $2B14"},
        "6416": {"synopsis":"The rest of the RAM image, values for $4099-$40A5 (the last image byte, ERROR_TEXT, is also\nthe first of ERROR_TEXT):\n- $4099-$409C 0: $4099 the last key read ($1DA5), $409A the last error code ($19B7), $409B\n  the printer's column ($038F), $409C the output selector (0 the display, $032F).\n- $409D 64: the display's line width (read at PRINT_FIT_DISP); $409E 48, the column past\n  which a comma starts a new display line (COMMA_DISPLAY); $409F 0.\n- $40A0 $434C: the stack's top until MEM_TOP_SET sets it (BASIC_INIT's call to RESET_STACK\n  puts SP there).\n- $40A2 $FFFE: the current line number; $FFFE marks the power-on question, and an error\n  while it holds $FFFE restarts at BOOT ($1A0E).\n- $40A4 $42E9: the start of the program; BASIC_INIT writes the 0 below it at $42E8."},
        "6429": {"synopsis":"\" Error\" and a zero: printed after the two letters of a report ($19FE)."},
        "6436": {"synopsis":"\" in \" and a zero: printed before the line number of a report ($0FA8)."},
        "6441": {"synopsis":"\"READY\", a carriage return and a zero: BASIC_READY prints it before each > prompt; also\nprinted at $2BD9 and $2C7A."},
        "6448": {"synopsis":"\"Break\" and a zero: STOP and the BREAK key print it, then \" in \" and the line ($1DDB)."},
        "6454": {"synopsis":"Looks down the stack for the FOR block of a variable. Blocks start with the byte $81 (the\nFOR token) and are 17 bytes long: the token, the variable's address, then 14 bytes. The\nsearch starts above its caller's return address and stops at the first byte that is not $81.\nEntry: DE = the variable's address, or 0 for any FOR block.\nExit: Z if found, HL = the block's byte after the address, BC = 14 (so HL + BC is past the\nblock); NZ if not, A = the byte found (another block's token, such as GOSUB's $91, or the 0\nat the stack's bottom).\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.\nCalled by FOR ($1CAA), RETURN ($1EE1) and NEXT ($22BF).","comment":"Past this routine's and the caller's return addresses"},
        "6458": {"synopsis":"The loop over the stack's blocks, one 17-byte FOR block at a time.","comment":"A block's first byte: the FOR token?"},
        "6463": {"comment":"BC = the block's variable address"},
        "6467": {"comment":"Keep the pointer past the address"},
        "6470": {"comment":"DE = 0: any block will do?"},
        "6476": {"comment":"The block's variable the one asked for?"},
        "6477": {"synopsis":"Returns Z with the block found, or steps HL over this block.","comment":"The rest of the block's length"},
        "6481": {"comment":"Found: Z"},
        "6482": {"comment":"Past this block, try the next"},
        "6485": {"synopsis":"Moves a block of bytes up in memory, top byte first, after checking the new top is below the\nstack with room to spare ($196C; error code 12, report OM, if not).\nEntry: BC = the block's top byte, HL = where it goes, DE = the block's bottom byte.\nExit: HL = DE, BC = where DE's byte went.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"Room for HL? (else report OM)"},
        "6488": {"synopsis":"The move without the check: BC = the source's top byte, HL = the destination's top. Called\nfrom $2982 and $2FC5.","comment":"Swap: HL = the source, BC = the destination"},
        "6491": {"synopsis":"Copies (HL) to (BC) and steps both down, until the byte at DE has been copied.","comment":"Is this the bottom byte?"},
        "6493": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "6494": {"comment":"The bottom byte done: return"},
        "6499": {"synopsis":"Checks there is room for 2 x C more bytes: the end of the variables at $40FD plus 2 x C must\nstay 58 bytes below the stack; stops with error code 12 (report OM) if not.\nEntry: C = the number of words wanted.\nExit: HL kept.\nChanges: AF, B.\nThe LD A,$E5 at $196B hides a PUSH HL at $196C: the entry for an address already in HL\n(MOVE_UP_CHECKED, $2776, $2C6A).","comment":"Keep HL"},
        "6500": {"comment":"HL = the end of the variables ($40FD)"},
        "6503": {"comment":"Plus 2 x C"},
        "6507": {"comment":"Hides PUSH HL at $196C: the entry with HL given"},
        "6509": {"comment":"HL = $FFC6 - HL, so HL + SP is SP - HL - 58"},
        "6516": {"comment":"HL above $FFC6: no room at all"},
        "6519": {"comment":"Does SP - 58 stay above HL? (carry if so)"},
        "6521": {"comment":"Room: return"},
        "6522": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 12 (report OM). Also taken by MEM_TOP_SET ($00EC) and CLEAR ($1E8D,\n$1E98).","comment":"E = 12: OM"},
        "6526": {"synopsis":"The statement loop has reached the program's end (a line link of 0). In direct mode (line\n$FFFF) or with no error trap running it ends like END (PROGRAM_STOPS); while an error trap's\nline is running ($40F2 non-zero) it stops with error code 34 (report NR).","comment":"The current line"},
        "6531": {"comment":"$FFFF: a direct command?"},
        "6534": {"comment":"An error trap running?"},
        "6538": {"comment":"E = 34: NR"},
        "6542": {"synopsis":"Ends the run without a report: on at PROGRAM_STOPS with Z.","comment":"Z: no Break report"},
        "6545": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 2 (report SN) in the DATA statement's line: READ's bad item ($2183)\nmakes the line kept at $40DA (by $22AA, the DATA line READ is in) the current one first.","comment":"The line of the DATA being read"},
        "6548": {"comment":"Report the error there"},
        "6551": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 2 (report SN).\nObserved: A=1) as a direct command came here and printed ?SN ERROR.","comment":"E = 2: SN"},
        "6553": {"synopsis":"Three LD BC,nn here each hide an LD E,n one byte in, so each is an error entry that falls\nthrough the rest to ERROR_E: $199A E = 20 (report /0; from $08A5, $0DE9, $1401), $199D E = 0\n(report NF; NEXT at $22C2), $19A0 E = 36 (report RW; RESUME at $1FB4).","comment":"Hides LD E,20 at $199A: /0"},
        "6556": {"comment":"Hides LD E,0 at $199D: NF"},
        "6559": {"comment":"Hides LD E,36 at $19A0: RW"},
        "6562": {"synopsis":"Stops with the error code in E: E / 2 picks the two letters from the table at ERROR_CODES\n(NF, SN, RG, ...). Copies the word at $40A2 to $40EA and $40EC and resets the stack through\nRESET_SP_HL, continuing at ERROR_REPORT.\nEntry: E = the error code.\nObserved: PRINT ( came here with E = $28 and printed ?MO ERROR, entry 20 of the table.\nDoes not return.","comment":"The line the error is in"},
        "6568": {"comment":"Also the line for \".\" ($1E53)"},
        "6571": {"synopsis":"ERROR_E without taking the current line as the error's line: the ERROR statement's path at\n$1F92 comes here with E = the last error code.","comment":"Continue at ERROR_REPORT"},
        "6574": {"synopsis":"Resets the stack to the statement's saved SP ($40E8) through RESET_SP_HL and goes on at BC.\nNMI_RESTART comes here with BC = $1A18.","comment":"The stack as at the statement's start"},
        "6577": {"comment":"Reset the stack, go on at BC"},
        "6580": {"synopsis":"ERROR_E continues here on the reset stack:\n1. Drops the 0 RESET_SP_HL pushed; keeps E at $409A, the text pointer of the failing\n   statement ($40E6) at $40EE.\n2. In a program line, keeps the line and text pointer at $40F5/$40F7 (for CONT).\n3. With an error trap set ($40F0 non-zero) and none running, marks one running ($40F2 = $FF)\n   and runs the trap's line (RUN_LINE_AT_HL).\n4. Else ERROR_PRINT.","comment":"Drop the 0 below the reset stack"},
        "6581": {"comment":"C = the error code, for later"},
        "6583": {"comment":"$409A = the last error code"},
        "6586": {"comment":"The failing statement's text"},
        "6589": {"comment":"Kept at $40EE (read by RESUME, RESUME_AT_ERROR)"},
        "6593": {"comment":"The error's line"},
        "6596": {"comment":"$FFFF, a direct command?"},
        "6601": {"comment":"$40F5 = the line, for CONT"},
        "6605": {"comment":"$40F7 = its text pointer"},
        "6608": {"synopsis":"Is there an error trap to run? $40F0 holds its line's address, 0 for none.","comment":"The error trap line's address"},
        "6611": {"comment":"Zero: no trap"},
        "6614": {"comment":"$40F2: non-zero while a trap runs"},
        "6619": {"comment":"A trap already running?"},
        "6622": {"comment":"$40F2 = $FF: the trap runs"},
        "6624": {"comment":"Run the trap's line"},
        "6627": {"synopsis":"Prints the report: a new line if the display's column is not 0, then \"?\", the two letters\nfrom ERROR_CODES and \" Error\", then \" in \" and the line unless in direct mode, and goes to\nBASIC_READY. An error while the line is $FFFE (the power-on question) restarts at BOOT\ninstead. Calls the RAM hook at $41A6 (a RET at power-on) with HL = ERROR_CODES.","comment":"No trap running now"},
        "6629": {"comment":"E = the error code"},
        "6630": {"comment":"New line if not at column 0"},
        "6636": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41A6, a RET at power-on"},
        "6639": {"comment":"DE = the code (A = 0 from NEWLINE_IF_COL)"},
        "6640": {"comment":"\"?\""},
        "6645": {"comment":"HL = the report's two letters"},
        "6650": {"comment":"The second letter"},
        "6654": {"comment":"\" Error\", and the line under it"},
        "6662": {"synopsis":"Prints the text at HL, then \" in \" and the line on the stack unless it is $FFFF, and goes on\nto BASIC_READY. STOP's \"Break\" comes here ($1DDE)."},
        "6666": {"comment":"Line $FFFE, the power-on question?"},
        "6670": {"comment":"Yes: restart the machine"},
        "6673": {"comment":"Not a direct command: \" in \" and the line"},
        "6679": {"comment":"Hides POP BC at $1A18: the entry that drops a word"},
        "6681": {"synopsis":"BASIC's main loop, after power-on, an error, a stop and the end of a program:\n1. Output back to the display (OUT_TO_DISPLAY), the RAM hook at $41AC (a RET at power-on),\n   the recorder's motor off (TAPE_STOP), a new line if needed, and READY_CR_TEXT.\n2. After error code 2 (SN) in a program line, EDIT_AFTER_SN (part of EDIT) opens that line\n   for editing.\nThen COMMAND_LOOP. The POP BC hidden at $1A18 is the entry for callers that leave a word on\nthe stack ($1DE1, $1EFC, $2B54, $2C90, NMI_RESTART).","comment":"Output to the display"},
        "6684": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41AC, a RET at power-on"},
        "6687": {"comment":"The recorder's motor off"},
        "6690": {"comment":"New line if not at column 0"},
        "6693": {"comment":"\"READY\" and a new line"},
        "6699": {"comment":"Was the last error SN?"},
        "6704": {"comment":"Yes: edit the failing line"},
        "6707": {"synopsis":"Reads and acts on one line typed at the keyboard. The current line becomes $FFFF (direct\nmode). With AUTO on ($40E1 non-zero) it prints the next line number and a \"*\" if that line\nexists, else a space, and reads the line; a BREAK turns AUTO off (AUTO_OFF). Otherwise\nCOMMAND_PROMPT.","comment":"Line $FFFF: direct mode"},
        "6713": {"comment":"AUTO on?"},
        "6719": {"comment":"The next AUTO line number"},
        "6723": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "6728": {"comment":"Does that line exist?"},
        "6731": {"comment":"\"*\": it does"},
        "6735": {"comment":"\" \": it does not"},
        "6737": {"synopsis":"Prints the mark after the AUTO number and reads the line."},
        "6740": {"comment":"Read the line (carry: BREAK)"},
        "6744": {"comment":"Not BREAK: work out the next number"},
        "6746": {"synopsis":"Turns AUTO off ($40E1 = 0) and starts again at BASIC_READY."},
        "6752": {"synopsis":"Steps the AUTO number by the increment at $40E4 and keeps it at $40E2; past 65529 turns AUTO\noff. Then ENTER_BUFFER passes the line typed to LINE_ENTERED with its number in DE.","comment":"The AUTO increment"},
        "6755": {"comment":"Past 65535: off"},
        "6759": {"comment":"65529 or more: off"},
        "6766": {"comment":"$40E2 = the next AUTO number"},
        "6769": {"comment":"NZ: a numbered line"},
        "6774": {"synopsis":"Prints \">\", reads a line; BREAK or an empty line asks again. A line starting with a digit\nhas its number read into DE (READ_LINE_NUM; 0 if none) and one space after it passed over;\nthen LINE_ENTERED.","comment":"\">\""},
        "6779": {"comment":"Read the line"},
        "6782": {"comment":"BREAK: again"},
        "6785": {"comment":"The first character (carry: a digit)"},
        "6788": {"comment":"Empty: again"},
        "6791": {"comment":"Keep the digit flag"},
        "6792": {"comment":"DE = the line number, if any"},
        "6795": {"synopsis":"Steps back over the spaces before the text the number read stopped at.","comment":"Back over spaces"},
        "6802": {"comment":"One space after the number?"},
        "6808": {"synopsis":"A line from the keyboard: tokenizes it (TOKENIZE_LINE), calls the RAM hook at $41B2 (a RET\nat power-on), then runs it as a direct command (EXEC_STATEMENT, the stack's flags NC) or\nstores it as a program line (carry: DE = its number).\nStoring: deletes a line with the same number (CLOSE_GAP), then, unless the new line is\nempty, opens a gap at its place (MOVE_UP_CHECKED), writes a link, the number and the tokens,\nrelinks the program (RELINK_FROM_DE), clears the variables (CLEAR_ALL) and goes back to\nCOMMAND_LOOP. Hooks at $41B5 and $41B8 (RETs at power-on) surround the clear.","comment":"Keep the line number"},
        "6809": {"comment":"Tokenize; BC = the stored length"},
        "6814": {"comment":"$40E6 = the tokenized text"},
        "6817": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41B2, a RET at power-on"},
        "6820": {"comment":"No line number: run it now"},
        "6825": {"comment":"$40DD = 0"},
        "6829": {"comment":"Anything after the number? (Z: empty)"},
        "6833": {"comment":"$40EC = the line number"},
        "6837": {"comment":"Find the line: BC = its place, carry if it exists"},
        "6841": {"comment":"It exists: delete it"},
        "6847": {"comment":"Empty line: only the deletion"},
        "6850": {"comment":"The program's end"},
        "6853": {"comment":"HL = the new line's length"},
        "6855": {"comment":"The new end"},
        "6857": {"comment":"Open the gap at the line's place"},
        "6861": {"comment":"$40F9 = the new end of the program"},
        "6865": {"comment":"A non-zero link, fixed by the relink"},
        "6870": {"comment":"The line number"},
        "6875": {"comment":"The tokens, from the buffer - 2"},
        "6881": {"synopsis":"Copies the tokenized text into the program up to and with its 0."},
        "6888": {"synopsis":"Relinks from the line's place, clears the variables and goes back to COMMAND_LOOP."},
        "6889": {"comment":"Relink from the changed line"},
        "6892": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41B5, a RET at power-on"},
        "6895": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "6898": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41B8, a RET at power-on"},
        "6904": {"synopsis":"Rewrites every line's link from the program's start ($40A4). No call to it in this ROM;\nfalls into RELINK_FROM_DE."},
        "6908": {"synopsis":"Rewrites the link of each line from DE on so that it holds the next line's address, found by\npassing over the line's number and text to its 0; stops at a link of 0, the end.\nEntry: DE = a line's address.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"HL = the line"},
        "6910": {"comment":"A link of 0: the end"},
        "6914": {"comment":"Past the link and the number"},
        "6918": {"synopsis":"Steps over the line's text to the byte after its 0."},
        "6922": {"comment":"HL = the line, DE = the next one"},
        "6923": {"comment":"Store the link"},
        "6928": {"synopsis":"Reads a line range for LIST ($2B2F) and DELETE (DELETE_CMD): nothing, n, n-, -m or n-m (\".\"\nfor the current line, LINE_NUM_OR_DOT). A missing start is 0, a missing end 65530; anything\nelse is error code 2 (report SN). Then finds the start's line (FIND_LINE).\nExit: as FIND_LINE for the start; the end's number is left on the stack under the return\naddress.","comment":"Start 0"},
        "6932": {"comment":"Nothing: the whole program"},
        "6935": {"comment":"DE = the first number"},
        "6939": {"comment":"Only n?"},
        "6941": {"comment":"Else a \"-\" must follow"},
        "6943": {"synopsis":"Reads the end's number, or takes 65530 when the statement ends here.","comment":"End 65530"},
        "6946": {"comment":"DE = the second number"},
        "6949": {"comment":"More text: report SN"},
        "6952": {"synopsis":"Leaves the end on the stack under the return address and finds the start."},
        "6954": {"synopsis":"Swaps HL with the word on the stack, keeps it there under the return address and finds line\nDE (FIND_LINE). Used by ON ERROR GOTO's path at $1F7A."},
        "6956": {"synopsis":"Finds a program line by its number, from the program's start ($40A4).\nEntry: DE = the line number.\nExit: carry and Z if found, BC = the line's address, HL = the next line's; NC if not, BC =\nthe first line with a higher number or the end (link 0), where a new line goes.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"From the program's start"},
        "6959": {"synopsis":"Looks at one line: its address into BC, then its link and number."},
        "6961": {"comment":"A link of 0: the end (NC, Z)"},
        "6968": {"comment":"HL = this line's number"},
        "6972": {"comment":"Compare it with DE"},
        "6973": {"comment":"HL = the next line"},
        "6979": {"comment":"Equal: carry and Z"},
        "6981": {"comment":"Higher than DE: NC, NZ"},
        "6985": {"synopsis":"The NEW statement: anything after it is left to the statement loop (report SN). Clears the\nscreen (CLS_CMD), turns TRON and AUTO off, empties the program (a zero link at its start,\nthe end two bytes on) and falls into CLEAR_ALL.","comment":"Text after NEW: back for report SN"},
        "6986": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "6989": {"synopsis":"NEW without the check and the clear screen: called by BASIC_INIT ($00F9) and by CLOAD at\n$2C40."},
        "6992": {"comment":"TRON off (A = 0)"},
        "6995": {"comment":"AUTO off"},
        "6998": {"comment":"A link of 0: no lines"},
        "7002": {"comment":"$40F9 = the program's end"},
        "7005": {"synopsis":"Clears the program's state for RUN and after a line is stored: $40DF = the program's start -\n1, then CLEAR_VARS."},
        "7009": {"synopsis":"Clears the variables with $40DF = HL (from CLEAR at CLEAR_CMD, RUN at $1EA0, $1EA9):\n1. All 26 letters' default types ($4101-$411A) back to 4 (single).\n2. No error trap ($40F0 = 0, $40F2 = 0), nothing to CONT ($40F7 = 0).\n3. The string space's free pointer ($40D6) back to the top of memory ($40B1); the DATA\n   pointer reset (RESTORE_CMD).\n4. $40FB and $40FD (the ends of the variables) = the program's end ($40F9).\n5. The RAM hook at $41BB (a RET at power-on), then RESET_STACK.","comment":"$40DF: given to the caller in HL"},
        "7012": {"comment":"26 letters"},
        "7017": {"synopsis":"Sets each letter's type byte to 4: single precision."},
        "7022": {"comment":"No error trap running"},
        "7028": {"comment":"No error trap set"},
        "7031": {"comment":"Nothing to CONT"},
        "7034": {"comment":"The top of memory"},
        "7037": {"comment":"The string space's free pointer"},
        "7040": {"comment":"DATA from the start"},
        "7043": {"comment":"No variables: they start at the program's end"},
        "7052": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41BB, a RET at power-on"},
        "7055": {"synopsis":"Resets the stack to the top of the stack area ($40A0) and keeps that less 2 at $40E8, the\nstatement's SP; returns to its caller through RESET_SP_HL. BASIC_INIT calls it ($00AF).","comment":"BC = the return address"},
        "7056": {"comment":"The stack's top"},
        "7061": {"comment":"$40E8 = the statement's SP"},
        "7066": {"synopsis":"SP = HL; then resets the temporary strings ($40B3 = $40B5), sends output back to the display\n(OUT_TO_DISPLAY, PRINT_END) and $40DC = 0, pushes a 0 (the bottom of FIND_FOR_BLOCK's\nsearch) and goes to BC with HL = $40DF."},
        "7067": {"comment":"No temporary strings"},
        "7073": {"comment":"Output to the display"},
        "7085": {"comment":"A 0 word: the stack's bottom mark"},
        "7086": {"comment":"Return to BC"},
        "7087": {"comment":"With HL = $40DF"},
        "7091": {"synopsis":"Prints \"? \" and reads a line from the keyboard into the line buffer (LINE_INPUT).\nExit: carry set if the line was ended with BREAK (SCF at LI_BREAK); HL = the byte before the\nbuffer.","comment":"\"?\""},
        "7096": {"comment":"\" \""},
        "7104": {"synopsis":"Turns a typed line into tokens, in place: the tokens are written from the line buffer - 2\n($40A7), behind the text being read.\n1. Spaces, digits, \":\" and \";\" are kept; \"?\" becomes PRINT ($B2).\n2. Anything else is looked up in the keyword table from KEYWORDS (KEYWORD_SEARCH); lower\n   case letters are made upper case.\n3. Text in quotes, after REM, and after DATA up to a \":\" is copied as it is ($40B0 non-zero\n   for DATA).\n4. ELSE gets a \":\" before it; \"'\" becomes \":\", REM and the $FB token, and the rest of the\n   line is copied.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: BC = the tokens' length + 5 (a program line's size), HL = the buffer - 3, three zeros\nafter the tokens.","comment":"$40B0 = 0: not in DATA"},
        "7108": {"comment":"C = 0 bytes stored"},
        "7110": {"comment":"DE = the buffer - 2: where tokens go"},
        "7116": {"synopsis":"Takes the next character of the text.","comment":"A space: keep it"},
        "7123": {"comment":"A quote: copy the string"},
        "7128": {"comment":"The end of the line"},
        "7132": {"comment":"In DATA: keep everything"},
        "7140": {"comment":"\"?\": the PRINT token"},
        "7147": {"comment":"Below \"0\": look it up"},
        "7152": {"comment":"Digits, \":\" and \";\": keep them"},
        "7157": {"synopsis":"Looks the text at HL up in the keyword table (KEYWORDS on, each word's first letter with bit\n7 set, $80 at the end); B counts the token from $80. Returns to KEYWORD_DONE with C = the\ntoken, or C = the character if no word matches."},
        "7158": {"comment":"One before the keyword table"},
        "7162": {"comment":"Return to KEYWORD_DONE"},
        "7166": {"comment":"B = $7F: one before the first token"},
        "7169": {"comment":"Lower case? Make it upper"},
        "7179": {"comment":"And in the text"},
        "7180": {"synopsis":"Tries the next keyword against the text's character in C.","comment":"C = the character"},
        "7181": {"comment":"HL = the table"},
        "7182": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next byte with bit 7 set: the start of the next keyword."},
        "7187": {"comment":"Its token"},
        "7189": {"comment":"$80: the table's end, no match"},
        "7192": {"comment":"The first letter matches?"},
        "7197": {"synopsis":"Compares the rest of the keyword with the text, letter by letter."},
        "7198": {"comment":"Bit 7: the next keyword starts, all matched"},
        "7204": {"comment":"GOTO? Spaces in the text are passed over"},
        "7211": {"synopsis":"The text's next character, made upper case."},
        "7219": {"synopsis":"Same letter: go on; else back to the text's start and the next keyword."},
        "7222": {"comment":"No: the text from its start again"},
        "7225": {"synopsis":"A whole keyword matched: C = its token, HL past it in the text."},
        "7229": {"synopsis":"Stores the search's result: ELSE gets a \":\" first, \"'\" becomes \":\" REM $FB and its line's\nrest; everything else goes to TOKEN_STORE."},
        "7230": {"comment":"A = the token or the character"},
        "7234": {"comment":"ELSE?"},
        "7236": {"comment":"A \":\" kept only before ELSE"},
        "7242": {"synopsis":"Is it the apostrophe, $FB?","comment":"\"'\"?"},
        "7246": {"comment":"\":\" and REM before it"},
        "7254": {"comment":"Two more bytes stored"},
        "7258": {"synopsis":"HL back to the text, then TOKEN_STORE."},
        "7259": {"synopsis":"Stores A, counts it, and sets the DATA flag: \":\" clears $40B0, DATA sets it; after REM the\nrest of the line is copied (COPY_LITERAL).","comment":"Store the byte, count it"},
        "7263": {"comment":"\":\": DATA's text ends"},
        "7267": {"comment":"DATA ($88): its text is kept"},
        "7271": {"synopsis":"$40B0 = 0 after \":\", $4E after DATA."},
        "7274": {"synopsis":"REM ($93)? The rest of the line is copied; else TOKENIZE_LOOP."},
        "7280": {"synopsis":"Copies the text up to the byte in B (the closing quote) or the line's end.","comment":"The end of the line?"},
        "7284": {"comment":"The closing byte: store it as usual"},
        "7287": {"synopsis":"Copies one character as it is."},
        "7293": {"synopsis":"The line's end: BC = the bytes stored + 5, three zeros after the tokens, HL = the buffer -\n3.","comment":"Link, number and the 0: 5 more"},
        "7300": {"comment":"HL = the buffer - 3, before the tokens"},
        "7306": {"comment":"Three zeros: the end and an empty link"},
        "7312": {"synopsis":"Compares HL with DE, high bytes first.\nExit: Z set if HL = DE; carry set if HL < DE.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"The high bytes differ?"},
        "7318": {"synopsis":"Checks the text: the byte at (HL) must be the one after the RST $08 that called this. Steps\nthe return address past that byte; if they match, goes on at NEXT_CHAR (the next character\nafter it), else stops with error code 2 (SYNTAX_ERROR).\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: as NEXT_CHAR.","comment":"A = the character in the text"},
        "7319": {"comment":"HL = the return address, the expected byte"},
        "7321": {"comment":"Return past the expected byte"},
        "7323": {"comment":"A match: on to the next character"},
        "7326": {"comment":"No: stop with report SN"},
        "7329": {"synopsis":"The FOR statement:\n1. Does the assignment (LET), drops an older FOR block of the same variable and all above\n   it.\n2. Checks room for 16 bytes (CHECK_STACK_ROOM), finds the statement's end (DATA_CMD).\n3. TO and the limit; a string or double variable stops with error code 24 (report TM).\n   STEP's value or 1, as an integer for an integer variable, else single.\n4. Pushes a 17-byte block: the end of the statement, the line number, the limit, the step,\n   the step's sign and the type, the variable's address ($40DF) and $81 on top, then goes on\n   at NEXT_STATEMENT.","comment":"$40DC = $64"},
        "7334": {"comment":"The assignment"},
        "7337": {"comment":"Text kept, HL = the return address"},
        "7338": {"comment":"An older block of this variable?"},
        "7342": {"comment":"No"},
        "7344": {"comment":"Drop it and everything above it"},
        "7346": {"comment":"$40E8 = the statement's SP"},
        "7349": {"synopsis":"Builds the new block on the stack."},
        "7350": {"comment":"Room for 8 words"},
        "7356": {"comment":"HL = the statement's end"},
        "7359": {"comment":"The end into the block"},
        "7361": {"comment":"The line number into it"},
        "7367": {"comment":"The variable's type"},
        "7368": {"comment":"String: report TM"},
        "7371": {"comment":"Double: report TM"},
        "7375": {"comment":"The limit's value"},
        "7380": {"comment":"Not integer: single"},
        "7383": {"comment":"Limit as an integer"},
        "7387": {"comment":"Step 1"},
        "7391": {"comment":"STEP?"},
        "7393": {"comment":"DE = STEP's integer"},
        "7399": {"comment":"A = the step's sign"},
        "7404": {"synopsis":"The single-precision loop: limit and step as single numbers.","comment":"The limit as a single"},
        "7407": {"comment":"BCDE = the limit"},
        "7413": {"comment":"BCDE = 1.0, the step without STEP"},
        "7419": {"comment":"STEP?"},
        "7421": {"comment":"Sign +1"},
        "7425": {"comment":"STEP's value"},
        "7435": {"comment":"A = its sign"},
        "7438": {"synopsis":"Text pointer back into HL."},
        "7439": {"synopsis":"Pushes the step, the sign and type, and the variable's address.","comment":"The step"},
        "7441": {"comment":"C = the sign"},
        "7442": {"comment":"B = the type - 3"},
        "7446": {"comment":"The variable's address, from LET"},
        "7450": {"synopsis":"Pushes the single byte $81 that starts a FOR block and falls into NEXT_STATEMENT; NEXT comes\nhere ($2321) to put a block back.","comment":"$81: the FOR token"},
        "7453": {"comment":"Only B stays on the stack"},
        "7454": {"synopsis":"The statement loop; every statement returns here. It reads the keyboard (KEY_GET; a key goes\nto KEY_PRESSED, BREAK stops), keeps the text pointer at $40E6 and SP at $40E8, then:\n- \":\": the next statement in the line (EXEC_STATEMENT);\n- the line's 0: the next line by its link; a link of 0 is END_OF_PROGRAM. The line's number\n  goes to $40A2; with TRON on ($411B) it prints \"<n>\";\n- anything else: error code 2 (report SN).","comment":"A key pressed?"},
        "7458": {"comment":"Yes: pause or BREAK"},
        "7461": {"comment":"$40E6 = the text pointer"},
        "7464": {"comment":"$40E8 = the statement's SP"},
        "7468": {"comment":"\":\": the next statement"},
        "7473": {"comment":"Not the line's end: report SN"},
        "7477": {"comment":"The next line's link"},
        "7478": {"synopsis":"Starts the line at HL (its link): a link of 0 is END_OF_PROGRAM, else its number becomes the\ncurrent line and its text runs. The error trap enters here ($19E0)."},
        "7480": {"comment":"A link of 0: the program's end"},
        "7485": {"comment":"DE = the line's number"},
        "7489": {"comment":"$40A2 = the current line"},
        "7492": {"comment":"TRON on?"},
        "7499": {"comment":"\"<\""},
        "7504": {"comment":"The line number"},
        "7507": {"comment":"\">\""},
        "7513": {"synopsis":"HL = the byte before the line's text."},
        "7514": {"synopsis":"Runs the statement after HL: pushes NEXT_STATEMENT as its return address, then\n- an empty statement returns at once;\n- a character below $80 is an assignment (LET, LET_CMD);\n- a token $80-$BB jumps through STMT_TABLE with the next character read (NEXT_CHAR);\n- a token from $BC on goes to STMT_HI_TOKEN: MID$ ($FA) to the RAM hook at $41D9, else\n  report SN.","comment":"The statement's first character"},
        "7515": {"comment":"Return to NEXT_STATEMENT"},
        "7519": {"synopsis":"EXEC_STATEMENT with A and Z already from NEXT_CHAR (IF's THEN at $2053)."},
        "7520": {"synopsis":"EXEC_STATEMENT for the token in A (ON ... GOTO/GOSUB at $1FA4).","comment":"Below $80: an assignment"},
        "7525": {"comment":"Not a statement token"},
        "7530": {"comment":"Twice the token: a word's offset"},
        "7535": {"comment":"STMT_TABLE"},
        "7542": {"comment":"The statement's routine, run by the RET"},
        "7543": {"comment":"Read the next character first"},
        "7544": {"synopsis":"Steps HL to the next character of the text and reads it, passing over spaces and the codes\n$09 and $0A.\nExit: A = the character; Z set for \":\" or $00 (end of statement or line); carry set for a\ndigit \"0\"-\"9\"; HL = its address.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"\":\" or above: not a digit (NC), Z if \":\""},
        "7549": {"comment":"A space? Pass over it"},
        "7554": {"comment":"$09 or $0A? Pass over it"},
        "7563": {"comment":"Carry for \"0\"-\"9\"","synopsis":"Sets the flags for the character: carry for a digit, Z for $00."},
        "7566": {"comment":"Z for $00, keeping the carry"},
        "7569": {"synopsis":"The RESTORE statement: the DATA pointer ($40FF) back to the program's start - 1. Keeps HL\nand changes DE."},
        "7570": {"comment":"The program's start - 1"},
        "7574": {"synopsis":"$40FF = HL, the DATA pointer, keeping DE; READ uses it at $2274."},
        "7579": {"synopsis":"Reads the keyboard (KEY_GET) and returns if no key is down; else KEY_PRESSED. LIST calls it\nbetween lines ($2B47)."},
        "7584": {"synopsis":"A key in A: $60 (SHIFT and the first key of row 0) waits for another key (KEY_WAIT_GET),\nwhich is taken instead; the key goes to $4099. BREAK ($01) stops the program like STOP\n(STOP_OR_END with NZ); any other key returns.","comment":"$60: pause until a key"},
        "7589": {"comment":"$4099 = the key"},
        "7592": {"comment":"BREAK ($01)?"},
        "7593": {"synopsis":"The STOP statement: anything after it is left for report SN; else STOP_OR_END with NZ.","comment":"Text after STOP: back for report SN"},
        "7594": {"comment":"NZ: report Break"},
        "7598": {"synopsis":"The END statement: anything after it is left for report SN; calls the RAM hook at $41BB (a\nRET at power-on) and goes on at STOP_OR_END with Z.","comment":"Text after END: back for report SN"},
        "7600": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41BB, a RET at power-on"},
        "7604": {"synopsis":"Stops the program: keeps the text pointer at $40E6, resets the temporary strings, drops the\nreturn to the statement loop and goes on at PROGRAM_STOPS. NZ prints \"Break\", Z nothing. The\nLD HL,$FFF6 at $1DBD hides an OR $FF at $1DBE (HL is loaded again at once): the entry that\nstops with NZ and \"Break\", taken by BREAK at INPUT's prompt ($21E0, $2221, after\nPROMPT_LINE) and at $1FEE.","comment":"$40E6 = where it stopped"},
        "7607": {"comment":"No temporary strings"},
        "7613": {"comment":"Hides OR $FF at $1DBE: the entry that stops with NZ"},
        "7616": {"comment":"Drop the return to NEXT_STATEMENT"},
        "7617": {"synopsis":"In a program line, keeps the line and the text pointer at $40F5/$40F7 for CONT; output back\nto the display, a new line if needed; then \"Break in n\" (NZ, through REPORT_TAIL) or\nstraight to BASIC_READY (Z).","comment":"The current line"},
        "7622": {"comment":"$FFFF: a direct command?"},
        "7627": {"comment":"$40F5 = the line, for CONT"},
        "7630": {"comment":"$40F7 = the text pointer"},
        "7636": {"synopsis":"Prints \"Break in n\" for NZ, or nothing for Z, and goes to BASIC_READY.","comment":"Output to the display"},
        "7639": {"comment":"New line if not at column 0"},
        "7643": {"comment":"BREAK_TEXT"},
        "7646": {"comment":"NZ: \"Break\" and the line"},
        "7649": {"comment":"Z: drop the line, READY"},
        "7652": {"synopsis":"The CONT statement: stops with error code 32 (report CN) if there is nothing to continue\n($40F7 = 0); else the line kept at $40F5 becomes the current one and the statement loop goes\non at the text pointer kept at $40F7.","comment":"Where to continue"},
        "7657": {"comment":"E = 32: CN"},
        "7663": {"comment":"The line it stopped in"},
        "7670": {"comment":"On at the kept text"},
        "7671": {"synopsis":"The TRON statement: $411B = $AF, so each line's number is printed as \"<n>\" when it starts\n(RUN_LINE_AT_HL). The LD A,$AF hides a XOR A at $1DF8, the TROFF statement's entry: $411B =\n0. NEW calls $1DF8 too.","comment":"Hides XOR A at $1DF8: TROFF"},
        "7673": {"comment":"$411B: the trace flag"},
        "7677": {"comment":"No call to it in this ROM"},
        "7680": {"synopsis":"The DEFSTR statement: E = 3 (string), then DEF_TYPE_LETTERS.\nThe table's DEFINT, DEFSNG and DEFDBL entries ($1E03, $1E06, $1E09) are LD E,2 / LD E,4 / LD\nE,8 hidden one byte inside the LD BC,nn instructions that follow; each LD BC is harmless for\nthe entries above it.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"E = 3, the string type"},
        "7682": {"synopsis":"Holds the DEFINT entry ($1E03, LD E,2) one byte in.","comment":"LD E,2 at $1E03 (DEFINT)"},
        "7685": {"synopsis":"Holds the DEFSNG entry ($1E06, LD E,4) one byte in.","comment":"LD E,4 at $1E06 (DEFSNG)"},
        "7688": {"synopsis":"Holds the DEFDBL entry ($1E09, LD E,8) one byte in.","comment":"LD E,8 at $1E09 (DEFDBL)"},
        "7691": {"synopsis":"Reads one letter or a letter range (\"A-F\") and writes the type in E into the default type\ntable at $4101 (one byte per letter A-Z) for each letter in it; a comma repeats. Anything\nthat is not a letter, or a range that runs backwards, stops with error code 2 (report SN)\nthrough the SYNTAX_ERROR address pushed first.\nEntry: HL = the text, E = the type (2, 3, 4 or 8).","comment":"A letter?"},
        "7694": {"comment":"A return to SYNTAX_ERROR for the RET Cs"},
        "7698": {"comment":"Not a letter: report SN"},
        "7699": {"comment":"B = C = the first letter's index, 0-25"},
        "7703": {"comment":"The next character: a \"-\" token for a range?"},
        "7708": {"comment":"The range's last letter"},
        "7713": {"comment":"B = its index"},
        "7717": {"synopsis":"A = the number of letters in the range less one; carry (a backward range) returns to\nSYNTAX_ERROR.","comment":"A = last - first"},
        "7719": {"comment":"Backwards: report SN"},
        "7720": {"comment":"A = the number of letters"},
        "7721": {"comment":"Drop the SYNTAX_ERROR return, keep the text"},
        "7722": {"comment":"HL = the type byte of the first letter"},
        "7728": {"synopsis":"DEF_TYPE_LETTERS's loop writing E into A bytes of the type table.","comment":"The letter's type"},
        "7733": {"comment":"The text back"},
        "7734": {"comment":"Another range after a comma?"},
        "7738": {"comment":"Past the comma, the next range"},
        "7741": {"synopsis":"Tests whether the byte at HL is a capital letter.\nExit: A = the byte; carry clear for \"A\"-\"Z\", set otherwise.\nChanges: AF."},
        "7742": {"comment":"Below \"A\"? Carry"},
        "7745": {"comment":"Above \"Z\"? Carry after the CCF"},
        "7749": {"synopsis":"NEXT_CHAR, then EVAL_INT_POS."},
        "7750": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression as an integer that must not be negative (EVAL_INT).\nExit: DE = the value; HL = the text after it.\nStops with error code 8 (report FC) when the value is negative.","comment":"DE = the expression as an integer, S from D"},
        "7753": {"comment":"Not negative: done"},
        "7754": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 8 (report FC). Many statements and functions jump here.","comment":"E = 8: FC"},
        "7759": {"synopsis":"Reads a line number from the text, or \".\", which stands for the line number at $40EC (the\nline an error stopped in, or the one last entered).\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: DE = the line number; A and the flags as NEXT_CHAR after it.","comment":"A \".\" for the current line?"},
        "7762": {"comment":"DE = the line number at $40EC (flags kept)"},
        "7767": {"comment":"\".\": past it and return as NEXT_CHAR"},
        "7770": {"synopsis":"Reads a decimal line number from the text into DE, from the byte at HL: steps HL back one\nand goes on at READ_NUM_HERE.\nEntry: HL = the first digit.\nExit: DE = the number (0 if no digit); HL and A at the first byte that is not a digit, carry\nclear, flags as NEXT_CHAR.\nStops with error code 2 (report SN) for a number above 65529.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"Back one, for the NEXT_CHAR below"},
        "7771": {"synopsis":"READ_LINE_NUM without the step back: the first digit is the byte after HL.","comment":"DE = 0 so far"},
        "7774": {"synopsis":"READ_LINE_NUM's loop: one digit, DE = DE x 10 + the digit.","comment":"The next character: a digit (carry)?"},
        "7775": {"comment":"Not a digit: done"},
        "7778": {"comment":"Is DE already 6552 or more?"},
        "7782": {"comment":"Then x 10 overflows: report SN"},
        "7785": {"comment":"HL = DE x 10: x2, x4, x5, x10"},
        "7791": {"comment":"The digit's value"},
        "7797": {"comment":"Add it"},
        "7799": {"comment":"The text back; next digit"},
        "7802": {"synopsis":"The CLEAR statement. Without an argument it clears the variables (CLEAR_VARS). With one, n,\nthe string space becomes n bytes below the top of memory ($40B1): the new bottom goes to\n$40A0 and the variables are cleared.\nStops with error code 12 (report OM) when n is larger than the top, or the new bottom is not\nmore than 40 bytes above the program's end ($40F9).\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword, Z set when nothing follows.","comment":"No argument: just clear the variables"},
        "7805": {"comment":"DE = the string space's size"},
        "7808": {"comment":"Anything after it? Return for the loop to report"},
        "7812": {"comment":"DE = the top of memory - the size"},
        "7821": {"comment":"Larger than the top: report OM"},
        "7824": {"comment":"HL = the program's end + 40"},
        "7831": {"comment":"Is the new bottom not above it? Report OM"},
        "7835": {"comment":"$40A0 = the string space's new bottom"},
        "7840": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "7843": {"synopsis":"The RUN statement. Without an argument it clears the variables and runs from the first line\n(CLEAR_ALL). With a line number it calls the RAM hook at $41C7 (a RET at power-on), clears\nthe variables (CLEAR_VARS) and goes to the line through GOTO_PUSH_BC, returning to the\nstatement loop at NEXT_STATEMENT.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword, Z set when nothing follows.","comment":"No line number: clear and run from the start"},
        "7846": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41C7, a RET at power-on"},
        "7849": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "7852": {"comment":"Return to the statement loop"},
        "7857": {"synopsis":"The GOSUB statement: checks for 6 bytes of stack (CHECK_STACK_ROOM), then puts a GOSUB frame\non the stack below the statement loop's return: the text address, the current line number\n($40A2) and a single byte $91 (the GOSUB token as the frame's mark). Then GOTO_PUSH_BC.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"Room for 6 bytes of stack?"},
        "7862": {"comment":"BC = the return to the statement loop"},
        "7863": {"comment":"The text address, into the frame"},
        "7865": {"comment":"The current line number, into the frame"},
        "7869": {"comment":"The frame's mark: $91, the GOSUB token"},
        "7872": {"comment":"One byte only"},
        "7873": {"synopsis":"Puts BC back as the return address and goes on at GOTO_CMD."},
        "7874": {"synopsis":"The GOTO statement: reads the line number (READ_LINE_NUM) and goes on at GOTO_LINE_DE.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"DE = the line number"},
        "7877": {"synopsis":"Continues the program at line DE. Skips to the end of the current line (the REM entry at\n$1F07); when DE is above the current line ($40A2) it searches from the next line\n(FIND_LINE_LOOP), else from the program's start (FIND_LINE).\nEntry: DE = the line number, HL = the text.\nExit: HL = the byte before the line found (its link follows), for the statement loop. Stops\nwith error code 14 (report UL) when there is no such line.","comment":"HL = the end of the current line"},
        "7881": {"comment":"Is the target above the current line?"},
        "7886": {"comment":"HL = the next line"},
        "7887": {"comment":"Above: search from the next line"},
        "7890": {"comment":"Else (or not found) from the program's start"},
        "7893": {"comment":"HL = the byte before the line found"},
        "7896": {"comment":"Found: on with the statement loop"},
        "7897": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 14 (report UL).","comment":"E = $0E: UL"},
        "7902": {"synopsis":"The RETURN statement. Passes over any FOR frames on the stack (FIND_FOR_BLOCK with D = $FF)\nand expects a GOSUB frame (mark $91); restores its line number to $40A2 and its text\naddress, then skips the rest of the GOSUB statement (DATA_CMD).\nA GOSUB from a direct command (line $FFFF) with $40DD not 0 goes back to the prompt at\n$1A18.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword; Z set when nothing follows.\nStops with error code 4 (report RG) when there is no GOSUB frame.","comment":"Text after RETURN? Back for the loop to report"},
        "7903": {"comment":"D = $FF matches no FOR variable: pass them all"},
        "7908": {"comment":"Drop the frames passed"},
        "7909": {"comment":"$40E8, the stack the statement loop keeps"},
        "7912": {"comment":"A GOSUB frame ($91)?"},
        "7916": {"comment":"No: report RG"},
        "7919": {"comment":"The GOSUB's line number"},
        "7923": {"comment":"Was it $FFFF, a direct command?"},
        "7928": {"comment":"A direct GOSUB with $40DD set: back to the prompt"},
        "7935": {"synopsis":"Puts the statement loop (NEXT_STATEMENT) back as the return address, takes the GOSUB's text\naddress into HL and skips the rest of its statement. The LD A,$E1 hides a POP HL at $1F04,\nthe entry INPUT takes for an empty answer.","comment":"The statement loop as the return"},
        "7938": {"comment":"HL = the GOSUB's text address"},
        "7939": {"synopsis":"Holds the entry $1F04 (POP HL) one byte in; from $1F03 itself A becomes $E1, unused.","comment":"A = $E1 (unused); $1F04 is a POP HL"},
        "7941": {"synopsis":"The DATA statement, and the routine that skips to the end of a statement: steps HL to the\nnext \":\" or the line's closing $00, ignoring a \":\" between quotes, and counts in D the IF\ntokens it passes.\nThe REM and ELSE entry ($1F07) is LD C,0 hidden in the LD BC,$0E3A: it ends only at $00.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL at the \":\" or $00, A = that byte (Z set).\nChanges: AF, BC, D, HL.","comment":"C = \":\", the statement's end; REM's C = 0 at $1F07"},
        "7945": {"comment":"B = 0 before the swap"},
        "7947": {"synopsis":"Swaps the end character in B with C: at the start and at each quote, so that within a string\nonly $00 ends the scan.","comment":"B = the end character, C = the other"},
        "7950": {"synopsis":"The scan's loop: one byte, stopping at $00 or the end character.","comment":"The line's end?"},
        "7953": {"comment":"The end character?"},
        "7956": {"comment":"A quote: swap, only $00 ends a string"},
        "7960": {"comment":"An IF token?"},
        "7964": {"comment":"D + 1 when B is not 0 (not REM, not in a string)"},
        "7969": {"synopsis":"The LET statement, and any statement that is not a keyword: finds the variable named at HL\n($260D, its address in DE), expects \"=\", keeps the address at $40DF and evaluates the\nexpression (EVAL_EXPR); then ASSIGN_VALUE.\nEntry: HL = the variable's name.","comment":"DE = the variable's address"},
        "7972": {"comment":"Expect \"=\""},
        "7975": {"comment":"$40DF = the variable's address"},
        "7980": {"comment":"The variable's type flags, kept"},
        "7982": {"comment":"The value"},
        "7986": {"comment":"HL = the variable's address, the text kept"},
        "7987": {"synopsis":"Stores the value in the accumulator ($4121) into the variable: converts it to the variable's\ntype (CONVERT_TYPE). A string whose text lies below the program ($40A4) or in the string\nspace is copied there (STR_COPY) first; one in the program text is kept where it is. INPUT\nand READ come here with the type and the variable's address on the stack.\nEntry: A = the variable's type less 3; HL = the variable's address; the text on the stack.\nExit: HL = the text, DE restored.","comment":"A = the variable's type"},
        "7989": {"comment":"Convert the value to that type"},
        "7995": {"comment":"A number: store it"},
        "7998": {"comment":"HL = the string's descriptor"},
        "8002": {"comment":"DE = the string's text address"},
        "8006": {"comment":"Below the program's start ($40A4)?"},
        "8010": {"comment":"Then copy it"},
        "8012": {"comment":"Below the string space's bottom ($40A0)?"},
        "8016": {"comment":"DE = the descriptor"},
        "8017": {"comment":"Then keep it where it is"},
        "8019": {"comment":"Against the program's end ($40F9)"},
        "8025": {"synopsis":"Holds the entry $1F5A (POP DE) one byte in: the copy path for a string below the program\ntakes the descriptor there.","comment":"A = $D1 (unused); $1F5A is a POP DE"},
        "8030": {"comment":"Copy the string into the string space"},
        "8034": {"synopsis":"Releases the string (TEMP_POP) and swaps the descriptor's address onto the stack."},
        "8037": {"comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "8038": {"synopsis":"Copies the value into the variable (COPY_DE_TO_HL, $40AF bytes) and returns.","comment":"Copy $40AF bytes of value into the variable"},
        "8042": {"comment":"The text back"},
        "8044": {"synopsis":"The ON statement. ON ERROR GOTO n stores the address of line n at $40F0 (the handler an\nerror goes to); ON ERROR GOTO 0 clears it, and inside a handler ($40F2 not 0) stops with the\nerror that is pending ($409A). Any other ON goes on at ON_GO_CMD.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword, A = its first character.\nStops with error code 14 (report UL) when line n does not exist.","comment":"ON ERROR?"},
        "8048": {"comment":"Past ERROR; expect GOTO"},
        "8051": {"comment":"DE = the handler's line number"},
        "8054": {"comment":"GOTO 0?"},
        "8058": {"comment":"Find the line: BC = its address, carry if found"},
        "8061": {"comment":"DE = the line's address"},
        "8064": {"comment":"Not there: report UL"},
        "8067": {"synopsis":"Stores DE at $40F0; with a line found (carry) that is all."},
        "8068": {"comment":"$40F0 = the handler (0 for none)"},
        "8072": {"comment":"A line set: done"},
        "8073": {"comment":"GOTO 0 inside a handler?"},
        "8078": {"comment":"Then stop with the pending error"},
        "8085": {"synopsis":"ON n GOTO / ON n GOSUB: evaluates n (0-255, EVAL_BYTE), then counts through the list of line\nnumbers after the keyword and runs GOTO or GOSUB (through STMT_TOKEN) at the n-th. When the\nlist is shorter than n, or n is 0, the statement does nothing.","comment":"E = n"},
        "8088": {"comment":"B = GOTO or GOSUB"},
        "8090": {"comment":"GOSUB?"},
        "8094": {"comment":"Else expect GOTO"},
        "8096": {"comment":"Back to the token, for the dispatch"},
        "8097": {"synopsis":"C = n, the number of line numbers to pass."},
        "8098": {"synopsis":"ON_GO_CMD's loop: the n-th number reached? Otherwise passes one number and its comma.","comment":"Count one number"},
        "8099": {"comment":"The n-th: run the GOTO or GOSUB at it"},
        "8103": {"comment":"Pass one line number"},
        "8106": {"comment":"A comma: another number"},
        "8111": {"synopsis":"The RESUME statement, in an error handler only ($40F2 not 0): clears $40F2 and the error\ncode at $409A. RESUME n goes to line n (GOTO_LINE_DE); RESUME and RESUME 0 run the statement\nthat stopped again (RESUME_AT_ERROR); RESUME NEXT runs the one after it.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.\nStops with error code 36 (report RW) outside a handler.","comment":"$40F2: inside an error handler?"},
        "8116": {"comment":"No: report RW"},
        "8119": {"comment":"A = 0: clear the error code and $40F2"},
        "8124": {"comment":"RESUME NEXT?"},
        "8129": {"comment":"DE = the line number, if one"},
        "8132": {"comment":"Text after it: return for the loop to report"},
        "8133": {"comment":"A line: go to it"},
        "8138": {"comment":"RESUME or RESUME 0: Z clear"},
        "8141": {"synopsis":"RESUME NEXT: nothing may follow; Z stays set for RESUME_AT_ERROR.","comment":"Past NEXT: text after it returns"},
        "8143": {"synopsis":"Back to the statement that stopped: its line ($40EA) to $40A2 and its text address ($40EE)\nto HL. With Z clear (RESUME or RESUME 0) the statement loop runs it again; with Z set\n(RESUME NEXT) the statement is passed over first.","comment":"DE = the stopped statement's text"},
        "8147": {"comment":"Its line becomes current"},
        "8153": {"comment":"HL = its text, DE = its line"},
        "8154": {"comment":"RESUME: run it again"},
        "8155": {"comment":"At the line's end?"},
        "8159": {"comment":"Past the next line's link and number"},
        "8163": {"synopsis":"Steps past the statement; from a direct command with $40DD - 1 = 0 the run stops ($1DBE),\nelse it skips to the next statement (DATA_CMD)."},
        "8164": {"comment":"Was it a direct command ($FFFF)?"},
        "8167": {"comment":"No: skip to the next statement"},
        "8170": {"comment":"$40DD = 1: stop"},
        "8180": {"synopsis":"The ERROR statement: evaluates n (1-255, EVAL_BYTE) and stops with error code 2 x (n - 1),\nthe same codes BASIC's own errors use; n above 23 gives error code 38 (report UE).\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.\nStops with error code 8 (report FC) for n = 0.","comment":"A = E = n"},
        "8183": {"comment":"Text after it: return for the loop to report"},
        "8184": {"comment":"n = 0: report FC"},
        "8188": {"comment":"E = 2 x (n - 1)"},
        "8191": {"comment":"A code BASIC uses (below $2D)?"},
        "8195": {"comment":"Else E = $26: UE"},
        "8197": {"synopsis":"Goes on at ERROR_E with the code in E."},
        "8200": {"synopsis":"The AUTO statement: turns on automatic line numbers at the prompt. AUTO s,i starts at s (or\n\".\", LINE_NUM_OR_DOT) in steps of i; s and i default to 10, and \"s,\" keeps the step last\nused. The next number goes to $40E2, the step to $40E4, and $40E1 is set not 0; then back to\nthe prompt at COMMAND_LOOP.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword, Z set when nothing follows.\nStops with error code 8 (report FC) for a step of 0, error code 2 (report SN) for text after\nthe step.","comment":"The step defaults to 10"},
        "8204": {"comment":"No arguments: start at 10, step 10"},
        "8206": {"comment":"DE = the start (or \".\")"},
        "8210": {"comment":"Start on the stack, HL = 10"},
        "8211": {"comment":"Nothing more: step 10"},
        "8214": {"comment":"Expect \",\""},
        "8217": {"comment":"DE = the step last used"},
        "8221": {"comment":"Nothing after the comma: keep it"},
        "8223": {"comment":"DE = the new step"},
        "8226": {"comment":"Text after it: report SN"},
        "8229": {"synopsis":"HL = the step (from DE)."},
        "8230": {"synopsis":"Stores the step and the start, sets the AUTO flag and goes back to the prompt.","comment":"A step of 0?"},
        "8232": {"comment":"Report FC"},
        "8235": {"comment":"$40E4 = the step"},
        "8238": {"comment":"$40E1 not 0: AUTO is on"},
        "8242": {"comment":"$40E2 = the next line number"},
        "8245": {"comment":"Drop the statement loop's return"},
        "8246": {"comment":"To the prompt"},
        "8249": {"synopsis":"The IF statement: evaluates the condition and passes over a \",\" and a THEN. For a true (not\nzero) condition goes on at IF_TRUE; for a false one at IF_FALSE.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"The condition"},
        "8252": {"comment":"Pass a \",\""},
        "8258": {"comment":"Pass THEN"},
        "8265": {"comment":"Is it zero?"},
        "8269": {"comment":"False"},
        "8271": {"synopsis":"Runs what follows THEN or ELSE: a line number goes to that line (GOTO_CMD), anything else is\nrun as a statement (STMT_DISPATCH).","comment":"A digit: a line number to go to"},
        "8275": {"comment":"Else run the statement"},
        "8278": {"synopsis":"The condition is false: looks for the ELSE that belongs to this IF (D counts the IFs met in\nbetween); without one the rest of the line is passed over.","comment":"D = 1: the ELSE of this IF"},
        "8280": {"synopsis":"IF_FALSE's loop: to the end of the next statement; the line's end ends the IF.","comment":"To the statement's end"},
        "8283": {"comment":"The line's end: nothing to run"},
        "8285": {"comment":"The next statement's first token: ELSE?"},
        "8290": {"comment":"This IF's ELSE?"},
        "8295": {"synopsis":"The LPRINT statement: $409C = 1 (output to the printer), then the PRINT statement's item\nloop (PRINT_LOOP).","comment":"Output to the printer"},
        "8303": {"synopsis":"The PRINT statement. After the RAM hook at $41CA (a RET at power-on):\n1. PRINT @ n, (n below 1024) puts the display's cursor ($4020) at $3C00 + n and the column\n   ($40A6) at n AND 63; it stops with error code 8 (report FC) for a larger n.\n2. PRINT # sends the tape's leader and sync (TAPE_WRITE_LEAD) and sets $409C = $80 (output\n   to tape).\n3. PRINT_LOOP prints the items.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword, A = its first character.","comment":"The RAM hook at $41CA, a RET at power-on"},
        "8306": {"comment":"PRINT @?"},
        "8310": {"comment":"DE = the position"},
        "8313": {"comment":"1024 or more? Report FC"},
        "8319": {"comment":"The cursor = $3C00 + the position"},
        "8326": {"comment":"The column = the position AND 63"},
        "8333": {"comment":"Expect \",\""},
        "8335": {"synopsis":"A \"#\"? Then output goes to tape."},
        "8339": {"comment":"Write the tape's leader and sync"},
        "8342": {"comment":"Output to tape"},
        "8347": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next character of the PRINT text: at the statement's end it prints a new line\n(PRINT_NEWLINE) and finishes (PRINT_END).","comment":"Back one for NEXT_CHAR"},
        "8349": {"comment":"The statement's end: a new line"},
        "8352": {"synopsis":"One PRINT item: USING goes to USING_PRINT, TAB( to PRINT_TAB, \",\" to PRINT_COMMA, \";\" to\nPRINT_NEXT; anything else is evaluated (EVAL_EXPR) and printed. A number is turned into text\n(NUM_TO_TEXT, STR_FROM_TEXT), calls the RAM hook at $41CD (a RET at power-on), starts a new\nline when it would not fit (on the display, column + length against $409D; on the printer,\ncolumn $409B + length against 132) and is printed with a space after it.\nEntry: Z clear (not the statement's end).","comment":"The end: finish"},
        "8355": {"comment":"USING?"},
        "8360": {"comment":"TAB(?"},
        "8366": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "8371": {"comment":"A semicolon?"},
        "8376": {"comment":"Drop the text kept"},
        "8377": {"comment":"Evaluate the item"},
        "8381": {"comment":"A string?"},
        "8384": {"comment":"The number as text"},
        "8387": {"comment":"As a string"},
        "8390": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41CD, a RET at power-on"},
        "8393": {"comment":"HL = the string's descriptor, length first"},
        "8396": {"comment":"The output: tape (minus), display (0) or printer"},
        "8400": {"comment":"Tape: no line width"},
        "8405": {"comment":"The printer's column + the length "},
        "8409": {"comment":"... against 132"},
        "8413": {"synopsis":"On the display: column ($40A6) + the text's length against the width at $409D.","comment":"B = the display's width ($409D)"},
        "8417": {"comment":"The column + the length"},
        "8422": {"synopsis":"A new line first when the text does not fit.","comment":"Does not fit: a new line first"},
        "8425": {"synopsis":"Prints the number's text (PRINT_STRING) and a space.","comment":"Print the number's text"},
        "8428": {"comment":"A space after the number"},
        "8433": {"comment":"Not zero: skip the next CALL"},
        "8434": {"synopsis":"Prints a string (PRINT_STRING) and goes on with the next item.","comment":"A string: print it"},
        "8437": {"comment":"The text back; the next item"},
        "8441": {"synopsis":"Starts a new line unless the display's column ($40A6) is already 0.\nExit: A = 0, Z set.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"The display's column"},
        "8444": {"comment":"Already 0: no new line"},
        "8446": {"synopsis":"Writes a NEW LINE ($0D) to the current output (OUT_CHAR), then calls the RAM hook at $41D0\n(a RET at power-on).\nExit: A = 0, Z set.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"NEW LINE"},
        "8451": {"synopsis":"The tail of PRINT_NEWLINE: the RAM hook at $41D0, then A = 0.","comment":"The RAM hook at $41D0, a RET at power-on"},
        "8454": {"comment":"A = 0, Z set"},
        "8456": {"synopsis":"A \",\" in PRINT, after the RAM hook at $41D3 (a RET at power-on): on tape it writes the comma\nitself; on the display or the printer it moves on to the next 16-column zone, or to a new\nline past the last zone (column $409E on the display, 112 on the printer).","comment":"The RAM hook at $41D3, a RET at power-on"},
        "8459": {"comment":"Tape (minus)?"},
        "8466": {"comment":"Tape: write the comma itself"},
        "8473": {"synopsis":"Display (Z) or printer: compare the column with the last zone's start.","comment":"The display?"},
        "8475": {"comment":"The printer's column against 112"},
        "8483": {"synopsis":"The display: B = the last zone's column ($409E), A = the column.","comment":"B = the display's last zone ($409E)"},
        "8487": {"comment":"A = the display's column"},
        "8491": {"synopsis":"Past the last zone: a new line and nothing more.","comment":"Past the last zone: a new line"},
        "8494": {"comment":"And no spaces"},
        "8496": {"synopsis":"A = the column MOD 16 less 16; its complement is the spaces to the next zone less 1.","comment":"The column MOD 16, less 16"},
        "8500": {"comment":"Spaces to the zone's start, less 1"},
        "8503": {"synopsis":"TAB(n) in PRINT: n (0-255, NEXT_EVAL_BYTE) AND 63 is the column to move to; spaces are\nprinted up to it unless the column is already there or beyond. On tape it stops with error\ncode 8 (report FC).","comment":"E = n AND 63, the column wanted"},
        "8509": {"comment":"Expect \")\""},
        "8511": {"comment":"Back one, for PRINT_NEXT's NEXT_CHAR"},
        "8513": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41D3, a RET at power-on"},
        "8516": {"comment":"Tape? Report FC"},
        "8523": {"comment":"The display?"},
        "8526": {"comment":"The printer's column"},
        "8531": {"synopsis":"The display's column.","comment":"The display's column"},
        "8534": {"synopsis":"A = n - column - 1; nothing to do when the column is past n.","comment":"A = n - column - 1"},
        "8536": {"comment":"Already there or past it: no spaces"},
        "8538": {"synopsis":"Prints A + 1 spaces.","comment":"B = the spaces"},
        "8540": {"comment":"A space"},
        "8542": {"synopsis":"The spaces' loop."},
        "8548": {"synopsis":"Takes the text back, steps past the separator and goes on with the next item.","comment":"The text back; past the separator"},
        "8550": {"comment":"The next item"},
        "8553": {"synopsis":"The end of a PRINT (and of INPUT): ends a tape record (TAPE_STOP) when the output was the\ntape, puts the output back to the display ($409C = 0) and calls the RAM hook at $41BE (a RET\nat power-on).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Was the output the tape?"},
        "8557": {"comment":"Then end the record"},
        "8560": {"comment":"Output back to the display"},
        "8564": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41BE, a RET at power-on"},
        "8568": {"synopsis":"\"?REDO\", a NEW LINE and $00: printed by INPUT_BAD_ITEM ($2190)."},
        "8575": {"synopsis":"An INPUT or READ item that does not suit its variable:\n- in READ ($40DE not 0): stops with error code 2 (report SN), reported in the DATA line\n  (DATA_SN_ERROR, $40DA);\n- in INPUT #: stops with error code 42 (report FD);\n- in INPUT: prints REDO_TEXT and runs the INPUT statement again from its start ($40E6).","comment":"READ?"},
        "8579": {"comment":"Report SN in the DATA line"},
        "8582": {"comment":"INPUT #?"},
        "8586": {"comment":"E = $2A: FD"},
        "8591": {"comment":"Drop the return"},
        "8592": {"comment":"Print \"?REDO\""},
        "8598": {"comment":"HL = the INPUT statement's start: ask again"},
        "8602": {"synopsis":"The INPUT statement, not allowed as a direct command (DIRECT_CHECK: error code 22, report\nID). After the RAM hook at $41D6 (a RET at power-on), $40A9 = the first character less \"#\",\nso 0 for INPUT #.\n1. INPUT #: reads a tape record (TAPE_FIND_SYNC, then bytes through TAPE_READ_BYTE) into the\n   line buffer ($40A7) up to a NEW LINE or 250 bytes, ends it with $00 and stops the tape\n   (TAPE_STOP).\n2. INPUT: prints a quoted prompt and its \";\" if there is one (INPUT_PROMPT), then \"? \" and\n   reads a line (INPUT_ASK).\n3. Both give their variables values from the buffer, as READ does from DATA (INPUT_START).\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"A direct command? Report ID"},
        "8605": {"comment":"The first character"},
        "8606": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41D6, a RET at power-on"},
        "8609": {"comment":"$40A9 = 0 for INPUT #"},
        "8615": {"comment":"Not #: the keyboard"},
        "8617": {"comment":"Read the tape's leader and sync"},
        "8621": {"comment":"At most 250 bytes"},
        "8623": {"comment":"Into the line buffer"},
        "8626": {"synopsis":"INPUT #'s loop: one byte from tape into the buffer, up to $0D or B bytes.","comment":"A byte from tape"},
        "8631": {"comment":"NEW LINE: the record's end"},
        "8637": {"synopsis":"Ends the buffer's text with $00 over the NEW LINE, stops the tape.","comment":"$00 over the NEW LINE"},
        "8640": {"comment":"Stop the tape"},
        "8643": {"comment":"HL = the byte before the buffer"},
        "8649": {"synopsis":"A quoted prompt? Prints it (STR_LITERAL, PRINT_STRING) and expects \";\" after it; either way\nreturns to INPUT_ASK.","comment":"INPUT_ASK as the return"},
        "8653": {"comment":"A quoted prompt?"},
        "8656": {"comment":"The prompt as a string"},
        "8659": {"comment":"Expect \";\""},
        "8662": {"comment":"Print the prompt"},
        "8667": {"synopsis":"Prints \"? \" and reads a line (PROMPT_LINE). BREAK stops the program ($1DBE); an empty line\nleaves the variables as they are and skips the rest of the statement ($1F04)."},
        "8668": {"comment":"\"? \" and a line from the keyboard"},
        "8671": {"comment":"BC = the statement text"},
        "8672": {"comment":"BREAK: stop"},
        "8675": {"comment":"An empty line?"},
        "8680": {"comment":"Yes: keep the values, skip the statement"},
        "8683": {"synopsis":"Writes a \",\" over the byte before the buffer, so the items are read from there; $40DE = 0\n(INPUT) through the XOR A at $21F4.","comment":"A \",\" before the items"},
        "8687": {"synopsis":"The READ statement: the items come from the DATA pointer ($40FF) and $40DE is set not 0\n(READ). The OR $AF hides an XOR A at $21F4: INPUT_START's entry, which sets $40DE = 0. Then\nfor each variable named: ITEM_VAR.\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword.","comment":"The statement text kept"},
        "8688": {"comment":"HL = the DATA pointer"},
        "8691": {"synopsis":"Holds the entry $21F4 (XOR A) one byte in: from $21F3 A is set not 0 (READ), from $21F4 it\nis 0 (INPUT).","comment":"A not 0: READ ($21F4 is XOR A: INPUT)"},
        "8693": {"comment":"$40DE: 0 for INPUT, not 0 for READ"},
        "8696": {"comment":"The item pointer kept, HL = the text"},
        "8699": {"synopsis":"A \",\" before the next variable of INPUT or READ.","comment":"Expect \",\" between the variables"},
        "8701": {"synopsis":"Finds the variable ($260D) and takes the next item for it: after a \",\" in the items it is\nread at once (ITEM_TAKE). Otherwise READ looks for the next DATA statement (READ_FIND_DATA)\nand INPUT asks again with \"??\" (PROMPT_LINE); INPUT # has run out and stops with error code\n6 (report OD).\nEntry: HL = the statement text; the item pointer on the stack.","comment":"DE = the variable's address"},
        "8704": {"comment":"HL = the item pointer, the text kept"},
        "8705": {"comment":"The variable's address kept"},
        "8706": {"comment":"A \",\": another item follows"},
        "8711": {"comment":"READ: look for the next DATA"},
        "8718": {"comment":"INPUT #: out of data, report OD"},
        "8727": {"comment":"\"?\", then PROMPT_LINE's \"? \""},
        "8735": {"comment":"DE = the variable, BC = the text"},
        "8737": {"comment":"BREAK: stop"},
        "8740": {"comment":"An empty line?"},
        "8745": {"comment":"Yes: skip the statement"},
        "8749": {"synopsis":"Reads one item for the variable whose address is on the stack, after the RAM hook at $41DC\n(a RET at power-on): a string (ITEM_STRING) or a number (ITEM_NUMBER), stored through\nASSIGN_VALUE, which returns to ITEM_AFTER.","comment":"The RAM hook at $41DC, a RET at power-on"},
        "8752": {"comment":"The variable's type: a string?"},
        "8754": {"comment":"A number"},
        "8756": {"comment":"The item's first character"},
        "8757": {"comment":"Quoted: it ends at the closing quote"},
        "8763": {"comment":"Else at \":\" or \",\""},
        "8767": {"comment":"Back one: the first character belongs to it"},
        "8768": {"synopsis":"Makes a string of the item (STR_DELIMITED): between quotes, or up to a \":\" or \",\".","comment":"The item as a string"},
        "8771": {"comment":"The type back"},
        "8772": {"comment":"DE = the item pointer"},
        "8773": {"comment":"ITEM_AFTER as the return"},
        "8777": {"comment":"Store it through LET's tail"},
        "8781": {"synopsis":"Reads a number from the item text: $0E6C for an integer or single variable (carry from\nTYPE_FLAGS), TEXT_TO_DBL for a double; returns to $2243 to store it.","comment":"The item's first character"},
        "8784": {"comment":"Return to $2243 to store it"},
        "8788": {"comment":"Integer or single: read a number"},
        "8791": {"comment":"Double: read a number"},
        "8794": {"synopsis":"After an item: the item text must end or go on with \",\"; anything else is a bad item\n(INPUT_BAD_ITEM).","comment":"Back one for NEXT_CHAR"},
        "8796": {"comment":"The item's end?"},
        "8798": {"comment":"Or a comma?"},
        "8800": {"comment":"Else a bad item"},
        "8803": {"synopsis":"More variables in the statement? Back to ITEM_NEXT_VAR. Then five NOPs (a gap), and: READ\nkeeps the item pointer as the DATA pointer (SET_DATA_PTR); INPUT prints EXTRA_TEXT when the\nanswer held more items than variables, then PRINT_END.","comment":"HL = the text, the item pointer kept"},
        "8805": {"comment":"Another variable?"},
        "8809": {"comment":"DE = the item pointer"},
        "8810": {"comment":"Five NOPs"},
        "8815": {"comment":"READ?"},
        "8820": {"comment":"READ: DE becomes the DATA pointer"},
        "8824": {"comment":"The RAM hook at $41DF, a RET at power-on"},
        "8827": {"comment":"Items left over?"},
        "8828": {"comment":"Print \"?Extra ignored\""},
        "8834": {"comment":"The text back; finish as PRINT does"},
        "8838": {"synopsis":"\"?Extra ignored\", a NEW LINE and $00: printed by INPUT when the answer held more items than\nvariables ($227C)."},
        "8854": {"synopsis":"READ's search for the next DATA statement: passes over statements (DATA_CMD) and, at each\nline's end, keeps the next line's number at $40DA (for the report of a bad item). Stops with\nerror code 6 (report OD) at the end of the program.","comment":"To the statement's end"},
        "8857": {"comment":"A \":\": the next statement"},
        "8860": {"comment":"The next line's link: the end of the program?"},
        "8864": {"comment":"Report OD"},
        "8869": {"comment":"$40DA = the line's number"},
        "8878": {"synopsis":"The statement's first token: DATA? Its items are read (ITEM_TAKE).","comment":"The statement's first token"},
        "8879": {"comment":"DATA?"},
        "8883": {"comment":"Read the item"},
        "8886": {"synopsis":"The NEXT statement: finds the FOR frame of the variable named (or the innermost frame when\nnone is, DE = 0) through FIND_FOR_BLOCK, dropping the frames above it. Adds the STEP to the\nvariable and compares it with the limit; while the loop goes on, the FOR's line and text\nbecome current again (FOR_BLOCK_TOKEN, which keeps the frame). When it ends the frame is\ndropped and, after a \",\", the next variable is handled the same way (NEXT_VAR_DE).\nEntry: HL = the text after the keyword; Z set when nothing follows.\nStops with error code 0 (report NF) when there is no such FOR frame.","comment":"DE = 0: the innermost FOR"},
        "8889": {"synopsis":"NEXT for the variable at DE (0 for any): the text kept at $40DF, the frame searched.","comment":"A variable named: DE = its address"},
        "8892": {"comment":"Keep the text at $40DF"},
        "8895": {"comment":"HL = its FOR frame"},
        "8898": {"comment":"None: report NF"},
        "8901": {"comment":"Drop the frames above it"},
        "8906": {"comment":"The STEP's sign, kept for $2314"},
        "8910": {"comment":"The loop's kind: minus for an integer loop"},
        "8916": {"comment":"The STEP into the accumulator"},
        "8921": {"comment":"Add the variable"},
        "8925": {"comment":"Store the result in the variable"},
        "8929": {"comment":"The limit into the registers"},
        "8933": {"comment":"Compare with the limit"},
        "8938": {"synopsis":"An integer loop: adds the integer STEP (INT_ADD); a result outside the integer range stops\nwith error code 10 (report OV, OVERFLOW_ERR). Then compares with the limit (INT_CMP).","comment":"Past the frame's single STEP"},
        "8942": {"comment":"BC = the integer STEP"},
        "8946": {"comment":"DE = the variable's value"},
        "8953": {"comment":"Add the STEP"},
        "8956": {"comment":"Did the result leave the integer range?"},
        "8961": {"comment":"Report OV"},
        "8964": {"comment":"Store the result in the variable"},
        "8971": {"comment":"DE = the limit"},
        "8976": {"comment":"Compare with the limit"},
        "8979": {"synopsis":"The comparison against the step's sign: Z means the loop has ended.","comment":"B = the STEP's sign"},
        "8981": {"comment":"Ended when the comparison matches the sign"},
        "8982": {"comment":"The FOR's line and text from the frame"},
        "8985": {"comment":"Ended?"},
        "8987": {"comment":"The FOR's line becomes current"},
        "8991": {"comment":"HL = the text after the FOR"},
        "8993": {"comment":"Keep the frame and go on there"},
        "8996": {"synopsis":"The loop has ended: the frame is dropped; a \",\" in the NEXT goes on with the next variable.\nNever returns: the CALL's address is dropped with the stack.","comment":"Drop the frame"},
        "9000": {"comment":"The NEXT's text"},
        "9003": {"comment":"A \",\": another variable?"},
        "9006": {"comment":"No: the next statement"},
        "9009": {"comment":"Past the comma"},
        "9010": {"comment":"NEXT for that variable; never returns here"},
        "9013": {"synopsis":"Expects \"(\", then EVAL_EXPR.","comment":"Expect \"(\""},
        "9015": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression from the text into the accumulator ($4121, type at $40AF): steps HL\nback one and starts at precedence 0. The routine every statement and function uses.\n1. EVAL_PREC_D reads an operand (EVAL_OPERAND) and keeps the text address at $40F3.\n2. EVAL_OPERATOR collects relational operators (\">\", \"=\", \"<\") as a mask in D and arithmetic\n   and logic operators (tokens $CD-$D3) with their precedence from OP_PRECEDENCE.\n3. An operator of higher precedence than the one on its left pushes the left value and the\n   routine to apply, evaluates its right operand at that precedence and applies it; one that\n   is not higher returns, for the operator on its left to be applied first.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL = the text after the expression; the value in the accumulator.\nChanges: all.","comment":"Back one for NEXT_CHAR"},
        "9016": {"synopsis":"EVAL_EXPR without the step back.","comment":"Precedence 0"},
        "9018": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression whose operators must be of higher precedence than D: keeps D on the\nstack, checks the stack has room (CHECK_STACK_ROOM) and reads one operand (EVAL_OPERAND).","comment":"The precedence on the left, kept"},
        "9019": {"comment":"Room for 2 bytes of stack?"},
        "9024": {"comment":"One operand"},
        "9027": {"comment":"$40F3 = the text after it"},
        "9030": {"synopsis":"The operator loop: HL from $40F3, B = the precedence on the left; D = 0 for the relation\nmask."},
        "9033": {"comment":"B = the precedence on the left"},
        "9034": {"comment":"No relation yet"},
        "9037": {"synopsis":"Gathers \">\" (1), \"=\" (2) and \"<\" (4) into the mask D; the same one twice stops with error\ncode 2 (report SN). $40D8 keeps the operator's address.","comment":"\">\", \"=\" or \"<\" (tokens $D4-$D6)?"},
        "9045": {"comment":"A = 1, 2 or 4"},
        "9048": {"comment":"Already in the mask? Carry"},
        "9051": {"comment":"Twice: report SN"},
        "9054": {"comment":"$40D8 = the operator's address"},
        "9057": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "9060": {"synopsis":"No relation: an arithmetic or logic operator (+ - * / [ AND OR, index 0-6 in E), or the\nexpression ends here. \"+\" with a string on the left joins strings (STR_CONCAT); otherwise\nthe precedence from the table at OP_PRECEDENCE is taken, and when it is not above B this\nreturns. Power ($7F) goes to EVAL_POWER, AND and OR (below $51) to EVAL_LOGIC.","comment":"Any relation?"},
        "9065": {"comment":"The operator's address at $40D8"},
        "9069": {"comment":"Below \"+\": the expression ends"},
        "9072": {"comment":"Above OR: the expression ends"},
        "9075": {"comment":"E = the operator's index"},
        "9076": {"comment":"A string and \"+\"?"},
        "9082": {"comment":"Then join the strings"},
        "9085": {"comment":"D = the operator's precedence"},
        "9089": {"comment":"Not above the one on the left?"},
        "9092": {"comment":"Then return to apply that first"},
        "9093": {"comment":"The left's precedence kept"},
        "9094": {"comment":"Back to the operator loop afterwards"},
        "9098": {"comment":"Power?"},
        "9104": {"comment":"AND or OR?"},
        "9109": {"synopsis":"Pushes the left operand for an arithmetic operator by its type: two bytes ($4121) for an\ninteger, four for a single, eight ($4121, $4123, $411D, $411F) for a double. A string stops\nwith error code 24 (report TM, TYPE_MISMATCH). The LD B,$F1 at $23C3 hides POP AF at $23C4,\nthe single's way out.","comment":"The accumulator"},
        "9113": {"comment":"A string? Report TM"},
        "9122": {"comment":"Its first word"},
        "9126": {"comment":"An integer: nothing more"},
        "9129": {"comment":"Its second word"},
        "9134": {"comment":"A single (odd parity: 1)?"},
        "9139": {"comment":"A double"},
        "9141": {"comment":"Never taken: carry is clear"},
        "9143": {"comment":"Its first four bytes at $411D"},
        "9146": {"synopsis":"Pushes the two remaining words of a double. The JR C to here is never taken: the carry is\nclear from the OR A at $2398.","comment":"Push them"},
        "9155": {"comment":"B = $F1 (unused); $23C4 is a POP AF"},
        "9157": {"synopsis":"Pushes B = the type and C = the operator's index, then the routine ARITH_DISPATCH that\napplies it.","comment":"A = the type"},
        "9159": {"comment":"C = the operator's index, B = the type"},
        "9162": {"comment":"Apply it at ARITH_DISPATCH"},
        "9165": {"synopsis":"Pushes the routine that applies the operator (BC) and evaluates the right operand from the\noperator ($40D8) at precedence D.","comment":"The routine that applies it"},
        "9166": {"comment":"From the operator, the right operand"},
        "9172": {"synopsis":"Power: the left operand as a single (CSNG_FN) is pushed (FP_PUSH); POWER_OP applies it, and\nthe right operand is evaluated at precedence $7F.","comment":"The left operand as a single"},
        "9175": {"comment":"Pushed"},
        "9178": {"comment":"POWER_OP applies the power"},
        "9181": {"comment":"Right operand at precedence $7F"},
        "9185": {"synopsis":"AND and OR: the left operand as an integer (CINT_FN) is pushed; LOGIC_AND_OR applies it."},
        "9186": {"comment":"The left operand as an integer"},
        "9190": {"comment":"Pushed"},
        "9191": {"comment":"LOGIC_AND_OR applies AND or OR"},
        "9196": {"synopsis":"A relation: when the precedence on the left is $64 or more this returns first. Else the mask\nis pushed with RELATION_RESULT (which turns the comparison into a result) and the right side\nis evaluated at precedence $64: numbers through EVAL_PUSH_NUM with operator index 4 (the\ncomparison), strings by pushing the descriptor ($4121) for STR_COMPARE.","comment":"Is the left's precedence $64 or more?"},
        "9199": {"comment":"Then return to apply it first"},
        "9200": {"comment":"The left's precedence and the mask kept"},
        "9202": {"comment":"D = $64, E = 4: the comparison"},
        "9205": {"comment":"RELATION_RESULT makes the result"},
        "9209": {"comment":"Numbers: push the left operand"},
        "9213": {"comment":"A string: push its descriptor"},
        "9217": {"comment":"STR_COMPARE compares strings"},
        "9222": {"synopsis":"Runs a binary arithmetic or comparison operator once its right operand is in the accumulator\n($4121, type at $40AF). The evaluator pushes this address, the left operand and a word with\nB = the left operand's type and C = the operator's index (0 +, 1 -, 2 *, 3 /, 4 compare).\n1. Stores the index at $40B0.\n2. If either side is double, both become double: the right one is copied to $4127, the left\n   one put in the accumulator, and the routine comes from the table at DOUBLE_OP_TABLE.\n3. Else if either side is single, both become single: left in BCDE, right in the\n   accumulator, routine from SINGLE_OP_TABLE.\n4. Else both are integers: DE = left, HL = right, routine from INT_OP_TABLE.\nA string on the right of a number stops with error code 24 (report TM).\nExit: through the chosen routine.","comment":"B = left type, C = operator index"},
        "9224": {"comment":"Operator index kept at $40B0"},
        "9227": {"comment":"Left side double?"},
        "9232": {"comment":"Right side double?"},
        "9240": {"comment":"D = right type"},
        "9241": {"comment":"Left side single?"},
        "9247": {"comment":"Right side a string? Stop with report TM"},
        "9253": {"comment":"Right side single and left integer"},
        "9256": {"comment":"Both integers: routine from the table at INT_OP_TABLE"},
        "9266": {"comment":"DE = left integer"},
        "9267": {"comment":"HL = right integer"},
        "9270": {"comment":"Jump to the routine"},
        "9272": {"synopsis":"The left operand is double: the right one is made double and copied to $4127, then the left\none is taken off the stack into the accumulator.","comment":"Right operand to double"},
        "9275": {"comment":"And copied to $4127"},
        "9278": {"comment":"Left double's low words back to $411D-$4120"},
        "9286": {"synopsis":"Takes a single from the stack into the accumulator, then runs the operator as a double\n(falls into ARITH_DBL_OP).","comment":"Left single from the stack"},
        "9288": {"comment":"Into the accumulator"},
        "9291": {"synopsis":"Makes the accumulator double and runs the double routine of the operator at $40B0 from the\ntable at DOUBLE_OP_TABLE.","comment":"Left operand to double"},
        "9294": {"comment":"The double routines"},
        "9297": {"synopsis":"Jumps to word number ($40B0) of the routine table at HL; BC is kept.","comment":"Operator index times two"},
        "9307": {"comment":"Fetch the routine's address"},
        "9312": {"synopsis":"The right operand is double and the left one is not: the right one goes to $4127 and the\nleft one is fetched back as single or integer, then made double.","comment":"Keep the left type"},
        "9313": {"comment":"Right double to $4127"},
        "9316": {"comment":"A = left type, now the accumulator's type"},
        "9320": {"comment":"Left single? Take it as one"},
        "9324": {"comment":"Left integer back into the accumulator"},
        "9330": {"synopsis":"The left operand is single and the right one integer or single: the right one is made\nsingle, the left one taken into BCDE, and the routine comes from the table at\nSINGLE_OP_TABLE (falls into ARITH_SNG_OP).","comment":"Right operand to single"},
        "9333": {"comment":"BCDE = left single"},
        "9335": {"synopsis":"Runs the single routine of the operator from the table at SINGLE_OP_TABLE.","comment":"The single routines"},
        "9340": {"synopsis":"The left operand is an integer and the right one single: the left one becomes single in\nBCDE, the right one goes back into the accumulator, then ARITH_SNG_OP.","comment":"HL = left integer"},
        "9341": {"comment":"Right single kept on the stack"},
        "9344": {"comment":"Left integer to single"},
        "9347": {"comment":"And into BCDE"},
        "9350": {"comment":"Right single back into the accumulator"},
        "9360": {"synopsis":"The / operator on two integers (the integer table's entry at $18C5): both are made single\nand divided as singles, so the result is single.\nEntry: DE = left (dividend), HL = right (divisor).\nExit: through the single division at FP_DIV_POPPED.","comment":"Keep the divisor"},
        "9361": {"comment":"Dividend to single"},
        "9366": {"comment":"Dividend on the stack"},
        "9369": {"comment":"Divisor to single, then divide"},
        "9375": {"synopsis":"Reads one operand of an expression from the text after HL and leaves its value in the\naccumulator ($4121, type at $40AF).\nThe operand can be a number, a variable, a quoted string, a function, NOT, a unary sign,\nERR, ERL, VARPTR, USR, MEM, POINT, INKEY$, STRING$ or a bracketed expression. TIME$, INSTR,\nFN and '&' go to RAM hooks (JP $012D at power-on). End of statement stops with error code 40\n(report MO).\nEntry: HL just before the operand.\nExit: HL past the operand, value in the accumulator.","comment":"Next character"},
        "9376": {"comment":"E = 40: MO"},
        "9378": {"comment":"Nothing there? Stop with report MO"},
        "9381": {"comment":"A digit: read a number"},
        "9384": {"comment":"A letter: a variable"},
        "9390": {"comment":"Token +: ignored"},
        "9394": {"comment":"A point starts a number"},
        "9399": {"comment":"Token -: negate"},
        "9404": {"comment":"A quote: a string literal"},
        "9409": {"comment":"Token NOT"},
        "9414": {"comment":"'&': the RAM hook at $4194, a JP $012D at power-on"},
        "9419": {"comment":"Token ERR?"},
        "9423": {"comment":"Yes: the value at $409A as an integer"},
        "9433": {"synopsis":"Not ERR: token ERL gives the line number at $40EA as a single.","comment":"Token ERL?"},
        "9439": {"comment":"Unsigned line number to single"},
        "9447": {"synopsis":"Not ERL: token VARPTR gives the address of a variable's value as an integer; a variable that\ndoes not exist stops with error code 8 (report FC).","comment":"Token VARPTR?"},
        "9454": {"comment":"DE = the value's address, 0 if none"},
        "9461": {"comment":"No such variable? Stop with report FC"},
        "9466": {"comment":"The address as an integer"},
        "9471": {"synopsis":"Not VARPTR: USR, INSTR, MEM, TIME$, POINT, INKEY$, STRING$ and FN by token, then the\nfunctions of the table at FN_DISPATCH (SGN on), else a bracketed expression.","comment":"Token USR?"},
        "9476": {"comment":"INSTR: the RAM hook at $419D"},
        "9481": {"comment":"Token MEM?"},
        "9486": {"comment":"TIME$: the RAM hook at $4176"},
        "9491": {"comment":"Token POINT?"},
        "9496": {"comment":"Token INKEY$?"},
        "9501": {"comment":"Token STRING$?"},
        "9506": {"comment":"FN: the RAM hook at $4155"},
        "9511": {"comment":"Token SGN or later: a function"},
        "9516": {"synopsis":"Evaluates '(' expression ')' from the text at HL (through EVAL_PAREN); a missing bracket\nstops with report SN.\nExit: HL past the ')', value in the accumulator."},
        "9519": {"comment":"Must close with ')'"},
        "9522": {"synopsis":"Unary minus: evaluates the operand at precedence $7D (so only ↑ binds tighter) and negates\nit.","comment":"Precedence of unary minus"},
        "9527": {"comment":"Text pointer after the operand"},
        "9531": {"comment":"Negate the accumulator"},
        "9534": {"comment":"Also the return pushed for functions: text back"},
        "9536": {"synopsis":"A variable as operand: finds it (the entry at $260D) and copies its value into the\naccumulator; for a string the accumulator holds the address of its descriptor. A variable\nthat does not exist leaves 0 or the empty string without returning here (VAR_READ_NONE).","comment":"DE = the value's address"},
        "9541": {"comment":"Address in the accumulator"},
        "9544": {"comment":"Not a string: copy the value itself"},
        "9550": {"synopsis":"A function token (SGN, $D7, on): BC = 2 x (token - $D7) is its entry in the table at\nFN_DISPATCH. LEFT$, RIGHT$ and MID$ take '(' string ',' number: the routine gets the number\non the stack above the string's descriptor and the text pointer in DE. The others take one\nbracketed argument (FN_ONE_ARG).","comment":"BC = token offset times two"},
        "9555": {"comment":"Past the token"},
        "9557": {"comment":"Before LEFT$? One argument"},
        "9561": {"comment":"'(' and the string"},
        "9566": {"comment":"Must be a string, else TM"},
        "9570": {"comment":"String descriptor under the table index"},
        "9576": {"comment":"Second argument, 0-255"},
        "9580": {"comment":"Number pushed, HL = table index"},
        "9583": {"synopsis":"A one-argument function: evaluates the bracketed argument and keeps the text pointer on the\nstack; SQR to ATN get their argument as single first (falls into FN_DISPATCH).","comment":"The bracketed argument"},
        "9586": {"comment":"Text kept, HL = table index"},
        "9587": {"comment":"SGN to POS? Argument as it is"},
        "9592": {"comment":"SQR to ATN? Argument to single"},
        "9599": {"synopsis":"Pushes $253E (pop the text pointer, return) and jumps to the function's routine (falls into\nFN_TABLE_JP).","comment":"Return through POP HL / RET"},
        "9603": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the routine whose address is at FN_DISPATCH + HL (the function table)."},
        "9606": {"synopsis":"Jumps through the word at HL + BC.\nEntry: HL = table base plus an offset, BC = the rest of the offset."},
        "9607": {"comment":"Fetch the routine's address"},
        "9612": {"synopsis":"Compares two strings for a relational operator: the left one's descriptor is on the stack,\nthe right one in the accumulator; both temporary strings are freed.\nExit: A = 0 if equal, 1 if left < right, $FF if left > right, carry set when left > right;\nthen the return goes on to RELATION_RESULT.","comment":"HL = right descriptor, freed"},
        "9615": {"comment":"A = right length, BC = its text"},
        "9620": {"comment":"DE = left descriptor"},
        "9623": {"comment":"Free the left string too"},
        "9626": {"comment":"D = right length"},
        "9627": {"comment":"E = left length, BC = left text"},
        "9632": {"comment":"HL = right text"},
        "9633": {"synopsis":"Compares one character pair at a time; the shorter string is the smaller when all its\ncharacters match.","comment":"Both ended? Equal"},
        "9636": {"comment":"Right ended first? Left is greater"},
        "9640": {"comment":"Left ended first? Left is smaller"},
        "9646": {"comment":"Same character?"},
        "9652": {"comment":"Differ: $FF if left greater, else 1"},
        "9656": {"synopsis":"Turns a comparison's result (A = 0, 1 or $FF with carry, as STR_COMPARE leaves it) into 1\nfor '>', 2 for '=' or 4 for '<', tests it against the operators' bits (B, pushed by the\nevaluator) and leaves -1 (true) or 0 (false) as an integer, then goes back to the operator\nloop at EVAL_OPERATOR.","comment":"Into bit 0 '>', bit 1 '=', bit 2 '<'"},
        "9658": {"comment":"B = the relation's bits"},
        "9660": {"comment":"A = $FF if one matches, else 0"},
        "9663": {"comment":"As integer -1 or 0"},
        "9668": {"synopsis":"NOT: evaluates its operand at precedence $5A, makes it an integer and complements every bit;\nthen drops the return into the evaluator and goes straight back to the operator loop at\nEVAL_OPERATOR.","comment":"Precedence of NOT"},
        "9673": {"comment":"HL = the operand as integer"},
        "9676": {"comment":"Complement both bytes"},
        "9685": {"comment":"Drop the return to $2343"},
        "9686": {"synopsis":"Goes on with the operator loop at EVAL_OPERATOR."},
        "9689": {"synopsis":"The flags of the type byte at $40AF. For 8 or more: A = type - 3, carry clear, Z clear.\nBelow 8: A = type - 3 with carry set, so a 2 gives S set, a 3 Z set and a 4 neither.\nExit: A = type - 3, flags as above.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Is the type 8 or more?"},
        "9701": {"synopsis":"A type of 8 or more: A = type - 3 with carry clear."},
        "9705": {"synopsis":"Runs AND or OR once the right operand is in the accumulator: both sides as integers,\ncombined bit by bit.\nEntry: the operator's precedence word, then the left integer, on the stack.\nExit: the result as an integer in the accumulator, through INT_SET_H.","comment":"B = the operator's precedence"},
        "9706": {"comment":"HL = right operand as integer"},
        "9710": {"comment":"DE = left integer"},
        "9711": {"comment":"Return stores HL with H = A"},
        "9715": {"comment":"Precedence $46: OR"},
        "9719": {"comment":"OR the low bytes"},
        "9725": {"synopsis":"AND: the bytes are ANDed; A = the high byte for INT_SET_H."},
        "9731": {"synopsis":"After each array of a DIM statement: returns at the end of the statement, else expects ','\nand goes on with the next array (falls into DIM_CMD).","comment":"Back to the last character"},
        "9733": {"comment":"End of statement? Done"},
        "9736": {"synopsis":"The DIM statement: pushes DIM_NEXT as the return, sets $40AE to a non-zero value with bit 7\nset and finds each array, which makes the finder create it with the given sizes. The byte at\n$260D (the OR's operand, XOR A) is the finder's main entry: VAR_FIND (no label of its own\nhere) clears $40AE and finds a variable, an array element when a '(' follows. Variable\nfinder, entry $260D: HL at the variable's name.\n1. B = first character (a letter, else report SN), C = the second letter or digit, 0 if\n   none; further letters and digits are skipped.\n2. The type comes from a suffix (% 2, $ 3, ! 4, # 8) or else from the table at $4101, one\n   byte per initial letter.\n3. The type goes to $40AF. With '(' (and $40DC zero) it is an array element; else the simple\n   variables from ($40F9) to ($40FB) are searched.\n4. A simple variable not found is made: the arrays move up and a zeroed entry (type, second\n   char, first char, value) is put at the old ($40FB).\nExit: DE = the value's address, HL past the name.","comment":"Each array returns to DIM_NEXT"},
        "9740": {"comment":"A <> 0: creating; $260D is XOR A, finding"},
        "9742": {"comment":"Creating-arrays flag at $40AE"},
        "9745": {"comment":"B = first character"},
        "9746": {"comment":"Not a letter? Stop with report SN"},
        "9752": {"comment":"C = 0: no second character yet"},
        "9754": {"comment":"A digit second?"},
        "9757": {"comment":"A letter second?"},
        "9762": {"synopsis":"Keeps the second character of the name in C."},
        "9763": {"synopsis":"Skips the rest of the name's letters and digits."},
        "9766": {"comment":"Another letter?"},
        "9771": {"synopsis":"Sets D to the variable's type from its suffix, or from the per-letter type table at $4101\nwhen there is none; returns to VAR_TYPE_SET.","comment":"Return to VAR_TYPE_SET"},
        "9775": {"comment":"'%': integer, 2"},
        "9780": {"comment":"'$': string, 3"},
        "9784": {"comment":"'!': single, 4"},
        "9788": {"comment":"'#': double, 8"},
        "9793": {"comment":"No suffix: index by the first letter"},
        "9802": {"comment":"D = the letter's type from $4101"},
        "9808": {"comment":"Back to the character after the name"},
        "9810": {"synopsis":"Stores the type at $40AF, steps past any suffix and decides between an array element and a\nsimple variable."},
        "9811": {"comment":"The type byte"},
        "9815": {"comment":"Subscripts not allowed ($40DC set)?"},
        "9822": {"comment":"A '(' after the name: an array"},
        "9828": {"synopsis":"Searches the simple variables from ($40F9) to ($40FB) for type D and name BC; clears $40DC\nfirst.","comment":"Clear the no-subscript flag"},
        "9834": {"comment":"DE = start of the simple variables"},
        "9837": {"synopsis":"Compares one entry (type, second character, first character) with the name; the byte at\n$2686 (INC DE, hidden in LD A,$13) skips the rest.","comment":"End of the simple variables?"},
        "9845": {"comment":"Same type?"},
        "9851": {"comment":"Same second character?"},
        "9856": {"comment":"Same first character?"},
        "9861": {"comment":"$2686 is INC DE; $2687 steps to the value"},
        "9864": {"comment":"Next entry: past the value's bytes"},
        "9870": {"synopsis":"The variable does not exist. Called from VARPTR (return $24F1) it gives DE = 0; called from\nan expression (return $2543) it gives 0 or the empty string; else it is made.","comment":"A = type"},
        "9872": {"comment":"HL = caller's return, text in its place"},
        "9875": {"comment":"From VARPTR?"},
        "9881": {"comment":"From an expression?"},
        "9888": {"comment":"Make it: HL = text, return put back"},
        "9892": {"comment":"BC = entry size: type + 3"},
        "9899": {"comment":"HL = new end of the arrays"},
        "9906": {"comment":"Room? Move the arrays up"},
        "9910": {"comment":"New end of the arrays"},
        "9915": {"comment":"New end of the simple variables"},
        "9918": {"synopsis":"Zeroes the new entry down to its start."},
        "9924": {"comment":"Type byte"},
        "9927": {"comment":"Second character, then first"},
        "9932": {"synopsis":"DE = the value's address, HL = the text."},
        "9935": {"synopsis":"For VARPTR: DE = 0, back to the caller.","comment":"DE = 0"},
        "9937": {"comment":"Drop the saved type and pointer"},
        "9941": {"synopsis":"For an expression: the accumulator gets 0, or for a string the descriptor at $1928 (a zero\nlength byte), and the return goes past OPND_VARIABLE straight to the evaluator.","comment":"Exponent 0: the number zero"},
        "9947": {"comment":"Integer part zero too"},
        "9950": {"comment":"A string?"},
        "9953": {"comment":"Empty string: the 0 byte at $1928"},
        "9959": {"synopsis":"Back past the operand fetch with the text pointer."},
        "9961": {"synopsis":"An array element. Saves $40AE and $40AF, evaluates the subscripts (each a non-negative\ninteger) onto the stack, expects ')' and keeps the text pointer at $40F3; then searches the\narrays from ($40FB) to ($40FD).\nAn array found while DIM creates stops with error code 18 (report DD); a wrong subscript\ncount with error code 16 (report BS). An array not found is made (ARR_CREATE).","comment":"Save the DIM flag and the type"},
        "9966": {"comment":"D = subscripts so far, 0"},
        "9967": {"synopsis":"Evaluates one subscript into DE and pushes it; repeats on ','."},
        "9969": {"comment":"Next subscript, 0-32767"},
        "9975": {"comment":"Subscript under the saved flag and type"},
        "9978": {"comment":"Count it"},
        "9980": {"comment":"Another one after ','?"},
        "9985": {"comment":"Must close with ')'"},
        "9987": {"comment":"Text pointer kept at $40F3"},
        "9991": {"comment":"DIM flag and type back"},
        "9994": {"comment":"D = the subscript count"},
        "9995": {"comment":"HL = start of the arrays"},
        "9998": {"comment":"$270F is ADD HL,DE: past an array"},
        "10005": {"comment":"End of the arrays? Not found"},
        "10011": {"comment":"Same type?"},
        "10015": {"comment":"Same second character?"},
        "10020": {"comment":"Same first character?"},
        "10022": {"comment":"$2727 is INC HL"},
        "10024": {"synopsis":"Reads the entry's length into DE and steps to its dimension count; on a mismatch goes on to\nthe next array."},
        "10025": {"comment":"DE = the length of the rest"},
        "10031": {"comment":"Found while DIM creates? Report DD"},
        "10040": {"comment":"Subscript count matches?"},
        "10045": {"synopsis":"Stops with error code 16 (report BS): a subscript out of range, a wrong count, or an array\ntoo big."},
        "10050": {"synopsis":"Makes the array not found: type, name, a length word, the dimension count and one size word\nper dimension (subscript + 1 under DIM, else 11), then the elements, zeroed. It checks there\nis room against the stack; without DIM it then goes on to find the element.","comment":"Type byte"},
        "10052": {"comment":"DE = element size, the type"},
        "10055": {"comment":"A = dimension count"},
        "10056": {"comment":"Second character, then first"},
        "10061": {"comment":"Room for the size words?"},
        "10064": {"comment":"Length word filled in later"},
        "10066": {"comment":"$40D8: the count byte's address"},
        "10071": {"comment":"Carry: creating under DIM"},
        "10076": {"synopsis":"Takes one dimension's size: 11 without DIM, else the subscript + 1 from the stack.","comment":"Default size 11 (0-10)"},
        "10081": {"comment":"DIM: size = subscript + 1"},
        "10083": {"synopsis":"Stores the size and multiplies the total byte count in DE by it (report BS on overflow)."},
        "10088": {"comment":"DE = DE x BC"},
        "10095": {"comment":"Keep carry: DIM or not"},
        "10096": {"comment":"BC = bytes of the elements"},
        "10099": {"comment":"HL = end of the new array"},
        "10102": {"comment":"Below the stack? Else report OM"},
        "10105": {"comment":"New end of the arrays"},
        "10108": {"synopsis":"Zeroes the elements back down to the first one."},
        "10114": {"comment":"BC = element bytes + 1"},
        "10116": {"comment":"E = dimension count"},
        "10121": {"comment":"Plus two bytes per size word"},
        "10124": {"comment":"Length word before the count byte"},
        "10131": {"comment":"DIM: done, nothing to find"},
        "10133": {"synopsis":"Finds the element: for each dimension the subscript must be below the size (else report BS)\nand index = index x size + subscript. The byte at $279A (POP HL, hidden in LD D,$E1) is the\nloop's start. Then index x element size (2, 3, 4 or 8) + first element.\nExit: DE = the element's address, HL = the text pointer from $40F3.","comment":"BC = index so far, 0"},
        "10135": {"comment":"A = dimension count"},
        "10137": {"comment":"$279A is POP HL: the size pointer back"},
        "10139": {"comment":"DE = this dimension's size"},
        "10143": {"comment":"HL = subscript, pointer kept"},
        "10145": {"comment":"Subscript below the size? Else BS"},
        "10149": {"comment":"DE = index x size"},
        "10152": {"comment":"Plus the subscript"},
        "10159": {"comment":"A = element type"},
        "10164": {"comment":"Index x 2"},
        "10165": {"comment":"Integer or string?"},
        "10169": {"comment":"Index x 4: single"},
        "10172": {"comment":"Index x 8: double"},
        "10173": {"synopsis":"Integer or string: a type of 2 (odd parity of $FE) stays x 2, a 3 adds one more for x 3."},
        "10174": {"comment":"Type 2? Stay at x 2"},
        "10177": {"comment":"Type 3: x 3"},
        "10178": {"synopsis":"Adds the address of the first element: DE = the element's address.","comment":"BC = first element"},
        "10181": {"synopsis":"HL = the text pointer saved at $40F3."},
        "10185": {"synopsis":"MEM: the free memory between the end of the arrays and the stack, as a single; steps past\nthe token.","comment":"Type 0: not a string, for FRE_FN"},
        "10196": {"synopsis":"The FRE function. With a number: the free memory between the end of the arrays ($40FD) and\nthe stack. With a string: frees it, collects the string space and gives the free string\nspace, from ($40A0) to ($40D6). The result is a single.","comment":"DE = end of the arrays"},
        "10200": {"comment":"HL = stack pointer"},
        "10204": {"comment":"A string argument?"},
        "10207": {"comment":"Free it"},
        "10210": {"comment":"Collect the string space"},
        "10213": {"comment":"DE = bottom of the string space"},
        "10217": {"comment":"HL = lowest string in use - 1"},
        "10220": {"synopsis":"HL = HL - DE as an unsigned single in the accumulator."},
        "10229": {"synopsis":"The POS function: the display column at $40A6 as an integer.","comment":"The cursor's column"},
        "10232": {"synopsis":"Puts A in the accumulator as an integer 0-255 (falls into INT_SET_H)."},
        "10234": {"synopsis":"Puts HL, with H = A, in the accumulator as an integer."},
        "10238": {"synopsis":"The USR function: calls the RAM hook at $41A9 (a RET at power-on), evaluates the bracketed\nargument and jumps to the address at $408E with A = the argument's type and the argument in\nthe accumulator. A string argument is freed first, its descriptor's address in DE. The\nroutine returns through POP_HL_RET, which takes the text pointer back.","comment":"The RAM hook at $41A9, a RET at power-on"},
        "10242": {"comment":"The bracketed argument"},
        "10246": {"comment":"Return through POP_HL_RET: text back"},
        "10254": {"comment":"A string? Free it"},
        "10261": {"comment":"The user routine's address"},
        "10265": {"synopsis":"Converts the accumulator to the type in A (2, 3, 4 or 8) through the table at\nTYPE_CONV_TABLE (to double, integer, a string check, single). For a string a number gives\nreport TM.\nEntry: A = the wanted type.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "10266": {"comment":"Index by type: 8 gives 0"},
        "10275": {"comment":"Jump through TYPE_CONV_TABLE + 2 x type"},
        "10280": {"synopsis":"Returns when a program is running; in direct mode (the line number at $40A2 is $FFFF) stops\nwith error code 22 (report ID).\nChanges: AF."},
        "10284": {"comment":"$FFFF: direct mode?"},
        "10289": {"comment":"E = 22: ID"},
        "10294": {"synopsis":"The STR$ function: turns the number into text (through NUM_TO_TEXT), makes it a string and\ncopies it into the string space as a new temporary string.","comment":"The number as text"},
        "10297": {"comment":"Text to a temporary string"},
        "10300": {"comment":"HL = its descriptor, freed"},
        "10303": {"comment":"Return: push the new string"},
        "10307": {"synopsis":"Copies the string whose descriptor HL points at into newly allocated string space and builds\nits descriptor at $40D3.\nExit: DE = $40D3.","comment":"A = length"},
        "10310": {"comment":"DE = space for it"},
        "10314": {"comment":"BC = the old text"},
        "10317": {"comment":"New descriptor at $40D3"},
        "10321": {"comment":"Copy the characters"},
        "10327": {"synopsis":"Allocates A bytes in the string space and builds a descriptor for them at $40D3 (falls into\nSTR_DESC_SET)."},
        "10330": {"synopsis":"Builds a descriptor at $40D3: length A, address DE.\nExit: HL = $40D3."},
        "10334": {"comment":"Length"},
        "10336": {"comment":"Address"},
        "10341": {"synopsis":"Makes a temporary string of the text at HL up to a 0 byte or a quote (falls into STR_LITERAL\nwith HL one back)."},
        "10342": {"synopsis":"Makes a temporary string of the text after HL up to a quote or a 0 byte, without copying it:\nthe descriptor points into the text. Steps past a closing quote.\nExit: HL past the string, A = its next character, the descriptor pushed onto the temporary\nstack (STR_PUSH_TEMP).","comment":"Ends at a quote"},
        "10345": {"synopsis":"As STR_LITERAL but ending at the character in B or in D, or a 0 byte."},
        "10346": {"comment":"C counts the characters"},
        "10348": {"synopsis":"Counts characters up to the end of the text or a delimiter."},
        "10351": {"comment":"End of the line?"},
        "10360": {"synopsis":"At the end: past a closing quote, then the descriptor for the text found.","comment":"A quote? Step past it"},
        "10365": {"comment":"HL = the start, the end kept"},
        "10367": {"comment":"DE = the first character"},
        "10368": {"comment":"Descriptor at $40D3 for C characters"},
        "10372": {"synopsis":"Pushes the descriptor at $40D3 onto the temporary-string stack at ($40B3) and points the\naccumulator at the new entry, type 3. The stack runs from $40B5; reaching $40D6 stops with\nerror code 30 (report ST).\nEntry: the text pointer on the stack.\nExit: HL = that text pointer, A = its character.","comment":"The new descriptor"},
        "10375": {"comment":"$2888 is PUSH DE"},
        "10377": {"comment":"Top of the temporary stack"},
        "10380": {"comment":"Accumulator: the new entry"},
        "10383": {"comment":"Type 3: string"},
        "10388": {"comment":"Copy the three bytes"},
        "10391": {"comment":"Stack full?"},
        "10398": {"comment":"Text pointer back"},
        "10401": {"comment":"E = 30: ST"},
        "10406": {"synopsis":"Prints the text after HL up to a 0 byte or a quote (falls into PRINT_TEXT one byte on)."},
        "10407": {"synopsis":"Prints the text at HL up to a 0 byte or a quote through the current device (falls into\nPRINT_STRING).","comment":"Make it a temporary string"},
        "10410": {"synopsis":"Prints the string in the accumulator through OUT_CHAR (the current device) and frees it;\nafter each carriage return calls NEWLINE_TAIL.","comment":"HL = descriptor, freed"},
        "10413": {"comment":"D = length, BC = text"},
        "10416": {"comment":"Length 0 ends at once"},
        "10417": {"synopsis":"Prints one character; ends when D is 0."},
        "10420": {"comment":"Out to the current device"},
        "10423": {"comment":"A carriage return?"},
        "10431": {"synopsis":"Allocates A bytes at the bottom of the string space: ($40D6) moves down by A, never below\n($40A0). When there is no room the string space is collected and the allocation tried once\nmore (from $28C1, the POP AF hidden in LD C,$F1); a second failure stops with error code 26\n(report OS).\nEntry: A = length.\nExit: DE = the new string's first byte, A kept.\nChanges: BC, DE, HL, F.","comment":"First try: Z clear when A <> 0"},
        "10432": {"comment":"$28C1 is POP AF: the retry after collecting"},
        "10435": {"comment":"DE = bottom limit of the string space"},
        "10439": {"comment":"HL = lowest string in use - 1"},
        "10442": {"comment":"HL = HL - A"},
        "10448": {"comment":"Below the limit? No room"},
        "10451": {"comment":"New bottom"},
        "10456": {"synopsis":"Takes the saved length and flags back and returns."},
        "10458": {"synopsis":"No room: on the retry stops with report OS, else collects the string space and tries again."},
        "10459": {"comment":"E = 26: OS"},
        "10461": {"comment":"Already collected? Stop with report OS"},
        "10464": {"comment":"Z set: this is the retry"},
        "10466": {"comment":"Return to $28C1: try again"},
        "10470": {"synopsis":"Collects the string space: moves every string in use up against the top ($40B1), highest\nfirst, so that the free space is one block above ($40A0).\n1. ($40D6) is set to the top.\n2. Each pass looks at the temporary stack ($40B5 to ($40B3)), the simple string variables\n   and every string array for the string with the highest address below ($40D6) and above\n   ($40A0); strings in the program text are skipped.\n3. That string is moved up to end at ($40D6), its descriptor gets the new address and\n   ($40D6) goes below it; then the next pass.\nIt ends when a pass finds none.","comment":"Start with the whole string space free"},
        "10473": {"synopsis":"One pass: the best descriptor so far (none) and its address (start ($40A0)) are kept on the\nstack."},
        "10476": {"comment":"No descriptor found yet"},
        "10480": {"comment":"Highest address so far: the bottom limit"},
        "10484": {"comment":"First the temporary stack"},
        "10487": {"comment":"DE = top of the temporary stack"},
        "10492": {"comment":"Last temporary? Go to the variables"},
        "10493": {"comment":"Each check returns to $28F7"},
        "10499": {"comment":"Then the simple variables"},
        "10502": {"synopsis":"Walks the simple variables and checks each string one."},
        "10507": {"comment":"End of the simple variables?"},
        "10510": {"comment":"Type, past the name to the value"},
        "10514": {"comment":"A string?"},
        "10518": {"comment":"Check its descriptor"},
        "10522": {"synopsis":"Steps past the value (A bytes)."},
        "10528": {"synopsis":"An array that is not of strings: skipped."},
        "10529": {"synopsis":"Walks the arrays; for a string array checks every element's descriptor."},
        "10534": {"comment":"End of the arrays? Move the best one"},
        "10540": {"comment":"Name in DE, BC = the length of the rest"},
        "10544": {"comment":"HL = the next array"},
        "10545": {"comment":"A string array?"},
        "10549": {"comment":"$40D8: where this array ends"},
        "10553": {"comment":"Past the dimension count and sizes"},
        "10559": {"comment":"DE = the array's end"},
        "10564": {"comment":"Last element? Next array"},
        "10567": {"comment":"Each check returns to $293F"},
        "10570": {"synopsis":"Pushes the return in BC and falls into COLLECT_CHECK."},
        "10571": {"synopsis":"Looks at the descriptor at HL: a non-empty string below ($40D6) and above the best address\nso far becomes the best one (its address and the pointer past its descriptor replace those\non the stack).\nExit: HL past the descriptor.","comment":"A = length; empty?"},
        "10574": {"comment":"DE = the string's address"},
        "10579": {"comment":"BC = past the descriptor"},
        "10581": {"comment":"Already moved up (above the bottom)?"},
        "10588": {"comment":"Higher than the best so far?"},
        "10596": {"comment":"Yes: replace the best address and pointer"},
        "10603": {"synopsis":"After a pass: if a string was found it is moved up to end at ($40D6), its descriptor updated\nand the next pass starts below it; else the collection is done.","comment":"DE = its address, HL = past its descriptor"},
        "10605": {"comment":"None found? Done"},
        "10608": {"comment":"BC = the string's address"},
        "10613": {"comment":"HL = its last byte"},
        "10618": {"comment":"DE = its first byte"},
        "10623": {"comment":"Move it up to end at ($40D6)"},
        "10630": {"comment":"Descriptor gets the new address"},
        "10635": {"comment":"New bottom: below the moved string"},
        "10639": {"synopsis":"The + operator on strings: evaluates the right operand (which must be a string, else report\nTM), allocates their total length (over 255 stops with error code 28, report LS), frees both\nand copies them into the new string, which is pushed onto the temporary stack. Then the\noperator loop goes on at $2349.","comment":"Keep the pending precedence"},
        "10641": {"comment":"Left descriptor on the stack"},
        "10645": {"comment":"The right operand"},
        "10649": {"comment":"Must be a string, else TM"},
        "10652": {"comment":"A = left length"},
        "10658": {"comment":"Total length over 255?"},
        "10659": {"comment":"E = 28: LS"},
        "10664": {"comment":"Space for both, descriptor at $40D3"},
        "10668": {"comment":"Free the right string"},
        "10672": {"comment":"Free the left string"},
        "10676": {"comment":"DE = the new string's text"},
        "10680": {"comment":"Copy the left string, then the right"},
        "10686": {"comment":"Return to the operator loop at $2349"},
        "10691": {"comment":"Push the new string"},
        "10694": {"synopsis":"Copies the string whose descriptor is on the stack (under the return) to DE.\nExit: DE past the copy.","comment":"HL = the descriptor, return kept"},
        "10696": {"comment":"A = length, BC = text"},
        "10702": {"synopsis":"Copies L bytes from BC up to DE.\nExit: BC and DE past the bytes.\nChanges: A, BC, DE, L."},
        "10703": {"synopsis":"Copies one byte until L is 0.","comment":"Any left?"},
        "10711": {"synopsis":"Checks that the accumulator holds a string (else report TM), then frees it (falls into\nSTR_ACC_FREE)."},
        "10714": {"synopsis":"Frees the string in the accumulator (STR_FREE with DE = ($4121)).\nExit: HL = its descriptor."},
        "10717": {"synopsis":"STR_FREE for the descriptor at HL."},
        "10718": {"synopsis":"Frees a temporary string: when DE is the top entry of the temporary stack it is popped, and\nwhen its text is also the lowest in the string space that space is given back.\nEntry: DE = the descriptor.\nExit: HL = the descriptor, BC = its text when it was popped.","comment":"Top of the temporary stack? Popped"},
        "10722": {"comment":"Not a temporary: done"},
        "10724": {"comment":"DE = its text - 1"},
        "10727": {"comment":"C = its length"},
        "10728": {"comment":"The lowest string in the space?"},
        "10734": {"comment":"Give its bytes back"},
        "10739": {"synopsis":"HL = the descriptor."},
        "10741": {"synopsis":"Pops the temporary stack ($40B3, 3 bytes an entry) when DE is its top entry.\nExit: Z and BC = that string's text when popped; else NZ.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"Top of the temporary stack"},
        "10744": {"comment":"BC = the top entry's text"},
        "10749": {"comment":"Is DE the top entry?"},
        "10751": {"comment":"Yes: pop it"},
        "10755": {"synopsis":"The LEN function: the length of its string argument as an integer. Pushes the integer-result\nstep at INT_FROM_A as its return, then falls into STR_ARG_LEN, whose A is the result.","comment":"Return through INT_FROM_A: A becomes the integer result"},
        "10759": {"synopsis":"Takes the string the evaluator just produced: checks it is a string and releases it from the\ntemporary stack and string space if it is the newest one (STR_ARG_FREE).\nExit: HL = its 3-byte descriptor (length, address), A = the length, D = 0, Z if the string\nis empty.","comment":"Check the result is a string and release it"},
        "10762": {"comment":"D = 0, so DE can be used as the length"},
        "10764": {"comment":"Length from the descriptor"},
        "10765": {"comment":"Z if the string is empty"},
        "10767": {"synopsis":"The ASC function: the code of the first character of its string argument as an integer,\nthrough INT_FROM_A. Falls into STR_FIRST_CHAR.","comment":"Return through INT_FROM_A: A becomes the integer result"},
        "10771": {"synopsis":"The first character of the string just evaluated; stops with error code 8 (report FC) when\nit is empty.\nExit: A = the character, DE = the string's address."},
        "10774": {"comment":"Empty string: FC"},
        "10777": {"comment":"DE = the text's address from the descriptor"},
        "10781": {"comment":"First character"},
        "10783": {"synopsis":"The CHR$ function: a one-character string of the code given.\n1. Reserves one byte of string space with its temporary descriptor at $40D3\n   (STR_ALLOC_DESC).\n2. Takes the argument as a byte 0-255 (RESULT_TO_BYTE), stores it there and returns the\n   string through STR_RESULT.","comment":"One character"},
        "10788": {"comment":"Argument as a byte in E"},
        "10791": {"comment":"The new string's address, from the descriptor at $40D3"},
        "10795": {"synopsis":"Ends a string function: drops the word under the return (for CHR$ the return to $253E, the\nnumeric functions' step pushed by the dispatch at FN_CALL; for STRING$ a spare copy of the\ncharacter) and pushes the temporary descriptor at $40D3 as the result (STR_PUSH_TEMP). Also\npushed as a return by $283F.","comment":"Drop the word under the return"},
        "10796": {"comment":"Descriptor at $40D3 becomes the result"},
        "10799": {"synopsis":"The STRING$ function, STRING$(count, character), reached from the evaluator at $251F. The\ncharacter is a string's first character or a number's value.\n1. Takes the count as a byte and the second argument as any expression, both in parentheses.\n2. Reserves count bytes of string space and fills them with the character.\n3. Returns the string through STR_RESULT.","comment":"Past the STRING$ token"},
        "10802": {"comment":"Count as a byte in E"},
        "10805": {"comment":"Keep the count"},
        "10808": {"comment":"Second argument"},
        "10813": {"comment":"HL = count, text pointer kept on the stack"},
        "10815": {"comment":"Is the second argument a string?"},
        "10818": {"comment":"Number: its value as a byte"},
        "10823": {"synopsis":"The second argument of STRING$ is a string: its first character is the filler.","comment":"String: its first character"},
        "10826": {"synopsis":"Reserves the count's bytes of string space (STR_ALLOC_DESC) and fills them with the\ncharacter in A, unless the count is 0.","comment":"E = the count"},
        "10827": {"comment":"Two copies of the character; one is dropped by STR_RESULT"},
        "10830": {"comment":"Reserve E bytes; descriptor at $40D3"},
        "10835": {"comment":"Count 0?"},
        "10839": {"comment":"Address of the new string"},
        "10842": {"synopsis":"Writes the character into each byte of the new string.","comment":"Store the character"},
        "10844": {"comment":"Until the count is used up"},
        "10849": {"synopsis":"The LEFT$ function: the first n characters of a string. Takes n into B (ARGS_CLOSE) and goes\non with offset 0 into SUBSTR_FROM.","comment":"B = n, the descriptor left on the stack"},
        "10852": {"comment":"Offset 0"},
        "10853": {"synopsis":"Common tail of LEFT$ and RIGHT$: A = the offset of the first character, B = the length\nwanted, the source's descriptor on the stack (swapped with HL here). The `LD A,$E5` at $2A67\nskips a `PUSH HL` at $2A68, the entry the PRINT USING string field calls with HL = the\ndescriptor, so that HL is pushed twice.","comment":"HL = source descriptor, text pointer on the stack"},
        "10854": {"comment":"C = offset"},
        "10855": {"comment":"Skips the PUSH HL at $2A68 (an entry from $2E31)"},
        "10857": {"synopsis":"Makes a new string of B characters taken from offset C of the string whose descriptor is in\nHL; MID$ pushes this address as its return.\n1. Limits the length to the source's length (then the offset becomes 0: the `LD DE,$000E` at\n   $2A6F hides `LD C,0` at $2A70).\n2. Reserves the space (STR_ALLOC), builds the descriptor at $40D3 (STR_DESC_SET) and copies\n   the characters (COPY_L_BYTES).\n3. Releases the source if it is the newest string (STR_FREE) and returns the result through\n   STR_PUSH_TEMP.","comment":"Keep the source descriptor"},
        "10858": {"comment":"Source length"},
        "10859": {"comment":"Shorter than wanted?"},
        "10862": {"comment":"Long enough: B characters"},
        "10863": {"comment":"Skipped bytes hide LD C,$00 at $2A70 (short source: offset 0)"},
        "10867": {"comment":"Reserve A bytes of string space"},
        "10871": {"comment":"Source descriptor again"},
        "10873": {"comment":"HL = the source's text address"},
        "10878": {"comment":"Add the offset"},
        "10881": {"comment":"BC = first character to copy"},
        "10883": {"comment":"Descriptor of the new string at $40D3"},
        "10886": {"comment":"L = count to copy"},
        "10887": {"comment":"Copy the characters"},
        "10890": {"comment":"Release the source if it is the newest string"},
        "10894": {"comment":"Push the result's descriptor"},
        "10897": {"synopsis":"The RIGHT$ function: the last n characters of a string. The offset is the length minus n\n(SUBSTR_MAKE limits a negative result).","comment":"B = n"},
        "10900": {"comment":"Peek at the source descriptor"},
        "10902": {"comment":"Length minus n is the offset"},
        "10906": {"synopsis":"The MID$ function, MID$(string, start[, length]).\n1. Takes the start into B; 0 stops with error code 8 (report FC).\n2. Reads an optional length (255 when missing) and the closing parenthesis.\n3. Turns start and length into an offset in C and a count in B, clipped to the string, and\n   returns into SUBSTR_MAKE, which builds the result.","comment":"HL = text pointer"},
        "10907": {"comment":"Next character"},
        "10908": {"comment":"B = the start, from the stack"},
        "10911": {"comment":"Start 0?"},
        "10916": {"comment":"Keep the start"},
        "10917": {"comment":"Length 255 unless one is given"},
        "10919": {"comment":"No length?"},
        "10925": {"comment":"Length as a byte in E"},
        "10928": {"synopsis":"Closes MID$'s arguments and computes the substring's offset and count."},
        "10930": {"comment":"A = the start"},
        "10931": {"comment":"HL = source descriptor, text pointer on the stack"},
        "10932": {"comment":"Return into SUBSTR_MAKE"},
        "10936": {"comment":"Offset = start - 1"},
        "10937": {"comment":"Offset at or past the end?"},
        "10938": {"comment":"Then an empty result"},
        "10941": {"comment":"C = offset"},
        "10943": {"comment":"Characters left after the offset"},
        "10944": {"comment":"Fewer than the length asked?"},
        "10945": {"comment":"Then all of them"},
        "10947": {"comment":"Else the length asked"},
        "10949": {"synopsis":"The VAL function: the number a string's text reads as. An empty string gives 0.\n1. Puts a $00 temporarily after the string's last character.\n2. Converts the text (TEXT_TO_DBL), then restores the byte the $00 replaced.","comment":"Descriptor and length"},
        "10952": {"comment":"Empty: result 0"},
        "10955": {"comment":"DE = length"},
        "10956": {"comment":"HL = the text's address"},
        "10962": {"comment":"Byte after the text"},
        "10963": {"comment":"Save it and put the end mark there"},
        "10965": {"comment":"HL = text start, end address kept"},
        "10968": {"comment":"Convert the text to a number"},
        "10973": {"comment":"Restore the byte after the text"},
        "10975": {"synopsis":"Ends a string function's argument list: steps past the `)` and falls into POP_COUNT.\nEntry: DE = the text pointer."},
        "10978": {"synopsis":"Takes the second argument (a byte the dispatch puts under the return at $2565-$256C) from\nunder the return address.\nExit: B = that byte, DE = the whole pushed word.","comment":"Return address"},
        "10979": {"comment":"The pushed argument"},
        "10983": {"synopsis":"Reached from the statement dispatch ($1D67) for a token past the table: token $FA (MID$ as a\nstatement) jumps to the RAM hook at $41D9, a RET at power-on; any other stops with error\ncode 2 (report SN).","comment":"Token $FA, MID$?"},
        "10988": {"comment":"RAM hook at $41D9, a RET at power-on"},
        "10991": {"synopsis":"The INP function: reads the port given. The port number goes into the RAM routine at $4093\n(`IN A,(n)` / `RET`, copied at power-on from RAM_IMAGE_4080 by BASIC_INIT), which is called;\nA becomes the integer result (INT_FROM_A).","comment":"Port as a byte"},
        "10994": {"comment":"Into the IN instruction at $4093"},
        "10997": {"comment":"Read the port"},
        "11000": {"comment":"Result as an integer"},
        "11003": {"synopsis":"The OUT statement, OUT port, value: the port goes into the RAM routine at $4096 (`OUT (n),A`\n/ `RET`, from BASIC_INIT's copy at RAM_IMAGE_4080), which is jumped to with the value in A.","comment":"Port into both RAM routines, value in A"},
        "11006": {"comment":"Write the port"},
        "11009": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next character and falls into EVAL_INT."},
        "11010": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression and converts it to an integer.\nExit: DE = the value, HL = the text pointer, A = D, Z if the value is 0-255.","comment":"Evaluate the expression"},
        "11013": {"synopsis":"Converts the current result to an integer (CINT_FN).\nExit: DE = the value, A = D, Z if it is 0-255; HL kept."},
        "11014": {"comment":"Result as an integer in HL"},
        "11019": {"comment":"High byte zero?"},
        "11022": {"synopsis":"Reads OUT's two arguments: the port (stored at $4094 and $4097, the operands of the RAM IN\nand OUT routines), a comma and the value.\nExit: A = E = the value.","comment":"Port as a byte"},
        "11025": {"comment":"Operand of IN A,(n) at $4093"},
        "11028": {"comment":"Operand of OUT (n),A at $4096"},
        "11033": {"comment":"Value as a byte"},
        "11035": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next character and falls into EVAL_BYTE."},
        "11036": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression whose value must be 0-255; falls into RESULT_TO_BYTE."},
        "11039": {"synopsis":"Converts the current result to a byte: a value above 255 stops with error code 8 (report\nFC). Rereads the current character so the flags describe it.\nExit: A = E = the value, D = 0, HL = the text pointer.","comment":"Value in DE"},
        "11042": {"comment":"Above 255: FC"},
        "11045": {"comment":"Reread the current character"},
        "11049": {"synopsis":"The LLIST statement: sets the output byte at $409C to 1 (the printer) and falls into\nLIST_CMD.","comment":"Output to the printer"},
        "11054": {"synopsis":"The LIST statement, LIST [from][-to]. It never returns to the statement loop: it ends\nthrough READY (BASIC_READY).\n1. Drops its return and reads the line range (LINE_RANGE): the last line stays on the stack,\n   BC = the first line's address.\n2. For each line up to the last: marks direct mode, checks the keyboard (CHECK_KEYS: key $60\n   waits for another key, key 1 stops), prints the number and a space, expands the line into\n   the line buffer (EXPAND_LINE) and prints it with a NEW LINE.","comment":"Drop the return: LIST ends at READY"},
        "11055": {"comment":"Read the range; end line on the stack"},
        "11058": {"comment":"Address of the first line"},
        "11059": {"synopsis":"Lists one line and loops; at the program's end, or past the last line asked for, goes to\nREADY.","comment":"Current line number $FFFF: direct mode"},
        "11065": {"comment":"HL = line address"},
        "11066": {"comment":"DE = last line to list"},
        "11067": {"comment":"BC = the link to the next line"},
        "11071": {"comment":"Link 0: end of the program"},
        "11076": {"comment":"RAM hook at $41DF, a RET at power-on"},
        "11079": {"comment":"Key $60 pauses, key 1 stops (CHECK_KEYS)"},
        "11082": {"comment":"Keep the link"},
        "11083": {"comment":"BC = the line number"},
        "11088": {"comment":"Text pointer kept, HL = the line number"},
        "11089": {"comment":"HL = last line, DE = this line"},
        "11090": {"comment":"Past the last line?"},
        "11092": {"comment":"Then done (drop the link, READY)"},
        "11095": {"comment":"Link and last line back on the stack"},
        "11098": {"comment":"HL = the line number"},
        "11099": {"comment":"Kept at $40EC (the line '.' means)"},
        "11102": {"comment":"Print the line number"},
        "11105": {"comment":"Then a space"},
        "11111": {"comment":"Expand the line into the line buffer"},
        "11114": {"comment":"Line buffer"},
        "11117": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "11120": {"comment":"NEW LINE"},
        "11125": {"synopsis":"Prints the text at HL up to a $00 through the current output (OUT_CHAR).\nExit: HL at the $00.","comment":"End of the text?"},
        "11134": {"synopsis":"Turns a line's tokenized text into plain text in the line buffer ($40A7): plain characters\nare copied, each token is replaced by its keyword from the table at KEYWORDS. At most 255\ncharacters are written.\nEntry: HL = the line's text (after the link and number).\nExit: the buffer ends with $00 (unless full).\n- Token $FB (the apostrophe) first takes back 4 characters, and ELSE ($95) one (DEC_BC_RET): the `:REM` and\n  `:` TOKENIZE_LINE stores before them."},
        "11135": {"comment":"BC = line buffer"},
        "11141": {"comment":"Room for 255 characters"},
        "11145": {"synopsis":"A plain character was stored: next buffer byte; stops when the buffer is full."},
        "11148": {"synopsis":"Stores the next byte of the line; the $00 at its end also ends the expansion.","comment":"Next byte of the line"},
        "11151": {"comment":"Stored as it is first"},
        "11152": {"comment":"End of the line?"},
        "11153": {"comment":"Not a token?"},
        "11156": {"comment":"The apostrophe token?"},
        "11160": {"comment":"Take back 4 characters"},
        "11168": {"synopsis":"Finds the keyword of the token in A in the table at KEYWORDS (each keyword starts with bit 7\nset) and copies it in place of the token.","comment":"ELSE takes back one character"},
        "11173": {"comment":"E = 1 for token $80"},
        "11177": {"comment":"Keyword table"},
        "11180": {"synopsis":"Skips to the E-th byte with bit 7 set: the start of the token's keyword.","comment":"Look for the next keyword start"},
        "11186": {"comment":"The token's keyword reached?"},
        "11189": {"comment":"Clear the start mark"},
        "11191": {"synopsis":"Copies the keyword's characters to the buffer until the next keyword's start.","comment":"Character into the buffer"},
        "11193": {"comment":"Buffer full: drop the text pointer and return (STR_ALLOC_OK)"},
        "11197": {"comment":"Next character of the keyword"},
        "11200": {"comment":"Not the next keyword yet?"},
        "11203": {"comment":"Back to the line's text"},
        "11206": {"synopsis":"The DELETE statement, DELETE [from][-to].\n1. Reads the range (LINE_RANGE); the last line must exist (FIND_LINE), else error code 8\n   (report FC); so must there be at least one line in the range.\n2. Prints READY (READY_CR_TEXT) and removes the lines by moving the rest of the program down\n   (CLOSE_GAP).\n3. Returns into LINE_STORED, which relinks the program and goes back to the command level.","comment":"Read the range"},
        "11209": {"comment":"DE = last line"},
        "11210": {"comment":"First line's address, twice"},
        "11212": {"comment":"Find the last line"},
        "11215": {"comment":"Not found: FC"},
        "11217": {"comment":"DE = address after the last line"},
        "11219": {"comment":"HL = address of the first line"},
        "11221": {"comment":"Is the range empty?"},
        "11222": {"synopsis":"FC unless the last line was found after the first."},
        "11225": {"comment":"READY text"},
        "11231": {"comment":"BC = first line: the gap's start"},
        "11232": {"comment":"Return into LINE_STORED, relink and command level"},
        "11236": {"synopsis":"Removes bytes from the program by moving everything after them down, up to the program's end\nat $40F9, and sets the new end. Also called by the line entry ($1AB9) to remove a line being\nreplaced.\nEntry: HL = the first byte to keep, BC = the first byte removed."},
        "11237": {"comment":"Program end"},
        "11240": {"synopsis":"Copies one byte down until the program's end."},
        "11244": {"comment":"At the end?"},
        "11247": {"comment":"New program end"},
        "11253": {"synopsis":"The CSAVE statement, CSAVE [#drive,] name.\n1. Selects the drive and starts the tape with its sync (TAPE_WRITE_LEAD: 255 zeros and $A5).\n2. Writes $D3 three times and the name's first character.\n3. Writes the program from its start ($40A4) to its end ($40F9) and stops the motor\n   (TAPE_STOP).","comment":"Drive, motor and leader"},
        "11256": {"comment":"Name expression"},
        "11260": {"comment":"A = the name's first character"},
        "11263": {"comment":"Header byte $D3, once here"},
        "11268": {"comment":"Twice more"},
        "11271": {"comment":"Then the name character"},
        "11275": {"comment":"DE = program start"},
        "11279": {"comment":"HL = program end"},
        "11282": {"synopsis":"Writes the program's bytes to the tape one by one."},
        "11287": {"comment":"Program end reached?"},
        "11290": {"comment":"Motor off"},
        "11295": {"synopsis":"The CLOAD statement, CLOAD [#drive,][?][name]. `CLOAD?` (the token PRINT, $B2) compares the\ntape with the program instead of loading it.\n1. Selects the drive and waits for the sync (TAPE_FIND_SYNC, which shows ** at the screen's\n   top right).\n2. Keeps the name's first character (0 for any) and the compare flag at $4121; a load clears\n   the program first (NEW_PROGRAM).\n3. Finds a header of three $D3 bytes; a file with another name has its name shown at $3C3E\n   and is skipped (CLOAD_SKIP).\n4. Reads bytes to the program start ($40A4) until three zero bytes in a row, checking memory\n   ($196C) and blinking the second asterisk (TAPE_BLINK). A compare that differs prints BAD.\n5. Sets the program's end ($40F9), prints READY, stops the motor and relinks the program\n   (LINE_STORED).\n- The `LD BC,$232F` at $2C28 hides `CPL` / `INC HL` at $2C29, the `?` path: flag $FF and\n  past the token.","comment":"Drive, motor, wait for the sync"},
        "11298": {"comment":"Is it CLOAD? (the PRINT token)"},
        "11303": {"comment":"Load: flag 0"},
        "11304": {"comment":"Hides CPL / INC HL at $2C29: compare flag $FF"},
        "11307": {"comment":"Keep the flag"},
        "11309": {"comment":"End of the statement: no name"},
        "11314": {"comment":"Name expression"},
        "11317": {"comment":"A = its first character"},
        "11321": {"synopsis":"Stores the name character (L) and the compare flag (H) at $4121; a load clears the program\nfirst."},
        "11325": {"comment":"L = name, H = compare flag"},
        "11328": {"comment":"Load: clear the program"},
        "11334": {"comment":"D = compare flag, E = name"},
        "11335": {"synopsis":"Waits for three $D3 bytes in a row, the file header."},
        "11337": {"synopsis":"Reads one header byte; anything but $D3 starts the count again.","comment":"Read a byte from the tape"},
        "11342": {"comment":"Not $D3: start the count again"},
        "11346": {"comment":"The file's name character"},
        "11349": {"comment":"Any name wanted?"},
        "11353": {"comment":"The name wanted?"},
        "11356": {"synopsis":"Reads the program into memory from its start.","comment":"Program start"},
        "11359": {"synopsis":"Restarts the count of zero bytes: three in a row end the program."},
        "11361": {"synopsis":"Reads one program byte, compares or stores it, and counts zeros."},
        "11364": {"comment":"Keep the byte"},
        "11365": {"comment":"Differs from memory (compare only)?"},
        "11369": {"comment":"Store it (a compare stores the same byte)"},
        "11370": {"comment":"Room left below the stack? OM otherwise"},
        "11373": {"comment":"A zero byte?"},
        "11378": {"comment":"Blink the second asterisk"},
        "11381": {"comment":"Three zeros: end"},
        "11383": {"comment":"New program end"},
        "11386": {"comment":"READY text"},
        "11392": {"comment":"Motor off"},
        "11398": {"comment":"Relink the lines, back to the command level"},
        "11402": {"synopsis":"A compare found a difference: prints BAD and goes to READY ($1A18).","comment":"BAD text"},
        "11411": {"synopsis":"Another file's name: shows its name character in the first asterisk's place, reads past it\nto three zero bytes in a row, waits for the next sync (TAPE_SYNC_SEARCH) and looks for the\nnext header.","comment":"Show the file's name on the screen"},
        "11414": {"synopsis":"Restarts the zero count."},
        "11416": {"synopsis":"Reads one byte of the file being skipped."},
        "11419": {"comment":"Not zero: count again"},
        "11424": {"comment":"Wait for the next file's sync"},
        "11429": {"synopsis":"The text CLOAD prints when a compare fails: `BAD` and a NEW LINE, ended by $00."},
        "11434": {"synopsis":"The PEEK function: the byte at the address given, as an integer.","comment":"Address in HL"},
        "11438": {"comment":"Result as an integer"},
        "11441": {"synopsis":"The POKE statement, POKE address, value.","comment":"Address in DE"},
        "11447": {"comment":"Value as a byte"},
        "11451": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "11453": {"synopsis":"PRINT USING format; values, reached from PRINT ($20A5) for the token USING. Each value takes\nthe format's next field; at the format's end the format starts again for the remaining\nvalues.\n1. Evaluates the format (must be a string, error code $18, report TM, otherwise) and expects\n   `;`.\n2. Scans the format (USING_SCAN): text that is not a field is printed as it is.\n3. A field takes the next value: `!` one character, `%...%` a string of the field's width,\n   `#` digits with `.`, `,`, `+`, `-`, `**`, `$$` and `[[[[` for a number (printed by\n   NUM_TO_TEXT_A).\n4. A pass that met no field while values remain stops with error code 8 (report FC).\n5. Ends with a NEW LINE unless the statement ends with `;` or `,`, releases the format and\n   resets the output (PRINT_END).\n- The byte at $40DE is the pass's flag: cleared at its start, set to the separator once a\n  field took a value.","comment":"Evaluate the format"},
        "11456": {"comment":"It must be a string"},
        "11461": {"comment":"DE = text pointer"},
        "11462": {"comment":"HL = the format's descriptor"},
        "11467": {"synopsis":"The format ended with values left: starts it again, unless the pass met no field (FC).","comment":"Did this pass meet a field?"},
        "11473": {"comment":"HL = format descriptor, DE = text pointer"},
        "11475": {"synopsis":"Starts a pass over the format."},
        "11476": {"comment":"No field met yet"},
        "11480": {"comment":"NZ: a value is still to come"},
        "11483": {"comment":"B = format length"},
        "11485": {"synopsis":"Empty format, or a pass with no field: error code 8 (report FC)."},
        "11488": {"comment":"HL = the format text"},
        "11495": {"synopsis":"A `%` in the format: a string field if spaces and another `%` follow; its width (C) counts\nboth marks. Otherwise the `%` is printed as it is. The jump to $2E17 lands inside the `LD\nA,$F1` at $2E16 (a POP AF).","comment":"Keep the count of format characters left"},
        "11497": {"comment":"Width 2 so far"},
        "11499": {"synopsis":"Looks at the next format character for spaces up to the closing `%`."},
        "11501": {"comment":"Closing %: a string field"},
        "11506": {"comment":"A space widens the field"},
        "11513": {"synopsis":"No closing `%`: prints the `%` as it is.","comment":"Back to the first %"},
        "11517": {"synopsis":"Prints a format character that is not part of a field (after a pending `+`)."},
        "11523": {"synopsis":"Scans the format for the next field, with D = the field's flags and E = its digit count,\nboth cleared here."},
        "11526": {"synopsis":"Prints a pending `+` that started no field, sets the flags in A and reads the next format\ncharacter.","comment":"Pending + printed"},
        "11532": {"comment":"! : one-character string field"},
        "11537": {"comment":"# : a number field"},
        "11541": {"comment":"Last format character?"},
        "11545": {"comment":"+ : sign flag $08, then on with USING_FLAGS"},
        "11551": {"comment":"Reread the character"},
        "11554": {"comment":"Decimal point?"},
        "11558": {"comment":"% : a string field (USING_PERCENT)"},
        "11562": {"comment":"Not doubled: print it"},
        "11565": {"comment":"$$ : currency field"},
        "11569": {"comment":"** : fill field, else print it"},
        "11573": {"comment":"Room for a third character?"},
        "11579": {"comment":"**$?"},
        "11582": {"synopsis":"Flag $20 for `**`; `**$` adds the currency flag.","comment":"Flag $20: fill with asterisks"},
        "11586": {"comment":"**$ : one more character"},
        "11588": {"comment":"Hides XOR A at $2D45, the $$ entry"},
        "11590": {"comment":"Flag $10: currency sign"},
        "11593": {"synopsis":"`**` and `$$` count as digit places; adds their flags to D."},
        "11596": {"synopsis":"Counts the field's digit places before the point, and commas (flag $40)."},
        "11597": {"comment":"No places after the point yet"},
        "11599": {"comment":"Format ended?"},
        "11604": {"comment":"Point: places after it follow"},
        "11612": {"comment":"Comma: flag $40"},
        "11622": {"synopsis":"A `.`: starts a number field only when a `#` follows; otherwise printed as it is."},
        "11629": {"comment":"The point counts as one place"},
        "11632": {"synopsis":"Counts the places after the point (C)."},
        "11642": {"synopsis":"Checks for `[[[[` after the digits: an exponent field sets flag bit 0 and uses four format\ncharacters. Otherwise returns to $2D97, which the `JP Z,$D1EB` at $2D96 hides (`EX DE,HL` /\n`POP DE`: back to where the check began)."},
        "11643": {"comment":"Return for no exponent: $2D97"},
        "11647": {"comment":"DE = where the check begins"},
        "11649": {"comment":"Four [ ?"},
        "11661": {"comment":"Four characters left?"},
        "11665": {"comment":"Drop the return and restore the flags"},
        "11668": {"comment":"Flag bit 0: exponent"},
        "11670": {"comment":"Never taken; hides EX DE,HL / POP DE at $2D97"},
        "11673": {"synopsis":"Unless a leading `+` was seen, a trailing `-` (flag $04) or `+` (flag $0C) is part of the\nfield."},
        "11676": {"comment":"Leading + already?"},
        "11681": {"comment":"Format ended?"},
        "11685": {"comment":"Trailing - ?"},
        "11690": {"comment":"Trailing + ?"},
        "11696": {"synopsis":"Adds the trailing-sign flag to D."},
        "11701": {"synopsis":"A number field is complete: if a value follows, evaluates it, checks the field has under 25\nplaces (FC otherwise) and prints it formatted with the flags in D and bit 7 set, B places\nbefore and C after the point (NUM_TO_TEXT_A, PRINT_TEXT).","comment":"Text pointer"},
        "11702": {"comment":"Z: no value left"},
        "11707": {"comment":"Evaluate the value"},
        "11714": {"comment":"B = places before the point"},
        "11717": {"comment":"24 places at most"},
        "11722": {"comment":"Flags, bit 7: formatted output"},
        "11725": {"comment":"Format the number"},
        "11728": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "11731": {"synopsis":"After a field: reads the separator. At the statement's end, carry asks for a NEW LINE at the\nend; `;` or `,` marks the pass's flag at $40DE and steps on; anything else stops with error\ncode 2 (report SN).","comment":"Text pointer"},
        "11733": {"comment":"End of the statement?"},
        "11737": {"comment":"A field took a value in this pass"},
        "11749": {"synopsis":"Past the separator; Z if the statement ends after it."},
        "11750": {"synopsis":"Goes on scanning the format after the field just used, or ends the pass if it was the\nformat's last character.","comment":"B = format characters left"},
        "11754": {"comment":"Keep the 'more values' flags"},
        "11756": {"comment":"Offset of the next format character"},
        "11759": {"comment":"HL = the format text"},
        "11766": {"comment":"Next format character"},
        "11767": {"comment":"Any format left?"},
        "11774": {"synopsis":"The format's last character was not a field: prints it."},
        "11780": {"synopsis":"The format is used up: starts it again if values remain."},
        "11785": {"synopsis":"Ends PRINT USING: a NEW LINE if carry, releases the format string (STR_DE_FREE) and resets\nthe output (PRINT_END).","comment":"NEW LINE unless ; or , ended it"},
        "11788": {"comment":"Format descriptor"},
        "11789": {"comment":"Release it"},
        "11793": {"comment":"Reset the output to the display"},
        "11796": {"synopsis":"A `!` field: width 1. The `LD A,$F1` skips a `POP AF` at $2E17, where the `%` field enters\nto drop its saved pointer. Then prints a pending `+`, evaluates the value (a string, else\nerror code $18, report TM), prints its first C characters (SUBSTR_FROM's entry $2A68,\nPRINT_STRING) and pads with spaces to the width.","comment":"Width 1"},
        "11798": {"comment":"Hides POP AF at $2E17, the % field's entry"},
        "11805": {"comment":"Z: no value left"},
        "11809": {"comment":"Evaluate the value"},
        "11812": {"comment":"It must be a string"},
        "11818": {"comment":"Its descriptor"},
        "11821": {"comment":"B = width, C = offset 0"},
        "11824": {"comment":"Keep the width"},
        "11825": {"comment":"Substring of the field's width"},
        "11828": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "11834": {"comment":"Width"},
        "11835": {"comment":"Minus the characters printed"},
        "11837": {"comment":"Pad with spaces"},
        "11840": {"synopsis":"Prints B spaces, then goes on as after a number field."},
        "11849": {"synopsis":"Prints a `+` when D is not zero (a `+` that has not become part of a field); keeps A and the\nflags."},
        "11859": {"synopsis":"Called from the command level ($1A30, after READY) after a stop with error code 2 (report\nSN): clears the error byte at $409A and, if the error was in a program line (its number at\n$40EA is not $FFFF), opens that line in the editor.","comment":"Clear the last error"},
        "11862": {"comment":"Line the error happened in"},
        "11865": {"comment":"$FFFF (direct mode)?"},
        "11868": {"comment":"DE = the line number"},
        "11872": {"synopsis":"The EDIT statement, EDIT line (or `.`). A further character after the number returns NZ (to\nthe statement loop, which reports it). Goes on into EDIT_LINE.","comment":"Line number in DE"},
        "11876": {"synopsis":"Edits the line numbered DE; never returns to its caller.\n1. Remembers the line at $40EC; a missing line stops with error code $0E (report UL).\n2. Expands the line into the line buffer (EXPAND_LINE).\n3. Prints the number and a space, turns the cursor on (code $0E) and reads commands\n   (EDIT_COMMAND) with HL = the cursor in the buffer, B = its column, C = the line's length.","comment":"Drop the return: EDIT ends elsewhere"},
        "11877": {"synopsis":"Finds line DE and copies it into the line buffer; the A command comes back here."},
        "11878": {"comment":"Line '.' means"},
        "11882": {"comment":"Find the line"},
        "11885": {"comment":"Missing: UL"},
        "11888": {"comment":"HL = the line"},
        "11890": {"comment":"Past the link"},
        "11892": {"comment":"BC = the line number"},
        "11896": {"comment":"Kept on the stack"},
        "11897": {"comment":"Expand it into the line buffer"},
        "11900": {"synopsis":"Prints the line number and a space and puts the cursor at the line's start; the L command\ncomes back here."},
        "11902": {"comment":"Print the line number"},
        "11910": {"comment":"Cursor at the buffer's start"},
        "11913": {"comment":"Code $0E: the display driver's cursor on (DISP_CURSOR_ON)"},
        "11919": {"comment":"Count the line's characters"},
        "11921": {"synopsis":"Counts characters to the line's $00: C = the length."},
        "11928": {"comment":"Column 0"},
        "11929": {"synopsis":"Reads one edit command: an optional repeat count of digits (D, 1 when none) and a key. Each\ncommand returns here (EDIT_DISPATCH pushes this address).","comment":"No count yet"},
        "11931": {"synopsis":"Waits for a key (KEY_WAIT_GET); a digit adds to the count D (D x 10 + digit).","comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "11934": {"comment":"A digit?"},
        "11942": {"comment":"D = D x 10 + digit"},
        "11952": {"synopsis":"Runs the command for the key (its code minus $30; a lower-case letter is made upper case\nfirst). BACKSPACE moves left, ENTER ends, SPACE moves right; the letters are A, C, D, E, H,\nI, K, L, Q, S, X. Any other key is ignored.","comment":"Each command returns to EDIT_COMMAND"},
        "11957": {"comment":"No count?"},
        "11962": {"comment":"Then 1"},
        "11963": {"synopsis":"Compares the key with each command (codes minus $30).","comment":"BACKSPACE"},
        "11968": {"comment":"ENTER"},
        "11973": {"comment":"SPACE"},
        "11977": {"comment":"Lower case?"},
        "11981": {"comment":"Upper case"},
        "11983": {"synopsis":"The letter commands.","comment":"Q"},
        "11988": {"comment":"L"},
        "11993": {"comment":"S"},
        "11997": {"comment":"I"},
        "12002": {"comment":"D"},
        "12007": {"comment":"C"},
        "12012": {"comment":"E"},
        "12017": {"comment":"X"},
        "12022": {"comment":"K"},
        "12026": {"comment":"H"},
        "12031": {"comment":"A, else ignore the key"},
        "12034": {"comment":"Drop the return and the line number"},
        "12036": {"comment":"NEW LINE"},
        "12039": {"comment":"Copy the line again: changes undone"},
        "12042": {"synopsis":"SPACE: moves the cursor D characters right, printing them; stops at the line's end. Also\nused by X (D = 255).","comment":"End of the line?"},
        "12045": {"comment":"One column on"},
        "12054": {"synopsis":"K: like S, but deletes the characters passed, shown between `!` marks (EDIT_BANG prints the\nclosing one)."},
        "12055": {"comment":"Return through EDIT_BANG"},
        "12059": {"comment":"Carry: delete"},
        "12060": {"synopsis":"S (carry clear) and K (carry set): waits for a character and moves past characters up to its\nD-th next occurrence, or the line's end."},
        "12061": {"comment":"The character to look for"},
        "12067": {"comment":"K: opening !"},
        "12070": {"synopsis":"Passes one character: printed, then deleted (K) or stepped over (S).","comment":"End of the line?"},
        "12075": {"comment":"Show the character"},
        "12080": {"comment":"K: delete it"},
        "12085": {"comment":"S: step over it"},
        "12087": {"synopsis":"Stops at the D-th occurrence of the character."},
        "12088": {"comment":"The character wanted?"},
        "12094": {"synopsis":"Restores the flags and returns."},
        "12096": {"synopsis":"L: prints the rest of the line and starts again at its start, keeping the changes.","comment":"Rest of the line"},
        "12102": {"comment":"Drop the return to EDIT_COMMAND"},
        "12106": {"synopsis":"D: deletes D characters from the cursor, shown between `!` marks.","comment":"End of the line?"},
        "12114": {"synopsis":"Shows and deletes one character."},
        "12121": {"comment":"Remove it from the buffer"},
        "12127": {"synopsis":"Prints a `!`."},
        "12133": {"synopsis":"C: replaces D characters from the cursor with keys typed.","comment":"End of the line?"},
        "12136": {"comment":"Wait for the new character"},
        "12139": {"comment":"Into the buffer"},
        "12149": {"synopsis":"H: deletes the rest of the line from the cursor and goes on as X.","comment":"Line ends here"},
        "12151": {"comment":"New length"},
        "12152": {"synopsis":"X: moves to the line's end (EDIT_RIGHT with D = 255) and falls into EDIT_INSERT."},
        "12157": {"synopsis":"I: inserts the keys typed at the cursor until ESC ($1B, back to the commands) or ENTER (ends\nthe edit); BACKSPACE removes the character before the cursor.","comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "12164": {"comment":"BACKSPACE?"},
        "12168": {"comment":"ENTER: end the edit"},
        "12173": {"comment":"$1B: back to the commands"},
        "12178": {"synopsis":"BACKSPACE in insert mode: at column 0 nothing happens; else moves left and deletes that\ncharacter."},
        "12180": {"comment":"At column 0?"},
        "12184": {"comment":"Cursor left on the screen"},
        "12189": {"comment":"Return to EDIT_INSERT"},
        "12193": {"synopsis":"Deletes the character at HL from the buffer, moving the rest left and the $00 with it, one\nless in C.\nExit: HL kept, carry set (through POP_HL_RET, POP HL / RET)."},
        "12194": {"comment":"One character fewer"},
        "12195": {"synopsis":"Moves each following character one place left until the $00 is moved."},
        "12196": {"comment":"End reached?"},
        "12208": {"synopsis":"Inserts the key's character at the cursor unless the line has 255 characters already."},
        "12209": {"comment":"Line full?"},
        "12215": {"synopsis":"Ignores the key."},
        "12217": {"synopsis":"Opens a gap at the cursor by moving the rest of the line, its $00 too, one place up\n(MOVE_UP), and stores the character there.","comment":"Characters after the cursor"},
        "12218": {"comment":"Length and column one more"},
        "12221": {"comment":"DE = cursor"},
        "12225": {"comment":"HL = the line's $00"},
        "12229": {"comment":"Move the tail up"},
        "12234": {"comment":"Character into the gap"},
        "12242": {"synopsis":"BACKSPACE: moves the cursor D characters left (code 8 to the screen); stops at column 0.","comment":"At column 0?"},
        "12247": {"comment":"Cursor left on the screen"},
        "12256": {"synopsis":"ENTER: prints the rest of the line and falls into EDIT_END."},
        "12259": {"synopsis":"E (and ENTER): ends the edit with a NEW LINE and enters the buffer's line into the program\nas if typed, through the line entry at LINE_ENTERED.","comment":"NEW LINE"},
        "12262": {"comment":"Drop the return and the line number"},
        "12264": {"comment":"Line number $FFFF?"},
        "12267": {"synopsis":"Enters the text in the line buffer at the line entry (LINE_ENTERED) with carry set and the\nflags from A; also reached from $1A73.","comment":"HL = line buffer - 1"},
        "12273": {"comment":"HL = line buffer"},
        "12275": {"comment":"Store the line in the program"},
        "12278": {"synopsis":"Q: leaves the line as it was and goes to READY (BASIC_READY).","comment":"Drop the return and the line number"},
        "12283": {"synopsis":"Five bytes after the last routine (DE C3 C3 44 B2). Nothing in the ROM jumps to them or\nreads them."}
      }
    }
  },
